  origin: ?{\text{the result, by one mechanism: the formed corner for every}\ n,\ \delta,\ k_0\ \text{and}\ d\ \text{and every character, read on the ties of its rows: a ray splits a cone into two whose terms add up to its own (split), over}\ Z\ \text{the terms of the cones through the divisor \(R\) add up to terms free of its cost by \cref{lem:cone}(apex) (cell), and for}\ \delta>n\ \text{the residue factors are quotients of positive masses (edge), (corner), read with the factor of the stratum on the two dual halves of one square of positive masses (square), and in one letter of positive q-numbers (qcorner); the classical cases are cited inside it: for}\ d=1\ \text{over}\ \Sigma\setminus Z\ \text{(i) is a case of \cite[Theorem~5.6, (Z1)]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}, for}\ \delta=1\ \text{and}\ f=x_1^{k_0}+x_2^{k_0+d}+\dots+x_n^{k_0+d}\ \text{(i) is the formula on the Newton polyhedron of \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992} and of \cite{DenefHoornaert2001}, for}\ \delta=1\ \text{(i) for}\ Z_{top,0}\ \text{agrees with the formula (I.2) of \cite{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}, read with its (1.6), (5.13), Theorem~5.11(a) and Remark~5.12, where}\ \sum_{1\ne e\mid \gamma}J_2(e)=\gamma^2-1\ \text{for the totient}\ J_2\ \text{of Jordan, and for}\ \varepsilon=1\ \text{and}\ f'=0\ \text{the germ is of the type}\ P(h,g)\ \text{of \cite[Theorem~B]{Nemethi1991}; the tools are standard: a toroidal resolution \cite{KempfKnudsenMumfordSaintDonat1973} read by \cref{fact:zetatop} and, at a good prime, the order laws on the tubes, the Euler characteristics of complete intersections \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966}, the Lefschetz theorem \cite{Lamotke1981}, the formula of A'Campo \cite{ACampo1975}, \cite[Theorems~3.3 and~3.4]{Denef1991} for (vi) and the estimates of \cite{LangWeil1954} and, at}\ \delta=1\text{, those of \cite[Theorem~8.1]{Deligne1974} for (vii); the rows are proved in Lean, and (i) to (vii) are proved in the text from them; (qcornersum), (cornercount) and (qcell): when the three letters are powers \(t^A,t^B,t^C\) of one letter, the masses are the sums \(S_k=\sum_{i=1}^kt^i\) of \cref{lem:qsum} over the frame \(S_{A+B+C}\), and the same ties hold at every \(t\) in \(\mathbb Q_{>0}\) (qcornersum), so at \(t=1\) they are the ties of the counts \(A+B\), \(A+C\), \(A\), \(C\), \(B\) over their sum (cornercount), which are (corner) at the costs \(A/N\), \(C/N\), \(B/N\) with \(N=A+B+C\); both are calls of (square), (qcornersum) at \(X=t^A\), \(P=t^{A+B}\), \(Q=t^{A+C}\), with the splits of \cref{lem:qsum}(add) and the exchanges that it gives in the two orders of its parts, and (cornercount) at \(X=P=Q=1\) on the counts, where \(x=A+B\), \(y=A+C\), \(b=A\) and \(n=A+B+C\); over \(Z\), a point of the box of the edge with the gap \(e+1\), in the counts \(k_0=e+r+1\), \(y=D+\sigma\) and \(c_R=k_0y+D\), ties in sum form as \(t^{D+ry}S_DS_yS_{e+1}(u)+S_DS_yS_{r+1}(u)+ut^DS_\sigma S_{c_R}=uS_{c_R}S_y+ut^{D+ry}S_\sigma S_D\) at the letter \(u=t^y\), where \(S_b(u)=\sum_{j=1}^bu^j\), for every \(t\) in \(\mathbb Q_{>0}\), and at \(t=1\), over \(D^2\), it is \((k_0+1)V+sR=VR+s\) in \(V=y/D\), \(R=c_R/D\), \(s=\sigma/D\), the tie of (cell) at one point (qcell): it ties by (add) at \(D\) and \(\sigma\), \(S_y=S_D+t^DS_\sigma\), and by the split of the cost, the range \(1..c_R\) cut at \(y(r+1)\), lengths that multiply, \(uS_{y(r+1)}=S_yS_{r+1}(u)\) and \(uS_{y(e+1)}=S_yS_{e+1}(u)\), and \(y\) and \(D\) added to \(ye+D\) in the two orders}}

-- \textit{(i), \(Z_{top,0}\).} Blowing up the origin gives the component \(E\), of multiplicity \(N\) and log discrepancy \(n\). In a chart \(x=t\xi\)
-- the function is \(t^N(\tilde h^{k_0}\tilde\varepsilon+t^dW)\), where \(\tilde h=h(\xi)\),
-- \(\tilde\varepsilon=\varepsilon(t\xi)\), \(g+f'=t^{N+d}W\) and \(W=\tilde g+t\tilde f'\); the strict transform of the
-- cone over \(\Sigma\) is \(u=0\), with \(u=\tilde h\), and that of \(\{g+f'=0\}\) is \(W=0\). Over \(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z\), \(W\) is a unit; over \(Z\), \(t\), \(u\) and \(y=W\) are coordinates. A component of the orders \(a\), \(b\) of
-- \(t\), \(u\), and \(c\) of \(y\) over \(Z\), has the multiplicity \(Na+\min(k_0b,da)\), or \(Na+\min(k_0b,da+c)\),
-- and the log discrepancy \(na+b\), or \(na+b+c\): the corner is the ray \(S=(\kappa,\rho)\), of multiplicity \(N_S\)
-- and log discrepancy \(\nu_S\), and over \(Z\) the divisor \(R\) is the ray \(R=(0,1,k_0)\), of multiplicity \(k_0\) and
-- log discrepancy \(k_0+1\), which spans with \(S\) the plane \(k_0b=da+c\). A toroidal resolution of the two models
-- \cite{KempfKnudsenMumfordSaintDonat1973} subdivides over \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) the cones \((e_t,S)\) and \((S,e_u)\),
-- of multiplicities \(\rho\) and \(\kappa\), and over \(Z\) the cones \((e_u,S,R)\), \((e_t,S,R)\) and \((e_t,R,e_y)\),
-- of multiplicities \(\kappa k_0\), \(\rho k_0\) and \(1\). Over \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) the polynomial of the edge is
-- \(u^{k_0}+t^dg(\xi)=t^d(v^{\gamma}+g(\xi))\), with \(v=u^\kappa/t^\rho\), so the strict transform meets each fibre of the
-- corner in the \(\gamma\) distinct points \(v^{\gamma}=-g(\xi)\), and no root of \(W\) is taken; over \(Z\) the exponent
-- \((-d,k_0,-1)\) of the edge is primitive. In \cref{fact:zetatop} a cone with the costs \(\sigma\) of its rays at
-- \(s\) gives its multiplicity over the product of these costs, and a ray \(w=au+bv\) inside a cone \((u,v)\), with
-- \(\sigma(w)=a\sigma(u)+b\sigma(v)\), splits it into \((u,w)\) and \((w,v)\), of multiplicities \(b\) and \(a\) times
-- that of \((u,v)\), keeping the sum (split); along a chain of such splits the sum is kept at every length, the
-- telescoping sum of \cref{lem:string}, whose one-step case is \cref{lem:cone}: so the subdivisions enter only through the
-- multiplicities of the cones,
-- with the costs \(\sigma_E\) of \(e_t\), \(\sigma_S\) of \(S\), \(\sigma_R=k_0+1+k_0s\) of \(R\), \(1+s\) of the
-- strict transform, and \(1\) of \(e_u\), which is no component of \(f\). Over a point of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) this gives
-- \(\Theta_2=\rho/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)+\kappa/\sigma_S-\gamma s/((1+s)\sigma_S)\), the last term from the fibre of the corner
-- less its two neighbours and its \(\gamma\) points, of Euler characteristic \(-\gamma\), and from these points; over a point
-- of \(Z\), where the function is \(t^N(u^{k_0}\tilde\varepsilon+t^dy)\), it gives \(\Theta_3=\big(\kappa
-- k_0/\sigma_S+\rho k_0/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)+1/\sigma_E-\kappa s/((1+s)\sigma_S)\big)/\sigma_R\), the last term from the
-- cone \((S,R)\) of multiplicity \(\kappa\) in the plane, where the strict transform meets the components. With
-- \(\sigma_S=\kappa\sigma_E+\rho(1+k_0s)\) and \(\sigma_R=k_0+1+k_0s\), the first clause of (cell) is
-- \(\Theta_3=\Theta_2-(\kappa-\gamma)s/((1+s)\sigma_S)\): by \cref{lem:cone}(apex), the terms of the cones through
-- \(R\) add up to terms free of \(\sigma_R\) in \(Z_{top,0}\). The facet of normal \(R\) is B1 for \(y\), with the
-- base \(t^Nu^{k_0}\) and the apex \(t^{N+d}y\), and \(R=e_u+k_0e_y\) lies in the face of \(e_u\) and \(e_y\); the
-- second clause of (cell) is the flip of the circuit \(S+\rho k_0e_y=\kappa e_t+\rho R\), the tie of the costs
-- \(\sigma_S+\rho k_0=\kappa\sigma_E+\rho\sigma_R\) divided by \(\sigma_E\sigma_S\sigma_R\), and the first is the
-- second, then (apex) at \(\mu=\kappa\) and \(X=\sigma_S\sigma_R\), then (countsplit) at \(\sigma_R=k_0(1+s)+1\),
-- which removes \(R\). With the term
-- \((n-\chi(\Sigma))/\sigma_E\) of \(E\), \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\Theta_2+\chi(Z)\Theta_3\) is (i) for \(Z_{top,0}\).
-- For \(d=1\) the chain of components between the corner and \(e_u\) grows one component at a time (chain), and the
-- first clause of (cell) at \(\kappa=k_0\) and \(\rho=1\) is the cancellation in that form. The classical cases of (i) agree with it: for
-- \(d=1\) over \(\Sigma\setminus Z\), (i) is a case of \cite[Theorem~5.6, (Z1)]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026};
-- for \(\delta=1\) and \(f=x_1^{k_0}+x_2^{k_0+d}+\dots+x_n^{k_0+d}\) it is the formula on the Newton polyhedron of
-- \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992} and of \cite{DenefHoornaert2001}; for \(\delta=1\), (i) for \(Z_{top,0}\) agrees
-- with the formula (I.2) of \cite{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}, read with its (1.6), (5.13),
-- Theorem~5.11(a) and Remark~5.12, where \(\sum_{1\ne e\mid\gamma}J_2(e)=\gamma^2-1\) for the totient \(J_2\) of Jordan;
-- and for \(\varepsilon=1\) and \(f'=0\) the germ is of the type \(P(h,g)\) of \cite[Theorem~B]{Nemethi1991}.
--
-- \textit{(i), \(Z_0\).} At a good prime the same two models give \(Z_0\) by the order laws on the tubes, with no reduction of the resolution.
-- The points of \((p\Z_p)^n\) other than the origin lie on the tubes \(x=t\xi\), with \(t\in p\Z_p\) and \(\xi\) in the
-- residue class of a point of \(\mathbb P^{n-1}(\mathbb F_p)\), and \(|dx|=|t|^{n-1}|dt|\,|d\xi|\). On a tube over a
-- point of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) or of \(Z\), the coordinates \((t,u)\) or \((t,u,y)\), completed by coordinates of the
-- residue class, are \(p\)-integral with a Jacobian that is a unit, since \(f\) is \(p\)-integral and the reductions
-- keep the two hypotheses; so they keep distances, induce a bijection modulo every power of \(p\), and keep volumes,
-- and \(\tilde\varepsilon\) is \(1\) modulo \(p\). Where \(t\) and \(u\) have the orders \(a\) and \(b\), a set of
-- volume \((1-p^{-1})^2p^{-a-b}\), the order of \(u^{k_0}\tilde\varepsilon+t^dW\) is the least of \(k_0b\) and \(da\)
-- when they differ. When \(k_0b=da\), write \(u=p^bu_0\) and \(t=p^at_0\) with units \(u_0\) and \(t_0\); then
-- \(u^{k_0}\tilde\varepsilon+t^dW=p^{k_0b}\phi\) with \(\phi=u_0^{k_0}\tilde\varepsilon+t_0^dW\), and modulo \(p\),
-- where \(\tilde\varepsilon\) is \(1\) and \(W\) is the unit \(g(\xi)\), \(\phi\) is
-- \(\bar\phi=\bar u_0^{k_0}+\bar t_0^dg(\xi)=\bar t_0^d(v^{\gamma}+g(\xi))\) with \(v=\bar u_0^\kappa/\bar t_0^\rho\). As
-- \(v\) maps \((\mathbb F_p^\times)^2\) onto \(\mathbb F_p^\times\) with fibres of \(p-1\) points, \(\kappa\) and
-- \(\rho\) being coprime, \(\bar\phi\) vanishes at \((p-1)r(\xi)\) of the \((p-1)^2\) residues of \((u_0,t_0)\). At
-- such a residue the two partials \(k_0\bar u_0^{k_0-1}\) and \(d\,\bar t_0^{d-1}g(\xi)\) of \(\bar\phi\) are not both
-- \(0\), as a good prime does not divide \(\gamma\), so not both \(k_0\) and \(d\); by Hensel's lemma \(\phi\) and the other
-- unit are then coordinates of the residue class with a Jacobian that is a unit, which keep volumes, and
-- \(\mathrm{ord}\,\phi\ge j\) on a part of relative volume \(p^{1-j}\) of the class; so the order is \(k_0b\) on a part of relative volume
-- \(1-r(\xi)/(p-1)\) and \(k_0b+j\), \(j\ge1\), on a part of relative volume \(r(\xi)p^{-j}\), and the component has the
-- factor \(1-r(\xi)F/(p-1)\), affine in \(r(\xi)\). Over \(Z\), for fixed \((a,b)\) the integral of
-- \(T^{\mathrm{ord}(u^{k_0}\tilde\varepsilon+t^dy)}\) over \(y\in p\Z_p\) is \(p^{-1}T^{k_0b}\) when \(k_0b\le da\) and
-- \(p^{-1}(1-F)T^{da}\) when \(k_0b>da\), whatever \(b\): for \(k_0b>da\) the orders \(c\) of \(y\) below, at and above
-- \(k_0b-da\) add up to it, the order at the tie growing on a part of \(y\) of relative volume \(p^{-j}\) as \(y\)
-- enters linearly; this is \cref{lem:cone}(apex) at a good prime, in its form (qapex), by which the terms of the
-- cones through \(R\) add up to terms free of the letter of \(R\) in \(Z_0\), so that no factor of \(R\) enters. The points of the open cones \((e_t,S)\) and \((S,e_u)\) are the
-- points of their boxes, those of \(P_1\) and \(P_2\), plus counts times the rays, and summing over the components gives
-- \(Z_0\) in (i), with the denominators \(1-X_E\), \(1-X_S\) and \(1-p^{-1}T\).
--
-- \textit{(ii).} At \(s_S\) the cost \(\sigma=\nu+Ns_S\) of a component vanishes on \(S\) and is linear on each of the cones \((e_t,S)\)
-- and \((S,e_u)\): on the first it is \(\alpha_E\det(w,S)/\rho\), with \(\alpha_E=\sigma_E(s_S)=\rho\alpha_L\), and on
-- the second \(\det(S,w)/\kappa\). So the neighbours of \(S\), the points of determinant \(1\), have the costs
-- \(\alpha_L\) and \(\alpha_R\), the strict transform has the cost \(1+s_S=\alpha_V\), and
-- \(N_S(\alpha_L+\alpha_R+\gamma\alpha_V)=k_0(n-\delta)+N+d+\gamma(N_S-\nu_S)=\gamma N_S\) is an identity of counts.
-- It is the relation of \cref{lem:node} at the price \(\nu_S/N_S\) of the corner: on a fibre over
-- \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) the costs of its \(\gamma+2\) neighbours, the two of determinant \(1\) and the \(\gamma\) points
-- of the strict transform, add up to their number less two. By (i), with
-- \(\kappa=1/\alpha_R\), \(\rho/\alpha_E=1/\alpha_L\) and \(s_S/(1+s_S)=1-1/\alpha_V\), \(N_S\) times the residue at
-- \(s_S\) is \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)A_\star+\chi(Z)A_Z\), and since \(\sigma_S(-n/N)=\rho(\delta-n)/\delta\) the
-- residue at \(-n/N\) is \(n(\delta-n+\chi(\Sigma))/(N(\delta-n))\). For \(\delta>n\), \(k_0\beta<1\) as
-- \(k_0(\delta-n)<N+d\), and \(\alpha_V=1-1/k_0+\beta\); so the masses \(1-\alpha_V\),
-- \(\alpha_V-k_0\beta=(k_0-1)(1-k_0\beta)/k_0\) and \(\alpha_V-\gamma\beta\alpha_R\) are positive, the last for every
-- \(\kappa\) by (law) at \(g=\gamma\), the costs \(\alpha_R\le1\) and \(\alpha_V<1\), and \(b=\beta\), as
-- \(\alpha_V-\gamma\beta\alpha_R=\alpha_V(1-\alpha_R^2)+\alpha_R(\gamma-\alpha_R)(1-\alpha_V)\) with \(\gamma-\alpha_R=(k_0-1)/\kappa\). Then (edge) gives the forms of \(-A_\star\), \(-A_Z\) and \(A_Z-A_\star\) in (ii). By
-- the Lefschetz theorem \cite{Lamotke1981}, \(\chi(\Sigma)=n-1+(-1)^nb_\Sigma\) and \(\chi(Z)=n-2-(-1)^nb_Z\) with
-- \(b_\Sigma,b_Z\ge0\), and \(b_\Sigma=((\delta-1)^n+(-1)^n(\delta-1))/\delta\) \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966}: with
-- \(e=\delta-1\) and \(n=2j\) or \(n=2j+1\), \(b_\Sigma\) is the \(b\) of (even) or of (odd). For \(\kappa\ge \gamma\) put
-- \(c_1=-A_\star\) and \(c_2=A_Z-A_\star\), so that \(c_2\ge0\), \(c_1-c_2=-A_Z>0\) and \(-N_S\) times the residue is
-- \(c_1\chi(\Sigma)-c_2\chi(Z)\). For even \(n\) it is \((c_1-c_2)(n-2)+c_1(1+b_\Sigma)+c_2b_Z>0\), and
-- \(\delta-n+\chi(\Sigma)>0\). For odd \(n\), \(\delta\ge4\) and \(b_\Sigma>\delta-1\ge n\) (odd), so
-- \(\chi(\Sigma)<0<\chi(Z)\), \(c_1\chi(\Sigma)-c_2\chi(Z)<0\), and \(\delta-n+\chi(\Sigma)=\delta-1-b_\Sigma<0\). For
-- \(\kappa<\gamma\) put \(j=\delta-n\), \(c=\gamma-\kappa\) and \(E=\kappa\delta+\rho\), so that \(D=N+d=\gamma E\),
-- \(\gamma^2-k_0=\gamma c\) and \(\beta=j/D\), and \(G=(k_0-1)D\chi(\Sigma)-\gamma cj\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\). With
-- \(\chi(Z)=\chi(\Sigma)-\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\), \(N_S\) times the residue is
-- \(\chi(\Sigma)A_Z+\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)(A_\star-A_Z)\), and with the forms of \(-A_Z\) and \(A_Z-A_\star\) above,
-- \(1-\alpha_V=(1-k_0\beta)/k_0\) and \(\alpha_V-k_0\beta=(k_0-1)(1-k_0\beta)/k_0\), \(\gamma\beta\alpha_V\) times it is
-- \(-(1-k_0\beta)G/(k_0D)\), so the residue has the sign of \(-G\). For \(n=2\),
-- \(\chi(\Sigma)=\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=\delta\) and \(\gamma cj<k_0(k_0-1)\delta<(k_0-1)D\), as \(\gamma\le k_0\), so \(G>0\).
-- For \(n=3\), \(\chi(\Sigma)=-\delta(\delta-3)\) and \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=-\delta(D+\delta-3)\)
-- \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966}, so that \(G=-\delta(\delta-3)\gamma\psi\) with \(\psi=E(\kappa^2-1-c^2)-c(\delta-3)\): for
-- \(c<\kappa\), that is \(\gamma<2\kappa\), (three) at \(c\), \(\kappa\), \(E\ge\delta+1\) and \(j=\delta-3\) gives \(\psi>0\),
-- and for \(c\ge\kappa\), \(\kappa^2-1-c^2<0\) and \(\psi<0\). For \(n=4\), \(\chi(\Sigma)=\delta c_\delta\) with
-- \(c_\delta=\delta^2-4\delta+6\) and \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=\delta(c_\delta+D(D+\delta-4))\)
-- \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966}, so (four) at \(g=\gamma\), \(c\), \(\kappa\), \(\rho\), \(j=\delta-4\), \(D\) and
-- \(k_1=k_0-1\) gives \(G<0\). For \(n\ge5\) put \(e=\delta-1\) and \(X=D-1\), so that \(X\ge2\delta+1\), as
-- \(\gamma\ge2\); (chiz) gives \((-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\ge\delta X^{n-2}\) as for \(\delta<n\) below, and
-- \(\delta|\chi(\Sigma)|\le e^n+n\delta\); (mup) at \(p=n-4\), \(j\), \(e\) and \(X\) for \((n,\delta)\ne(5,6)\), and
-- (mupsix) at \(X\) for \((n,\delta)=(5,6)\), give \((X+1)^2(e^n+n\delta)<2\delta^3jX^{n-2}\), so
-- \(D^2|\chi(\Sigma)|<2\delta j|\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)|\); and \(\gamma c/(k_0-1)>c/\kappa>2\delta/D\) by (phi) at
-- \(g=\gamma\), \(c\), \(\kappa\), \(\rho\) and \(\delta\), so \(\gamma cj|\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)|>(k_0-1)D|\chi(\Sigma)|\),
-- and \(G\) has the sign \((-1)^{n+1}\) of \(-\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\). For
-- \(\gamma=1\), the tie \(\alpha_L+\alpha_R+\alpha_V=1\) gives
-- \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)A_\star+\chi(Z)A_Z=\frac{(1-\alpha_R)(1-\alpha_V)}{\alpha_L\alpha_R\alpha_V}\big((1-\alpha_L)\chi(\Sigma)+\alpha_L\chi(Z)\big)\),
-- and \((N+d)\big((1-\alpha_L)\chi(\Sigma)+\alpha_L\chi(Z)\big)=Q\). Let \(d=1\) and \(\delta<n\), and put \(D=N+1\) and
-- \(m=n-\delta\). Then \(\alpha_L>0\), \(1-\alpha_V=\alpha_L+\alpha_R>0\) and \(k_0D\alpha_V=k_0(N-n)+k_0-1\), so the
-- factor of \(Q\) is positive for \(n\le N\) and negative for \(n>N\). By \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966},
-- \(\delta\chi(\Sigma)=n\delta-1+(1-\delta)^n\) and
-- \(\delta D(D-\delta)\chi(Z)=n\delta D(D-\delta)+D^2((1-\delta)^n-1)-\delta^2((1-D)^n-1)\), so that
-- \(\delta D(D-\delta)Q=A+(-1)^n(B-C)\) with \(A=(D-\delta)(D^2(n\delta-1)-m\delta)>0\),
-- \(B=D(D(D-\delta)+m\delta)(\delta-1)^n\ge0\) and \(C=m\delta^2N^n>0\). For \(\delta=1\),
-- \((k_0+1)Q=(n-1)\big((k_0+1)^2-1+(-k_0)^{n-1}\big)\): positive for odd \(n\), \(k_0\) at \(n=2\), and for even
-- \(n\ge4\) of the sign of \(k_0+2-k_0^{n-2}\), negative except at \((n,k_0)=(4,2)\), where it is \(0\). For \(\delta\ge2\)
-- and \(n\ge4\), (sign) at \(k_1=k_0-1\), \(e=\delta-1\) and \(m=n-\delta\), where \(m+e=n-1\ge3\), is \(C>A+B\); as
-- \(A>0\) and \(C>B\), \(Q>0\) for odd \(n\), and \(Q<0\) for even \(n\). For \(n=3\) and \(\delta=2\),
-- \(2D(D-2)Q=64k_0^3+16k_0^2-16k_0-4>0\). Let \(\delta<n\), and put \(e=\delta-1\) and \(X=D-1\), so that
-- \(X\ge2\delta\). The counts of the ray tie as \(D=\gamma(\kappa\delta+\rho)\), \(\nu_S=(\delta+m)\kappa+\rho\),
-- \(k_0=\gamma\kappa\) and \(V+\nu_S=\kappa D\); so (rstar) for \(V>0\), and (rneg) at \(W=-V\) for \(V<0\), at \(g=\gamma\),
-- \(\delta\), \(\nu=\nu_S\) and \(k_1=k_0-1\), give \(A_\star=\nu_S(V+\gamma m)/(mV)\) and
-- \(A_Z=(k_0-1)\nu_SD/(\gamma mV)\), and with \(\chi(Z)=\chi(\Sigma)-\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\) and the identity of counts
-- \(\gamma V=(k_0-1)D-k_0m\), \(N_S\) times the residue is \(\nu_SG/(\gamma mV)\). By \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966},
-- \(\delta\chi(\Sigma)=n\delta-1+(-e)^n\), so \(\delta|\chi(\Sigma)|\le e^n+n\delta\), and
-- \(F=(-1)^nD(D-\delta)\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=\delta X^n-De^n+(-1)^n(D-\delta)\); for \(\delta\ge2\), (chiz) at
-- \(y=D-\delta\) and \(k=n-2\) gives \(F\ge\delta D(D-\delta)X^{n-2}\), and for \(\delta=1\),
-- \(D\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=1+(-1)^nX^{n-1}\); so \((-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)>0\). Let \(n\ge4\). For
-- \(\delta\ge2\), (magn) at \(e\), \(m\) and \(X\), where \(m+e=n-1\), gives \(D^2|\chi(\Sigma)|<2\delta
-- m|\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)|\); for \(\delta=1\), \(\chi(\Sigma)=m\), and (magnone) at \(X\) and \(k=n-3\) gives
-- \(D^3\le2X^{n-1}-2\), so \(D^2|\chi(\Sigma)|\le2m|\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)|\), except at \(n=4\) and \(D\le4\). With
-- \(\varphi=(k_0-\gamma^2)/(k_0-1)\), \(G=(k_0-1)\big(D\chi(\Sigma)-\varphi m\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\big)\). For
-- \(\kappa\ne \gamma\), \(|\varphi|>2\delta/D\): for \(\gamma=1\), \(\varphi=1\) and \(D\ge2\delta+1\); for \(\gamma\ge2\), with
-- \(c=|\kappa-\gamma|\), \(|\varphi|=\gamma c/(k_0-1)>c/\kappa\), and (phi) at \(g=\gamma\), \(c\), \(\kappa\), \(\rho\) and
-- \(\delta\) is \(c/\kappa>2\delta/D\). So \(|\varphi|m|\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)|>D|\chi(\Sigma)|\), and \(G\) has the
-- sign of \(-\varphi(-1)^n\), that of \((\kappa-\gamma)(-1)^{n+1}\); at the three tuples with \(n=4\), \(\delta=1\) and
-- \(D\le4\), \(G=0\) at \((4,1,2,1)\), \(G=-18\) at \((4,1,3,1)\) and \(G=54\) at \((4,1,2,2)\). For \(\kappa=\gamma\),
-- \(G=(k_0-1)D\chi(\Sigma)\), and \(n\delta-1+(-e)^n\) is positive for even \(n\), is \(n-1\) or \(2n-2\) for
-- \(\delta\le2\), and is negative for odd \(n\) and \(\delta\ge3\) by (chiodd) at \(e\) and \(n\). For \(n=3\),
-- \(\delta\le2\), \(\chi(\Sigma)=2\) and \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=2-\delta D\), so
-- \(G=2\big((k_0-1)D+(k_0-\gamma^2)(D-m)\big)\), positive for \(\kappa\ge \gamma\); for \(\kappa<\gamma\), with \(j=\gamma-\kappa\),
-- \(G/2=\gamma D(\kappa-j)-D+\gamma jm\) is positive for \(j<\kappa\), below \(-D\) for \(j>\kappa\), as \(\gamma jm<2\gamma^2\le \gamma D\),
-- and \(k_0m-D\) for \(j=\kappa\). For \(n=2\), \(\delta=m=1\) and \(\chi(\Sigma)=\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=1\), so
-- \(G=(k_0-1)(D-1)+\gamma^2-1\), and \(V=\kappa(D-2)-\rho\ge\rho(k_0-1)>0\). At \(\gamma=1\), \(\varphi=1\) and
-- \(D-m=N+d+\delta-n\), so \(G=(k_0-1)Q\). This gives (ii).
--
-- \textit{(iii).} At \(T=t^{\nu_S}\) a component has the weight \(p^{-\nu}T^{N}=t^{-N_S\sigma}\), and \(X_S=1\), \(X_E=t^{\rho
-- k_0(\delta-n)}\) and \(p^{-1}T=t^{\nu_S-N_S}\), neither of the last two equal to \(1\), since \(t^k=1\) gives
-- \(p^k=1\). The points of the box of \((e_t,S)\) have \(\det(w,S)=1,\dots,\rho\), each once, so their costs are
-- \(j\alpha_L\), \(1\le j\le\rho\), and \(\sum_{j\le\rho}y^j/(1-y^\rho)=y/(1-y)\) at \(y=p^{-\alpha_L}\) gives
-- \((1-p^{-1})P_1/(1-X_E)=L(\alpha_L)/p\); in the same way \(P_2=L(\alpha_R)/p\), and \(F=1-L(\alpha_V)\). With
-- \(\#\Sigma=\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\#Z\) and \(\#\tilde V=\sum_\xi r(\xi)\) this gives the value of \((1-X_S)Z_0\) in
-- (iii): the fibre of the corner over \(\xi\) is a line with its two neighbours and the \(r(\xi)\) points of the strict
-- transform over \(\mathbb F_p\) as marks, each mark of cost \(\alpha\) counting \(L(\alpha)\) in place of one point.
--
-- For \(\delta>n\) put \(M=N_S\), \(R=N+d\), \(E=\nu_S\) and \(V=M-E\), so that \(-\alpha_LM=A\), \(\alpha_RM=R\),
-- \(\alpha_VM=V\), and the tie reads \(A+\gamma E=R\); then \((p-1)+L(\alpha_L)=-[M]/[A]\), \(L(\alpha_R)=[M]/[R]\) and
-- \(L(\alpha_V)=[M]/[V]\). For a rational \(t>1\) put \(e=t-1\), so that the counts \([k]\) are positive masses with
-- \(e[k]+1=t^k\): (qstep) gives \([x+y]=[x]+t^x[y]\) and \(1/[x]=1/[x+y]+t^x[y]/([x][x+y])\), (qincr) gives for counts
-- \(x<y\) that \(I(x)=t^x/([x][x+E])\) exceeds \(I(y)\) by
-- \(t^x[y-x]\big([x]+t^x[y-x]+t^y[x+E]\big)/([x][x+E][y][y+E])\), and \cref{lem:qnumber}(product)
-- gives \([a+b][a+c]=[a+b+c][a]+t^a[b][c]\). Telescoping the steps of \(E\) from \(A\) to \(R\),
-- \(-\Lambda_r=[M][E]\big(\sum_{i=1}^{\gamma}(I(R-iE)-I(V))+(\gamma-r)I(V)\big)\),
-- \(-\Lambda_Z=[M]\big([M]t^A[(\gamma-1)E]+t^{R-E}[M-R][E]\big)/([A][R][V])\),
-- the second by \cref{lem:qnumber}(product) at \(a=R-E\), \(b=E\), \(c=M-R\) and by \([R-E]=[A]+t^A[(\gamma-1)E]\), each immediate when the
-- count \(M-R\) or \((\gamma-1)E\) is \(0\). Each \(R-iE\) is at most \(V\), and below it for \(i=1\) when \(\kappa>1\) and
-- for \(i=2\) when \(\kappa=1\), as then \(\gamma=k_0\ge2\); and \(\kappa\) and \(\gamma\) are not both \(1\). So \(-\Lambda_r\)
-- and \(-\Lambda_Z\) are quotients of sums of powers of \(t\) with nonnegative coefficients, neither zero. For \(\gamma=1\),
-- (qincr) at \(x=A=R-E\), \(y=V\) and \([A]+t^A[M-R]+t^{A+M-R}[R]=[M+A]\) give
-- \(-\Lambda_1=t^A[E][M-R][M+A]/([A][R][V])\) and \(-\Lambda_Z=t^A[M][M-R][E]/([A][R][V])\), the expression of (iii). In
-- the letter \(z=1/t\), with \([x]_z=(1-z^{xN_S})/(1-z^{N_S})\) for a cost \(x\), it is
-- \(-(1-p^{-1})p^{2-n}z^A\frac{[1-\alpha_R]_z[1-\alpha_V]_z}{[\alpha_R]_z[\alpha_V]_z[\beta]_z}\big([1+\beta]_z\#(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z)+z^A\#Z\big)\), the q-analogue of (corner): \(X=z^A\), \(Y=z^{\nu_S}\) and \(W=z^{N_S-N-d}\), which lie below \(1\)
-- at every rational \(t>1\), as \(A\), \(\nu_S\) and \(N_S-N-d=(k_0-1)(N+d)\) are at least \(1\) for \(\gamma=1\), where
-- \(\kappa=k_0\ge2\), have \(XY=z^{N+d}\), \(XW=z^{N_S-\nu_S}\) and \(XYW=z^{N_S}\), the masses \([\alpha_R]_z\),
-- \([\alpha_V]_z\), \([\beta]_z\), \([1-\alpha_R]_z\) and \([1-\alpha_V]_z\) satisfy the ties of (qcorner), whose
-- first and last clauses give the two factors, and (qtie) says that their q-numbers exceed the tie of the costs by a
-- positive mass.
--
-- For \(\delta<n\) and
-- \(s_S>-1\) the three costs are positive, \(L(\alpha)=[M]/[\alpha M]\), and \(p-1-r\ge0\), so \(\Lambda_r\) and
-- \(\Lambda_Z\) are such quotients. These are identities between quotients of polynomials in \(t\) with rational
-- coefficients, true at every rational \(t>1\); so they hold as identities of rational functions, and in the field
-- \(\mathbb Q[t]/(t^{N_S}-p)\), where \(t^{N_S}-p\) is irreducible by the criterion of Eisenstein. There a sum of
-- powers of \(t\) with nonnegative coefficients, not all zero, is not zero, since by \(t^{N_S}=p\) it reduces to a
-- combination of \(1,t,\dots,t^{N_S-1}\) whose coefficients are nonnegative and not all zero; so no \([k]\) with
-- \(k\ge1\) is zero there, and a sum over the points of \(\Sigma\) of such quotients is zero only when there is no
-- point. For \(n\ge3\), \(\Sigma\) is irreducible and has a point over \(\mathbb F_p\) at every large prime
-- \cite{LangWeil1954}. This evaluation is the bridge from the rows to (iii). For \(\gamma=1\), \(r(\xi)=1\) at every point
-- of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\), and the tie reads \(\alpha_L+\alpha_R+\alpha_V=1\). Put \(a=t^{\alpha_LN_S}\),
-- \(b=t^{\alpha_RN_S}\) and \(c=t^{\alpha_VN_S}\), so that \(abc=p\) and \(L(\alpha_L)=(abc-1)/(a-1)\), and in the same
-- way at \(b\) and \(c\). With \(u=a-1\), \(v=b-1\), \(w=c-1\) and \(m=abc-1\), the clauses of (qone), with \(m\)
-- replaced, are identities of polynomials in \(u,v,w\), as they hold at all positive rationals; as
-- \(uvw\Lambda_1=(m-1)uvw+m(vw+uw+uv)\) and \(uvw\Lambda_Z=m(uvw+vw+mu)\), they read \(uvw\Lambda_1=(ab-1)(ac-1)(bc-1)\)
-- and \(uvw\Lambda_Z=m(ab-1)(ac-1)\) for every \(a,b,c\ne1\) in the field. With \(ab=t^{(1-\alpha_V)N_S}\),
-- \(ac=t^{(1-\alpha_R)N_S}\), \(bc=t^{(1-\alpha_L)N_S}\) and \(m=p-1\), dividing by \((p-1)^3\) gives the value for
-- \(\gamma=1\), and for \(\delta>n\) the expression in \([k]\) follows from \(\langle\alpha\rangle=[\alpha N_S]/[N_S]\) at
-- the positive costs and \(\langle\alpha_L\rangle=-t^{-A}[A]/[N_S]\). For a cost \(\alpha>0\),
-- \(\langle\alpha\rangle=[\alpha N_S]/[N_S]\), and for \(\alpha<0\), \(\langle\alpha\rangle=-t^{\alpha
-- N_S}\langle-\alpha\rangle\). Here \(1-\alpha_V=-s_S\) and \(1-\alpha_R=1-1/k_0\) are positive, \(\alpha_L\) has the
-- side of \(n-\delta\) and \(\alpha_V\) that of \(s_S+1\), and \(\alpha_L<0\) gives \(s_S>-1\). So for \(\alpha_L<1\),
-- \(\langle1-\alpha_L\rangle\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\#Z=\big([(1-\alpha_L)N_S]\#(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z)+[N_S]\#Z\big)/[N_S]\), which gives the sign \((-1)^{[\delta>n]+[s_S<-1]}\); at \(\alpha_L=1\),
-- \(\langle0\rangle=0\) and \(\alpha_V=-\alpha_R<0\). For \(\alpha_L>1\),
-- \((p-1)\big(\langle1-\alpha_L\rangle\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\#Z\big)=(t^{(1-\alpha_L)N_S}-1)\#(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z)+(p-1)\#Z\); if \(\alpha_L\) is not whole, \(t^{(1-\alpha_L)N_S}\) is a rational times \(t^j\) with \(0<j<N_S\), so
-- this is zero only when both counts are, and if \(\ell=\alpha_L-1\) is whole it is
-- \(p^{-\ell}\big(p^\ell(p-1)\#Z-(p^\ell-1)\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)\big)\).
--
-- \textit{(iv).} For \(\delta=n\), \(\sigma_E=N(s+1/k_0)\) and \(\sigma_S=N_S(s+1/k_0)\), and \(\rho\chi(\Sigma)/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)\)
-- gives the pole of order two; there \(\chi(\Sigma)\ne0\) for \(n\ne3\): it exceeds \(n-2\) for even \(n\), and for odd
-- \(n\ge5\) it is \(n-1-b_\Sigma<0\) by (odd) at \(e=n-1\). For \(n=3\), \(\Sigma\) is a smooth plane cubic,
-- \(\chi(\Sigma)=0\), \(Z\) is \(3(N+d)\) points, and with \(s/(1+s)=-1/(k_0-1)\) at \(-1/k_0\) the residue is
-- \(1/k_0+3(\kappa-\gamma)/(\kappa(k_0-1))\); it is zero only if \(\kappa(4k_0-1)=3k_0\gamma\), that is \(\gamma(4\kappa-3\gamma)=1\),
-- which forces \(\gamma=\kappa=1\) and \(k_0=1\). This gives (iv).
--
-- \textit{(v).} If the origin were not an isolated critical point, a curve of critical points would pass through it
-- \cite[\S3]{Milnor1968}, and \(f\) would be \(0\) on it, as it is constant on it; but the resolution is an isomorphism
-- off the fibre over the origin, and near that fibre the strict transform is smooth, of multiplicity \(1\), so near the
-- origin the gradient of \(f\) does not vanish on \(f=0\) off the origin. The parts over the origin of the components, off
-- the other components of \(f=0\), have the Euler characteristics \(n-\chi(\Sigma)\) for \(E\);
-- \(-(\gamma-[\kappa=1])\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\) for the corner, a line less its \(\gamma\) points, less the neighbour on the
-- side of \(e_t\), and less the neighbour on the side of \(e_u\) when \(\kappa>1\); for \(\kappa>1\),
-- \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\) for the component next to \(e_u\), of multiplicity \(N+d\), a line less one point; \(0\) for
-- every other component over \(\Sigma\setminus Z\), a line less two points; and \(0\) over \(Z\), where the fibres are tori
-- cut by the strict transform along tori. By the formula of A'Campo \cite{ACampo1975}, as stated in
-- \cite[(2.1.2)]{Denef1991}, a root of unity of order \(e\ge2\) has the multiplicity \((-1)^{n-1}\sum_{e\mid
-- N_i}\chi(E_i^\circ\cap\pi^{-1}(0))\), and the Milnor fibre has the Euler characteristic
-- \(\sum_iN_i\chi(E_i^\circ\cap\pi^{-1}(0))\) \cite{ACampo1975}, which is \(1+(-1)^{n-1}\mu\) \cite{Milnor1968}. With
-- \((-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))=b_\Sigma+(-1)^{n-1}\) and \((-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=B\) this gives \(m(e)\) and
-- the Milnor number; for \(\kappa=1\), \(N_S=N+d\) and the terms of the corner and of \(e_u\) merge. For the
-- positivity, \(e_S\) divides \(N_S\), and it divides \(N+d\) only if \(\kappa=1\), since \(\gcd(\nu_S,\kappa)=1\);
-- \(e_E\) divides \(N\), and when it divides \(N+d\) it divides \(N_S\). First \(B\ge1\): for even \(n\) clearly; for
-- odd \(n\) it asks \(b_\Sigma+b_Z\ge2\), which holds by (odd) for \(\delta\ge4\), for \(\delta=3\) by the tie
-- \(3b_\Sigma+e=e^n\ge e^3\) at \(e=2\), for \(\delta=1\) by (even), as \(Z\) is then a hypersurface of degree
-- \(e=N+d\ge3\) in a projective space of dimension \(n-2\) and \(b_Z\ge e-1\), and for \(\delta=2\) by \(b_Z=2(N+d)-1\)
-- at \(n=3\), \(Z\) being \(2(N+d)\) points, and by (bz) at \(n\ge5\). There \(Z\) is a complete intersection of type
-- \((2,e)\), \(e=N+d\ge5\), of even dimension \(m=n-3\), and the formula of the Chern classes of complete intersections
-- \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966}, \(\chi(Z)=2e[h^m](1+h)^{m+3}/((1+2h)(1+eh))\), with \(b_Z=\chi(Z)-m-1\) and partial
-- fractions in \(h\), gives \(e(e-2)b_Z=2(e-1)^{m+3}+e^2-4e+2\), the tie of (bz) at \(c=e-2\) and \(x=(e-1)^{m+1}\); so
-- \(b_Z>2(e-1)^{m+1}\). Then \(m(e_S)=(\gamma-[\kappa=1])B+[e_S\mid N](b_\Sigma+(-1)^{n-1})\), whose first coefficient is
-- at least \(1\), as \(\kappa=1\) forces \(\gamma=k_0\ge2\): the second term is at least \(0\) for odd \(n\) and for even
-- \(n\) with \(\delta\ge2\), by (even), and for even \(n\) and \(\delta=1\) it is at least \(-1\) while
-- \(B-1=b_Z\ge2\), by (odd) for \(e\ge4\) and for \(e=3\) by the tie \(3b_Z+2=(e-1)^{n-1}\ge8\). And
-- \(m(e_E)=b_\Sigma+(-1)^{n-1}+c_EB\), with \(c_E=\gamma[e_E\mid N_S]-[e_E\mid N+d]\ge0\): it is at least \(1\) for odd
-- \(n\), and for even \(n\) and \(\delta\ge3\) by (even); for even \(n\) and \(\delta\le2\), \(\delta\) divides \(n\),
-- so \(e_E\) divides \(k_0\), which divides \(N_S\), and \(c_E=0\) would ask \(\gamma=1\) and \(e_E\mid\gcd(N,N+d,k_0)\),
-- which divides \(\gcd(k_0,d)=1\); so \(c_E\ge1\) and \(m(e_E)\ge b_\Sigma-1+B\ge1\), as \(b_\Sigma=1\) for
-- \(\delta=2\) and \(b_Z\ge2\) for \(\delta=1\). For \(n=2\), \(Z=\emptyset\), \(b_Z=0\), \(b_\Sigma=\delta-1\) and
-- \(B=\delta\), so for \(\delta\ge2\), \(m(e_S)\ge\delta+[e_S\mid N](\delta-2)\ge2\) and
-- \(m(e_E)=\delta-2+c_E\delta\ge1\), with \(c_E\ge1\) for \(\delta=2\) as above. For \(n=2\) and \(\delta=1\),
-- \(N_S=\kappa k_0+k_0\rho>2\kappa+\rho=\nu_S\), so \(e_S\ge2\), and \(m(e_S)=\gamma-[\kappa=1]-[e_S\mid k_0]\), as
-- \(b_\Sigma=0\) and \(B=1\); its first two terms are at least \(2\) unless \(\gamma=1\), or \(\gamma=k_0=2\), and then
-- \(e_S>k_0\): for \(\gamma=1\), \(\nu_S=2k_0+d\) is prime to \(k_0\) and to \(k_0+d\), so \(e_S=k_0(k_0+d)\), and for
-- \(\gamma=k_0=2\), \(N_S=2+2\rho\) and \(\nu_S=2+\rho\), so \(e_S=2(1+\rho)/\gcd(2,\rho)\ge3\). This gives (v).
--
-- \textit{(vi).} On the tube of \(\xi\) write \(\tau\), \(\upsilon\) and \(\eta\) for the residues of the angular parts of
-- \(t\), \(u\) and \(y\). On a component the angular part of \(f\) is one monomial in them: \(\tau^Nh(\xi)^{k_0}\) off
-- \(\Sigma\); \(\tau^N\upsilon^{k_0}\) where \(u^{k_0}\) leads; \(\tau^{N+d}g(\xi)\) over \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) and
-- \(\tau^{N+d}\eta\) over \(Z\) where \(t^dW\) leads; on the corner \(\tau^N(\upsilon^{k_0}+g(\xi)\tau^d)\) where this
-- is not \(0\), and where it is \(0\) the angular part is uniform, by Hensel's lemma. The sum of \(\omega\) over the
-- residues of a component is \(0\) unless \(\omega\) is trivial on its monomial; on the corner the substitution
-- \((\upsilon,\tau)\mapsto(\lambda^\rho\upsilon,\lambda^\kappa\tau)\) multiplies the monomial by \(\lambda^{N_S}\), as
-- \(\kappa d=\rho k_0\), so its sum is \(0\) unless \(\omega^{N_S}=1\). Over \(Z\) the component \((e_u,S,R)\) carries
-- \(\omega(\eta)\) and sums to \(0\); for fixed \(\tau,\upsilon\) the value \(\upsilon^{k_0}+\tau^d\eta\) runs over
-- \(\mathbb F_p\) less \(\upsilon^{k_0}\), and for fixed \((a,b)\) with \(k_0b>da\) the components above the plane and those
-- on it cancel, as \(p^{-(m+1)}/(1-p^{-1})=p^{-m}/(p-1)\). So the terms with the denominator \(1-X_E\) need
-- \(\omega^N=1\), those with \(1-X_S\) need \(\omega^{k_0}=1\), \(\omega^{N+d}=1\) or \(\omega^{N_S}=1\), each of which
-- gives \(\omega^{N_S}=1\), and no term has the denominator \(1-p^{-1}T\). If \(\omega\) is not trivial on \(1+p\Z_p\)
-- and \(p\) does not divide \(N(N+d)\), on each residue polydisc of each component \(f\) is, after a change of coordinates
-- that keeps the measure, a unit constant times \(z^m\) times a factor free of the coordinate \(z\), with \(m\) one of
-- \(N\), \(k_0\), \(N+d\), \(1\), prime to \(p\). On the corner this rests on two facts. First, \(p\) does not divide
-- \(k_0\), a divisor of \(N\), so the derivative \(k_0\bar u_0^{k_0-1}\) of \(\bar\phi\) in \(u_0\) is not \(0\), and
-- by Hensel's lemma \((t_0,\phi)\) are coordinates of the residue class in place of \((t_0,u_0)\), with a Jacobian that
-- is a unit; as \(f=p^{Na+k_0b}t_0^N\phi\), \(z\) is \(t_0\) and \(m=N\) where \(\bar\phi\ne0\), and \(z\) is \(\phi\)
-- and \(m=1\) where \(\bar\phi=0\). Second, for \(m\) prime to \(p\), \(x\mapsto x^m\) is an automorphism of
-- \(1+p\Z_p\), a pro-\(p\) group, as it is one of each quotient \((1+p\Z_p)/(1+p^j\Z_p)\), of order \(p^{j-1}\); this
-- holds for \(p=2\) too, where \(1+2\Z_2=\Z_2^\times\), and \(2\) can be such a prime, when \(N\) is odd and \(d\) is
-- even. So \(\omega^m\) is not trivial on \(1+p\Z_p\), and its integral over a coset of \(1+p\Z_p\), the values of
-- \(z\) of one order and one residue, is \(0\); this agrees with \cite[Theorem~3.3]{Denef1991}, whose value is \(0\)
-- here, as at all but finitely many primes the origin is the only critical point of \(f\) in \((p\Z_p)^n\). For
-- \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\) the cones of a unimodular subdivision are kept whole or not at all: their rays are a basis of the
-- lattice of their span,
-- on which the multiplicity is linear, so \(e\) divides it on every ray exactly when \(e\) divides its coefficients on
-- that lattice, \(k_0\) on \((e_t,S)\), \((e_t,S,R)\), \((e_t,R,e_y)\) and the plane of \(S\) and \(R\), \(N+d\) on
-- \((S,e_u)\), and \(1\) on \((e_u,S,R)\); the strict transform, of multiplicity \(1\), is never kept. So the term of
-- \(E\) is kept when \(e\mid N\), the terms over \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) are \([e\mid k_0]\rho/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)+[e\mid
-- N+d]\kappa/\sigma_S-[e\mid N_S]\gamma/\sigma_S\), and over \(Z\), by the second clause of (cell), \([e\mid k_0]\big(\rho
-- k_0/(\sigma_E\sigma_S\sigma_R)+1/(\sigma_E\sigma_R)-\kappa/(\sigma_S\sigma_R)\big)=[e\mid
-- k_0]\rho/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)\). This gives (vi).
--
-- \textit{(vii).} Let \(\delta=1\). Then \(N=k_0\), \(\Sigma\) is a hyperplane, \(\chi(\Sigma)=n-1\), and with
-- \(q=p^{n-1}\), \(\#(\mathbb P^{n-1}\setminus\Sigma)=q\) and \(\#\Sigma=(q-1)/(p-1)\). By (i) the terms of
-- \((1-X_S)Z_0/(1-p^{-1})\) with the denominator \(1-X_E\) have the numerator
-- \(p^{1-n}qX_E(1-X_S)+p^{2-n}(1-p^{-1})\#\Sigma P_1\). At a zero of \(1-X_E\), \(T^{k_0}=p^n\); as
-- \(N_S=k_0(\kappa+\rho)\), \(X_S=q^\rho\), and each term of \(P_1\) is \(q^b\), so \(P_1/(1-X_S)=-q/(q-1)\) and the
-- numerator is \((1-q^\rho)+(q^\rho-1)=0\). The \(N\) zeros of \(1-X_E\) are simple, so \(1-X_E\) divides the
-- numerator, and \((1-X_S)(1-p^{-1}T)Z_0\) is a polynomial. For \(n\ne N\), \(T=p\) is no zero of \(1-X_E\), and
-- \(X_S=q^\rho\ne1\) at its zeros, so none of them is a pole. At \(-n/N\), \(\sigma_S=\rho(1-n)\), as \(\kappa d=\rho
-- k_0\), so by (i) and (vi) the residues of \(Z_{top,0}\), for \(n\ne N\), and of \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\) are \(1/N\) and
-- \([e\mid k_0]/N\) times \(n-\chi(\Sigma)+\rho\chi(\Sigma)/(\rho(1-n))=0\). If \(s_S=-1\), then \(\nu_S=N_S\), so
-- \(\kappa\) divides \(\rho\), hence \(\kappa=1\), and \(n+\rho=N+d\), with \(n-N=\rho(k_0-1)>0\); the converse is
-- clear. Then \(\sigma_S=N_S(1+s)\), and in (i) the only term with \((1+s)^2\) in its denominator is
-- \(-\big(\gamma\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\chi(Z)\big)s/(N_S(1+s)^2)\). There \(\delta<n\), \(V=0\), \(\gamma=k_0>\kappa\) and
-- \(k_0m=(k_0-1)D\), so \(G=(k_0-1)D\big(\gamma\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\chi(Z)\big)\); for \(n\ge4\) its sign in (ii), which
-- does not use \(s_S\ne-1\), is \((-1)^n\), and none of the three tuples with \(n=4\), \(\delta=1\) and \(D\le4\) has
-- \(s_S=-1\). For \(n=3\), \(k_0\delta+(k_0-1)\rho=3\) forces \((k_0,\delta,\rho)=(2,1,1)\), so \(d=2\), \(\Sigma\) is a line and \(Z\)
-- four points, and \(\gamma\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)+\chi(Z)=2(-2)+4=0\); there (i) is
-- \(\frac1{3+2s}+\frac{2+s}{(1+s)(3+2s)}\), with the residue \(1\) at \(-1\). At \(T=p\), \(X_S=1\), \(X_E=p^{N-n}\ne1\) and
-- \(P_2=p^{N+d-n-\rho-1}=p^{-1}\), and in (i) only the terms with \(F\) are not regular at \(T=p\) after the factor
-- \(1-X_S\); so the value is \((1-p^{-1})p^{2-n}(1-p)(-p^{-1})(\#\tilde V+\#Z)\).
--
-- Let \(s_S<-1\) and \(\gamma>1\). Then
-- \(n\kappa+\rho>\kappa(N+d)\), and \(N\ge k_0=\gamma\kappa\ge2\kappa\) and \(d=\gamma\rho\ge2\rho\) give
-- \(n>N+d-\rho/\kappa\ge2\kappa+\rho\ge3\) and \(n>N>\delta\). With \(M=N_S\), the costs give the counts
-- \(a_L=\alpha_LM=k_0(n-\delta)\), \(a_R=\alpha_RM=N+d\le M\) and \(k=-\alpha_VM=\nu_S-N_S\ge1\), and the tie gives
-- \(\alpha_L=\gamma(1-\alpha_V)-\alpha_R\ge1-2\alpha_V>1-\alpha_V\), so \(a_L>M+k\). As \(p-1=(t-1)[M]\),
-- \(L(\alpha_L)=[M]/[a_L]\), \(L(\alpha_R)=[M]/[a_R]\), \(L=[M]/[k]\) and \(L(\alpha_V)=-(p-1)-L\), which gives
-- \(\Lambda_r=\Lambda_0-r(p+L)\) and, summed over the points, the value. \(\Lambda_0=(p-1)+L(\alpha_L)+L(\alpha_R)\) is
-- such a quotient; by the add law of (qstep), \([M]-[a_R]=t^{a_R}[M-a_R]\) and
-- \([M][a_L]-[a_R][k]=[M]t^k[a_L-k]+t^{a_R}[M-a_R][k]\), so \(-\Lambda_Z=(p-1)(L(\alpha_R)-1)+L(\alpha_R)L-L(\alpha_L)\)
-- is \((p-1)t^{a_R}[M-a_R]/[a_R]+[M]\big([M]t^k[a_L-k]+t^{a_R}[M-a_R][k]\big)/([a_R][k][a_L])\), not zero as \(a_L>k\).
--
-- Let \(-3/2<s_S<-1\), so that \(2k<M\), and say that \(y\) exceeds \(x\) when \(y-x\) is such a quotient, not zero, so
-- that \(y\ne x\), as in (iii). A power \(t^a\) with \(a\ge1\) exceeds a rational \(c\ge0\) when \(p^a>c^M\), as
-- \(t^a-c=(p^a-c^M)/\sum_{i<M}c^it^{a(M-1-i)}\); and such a quotient \(x\) exceeds \(y\), \(0\) or such a quotient,
-- when \(x^2\) exceeds \(y^2\), as \(x-y=(x^2-y^2)/(x+y)\). For \(n\ge4\), \(\Sigma\), \(Z\) and the cover \(Y\) of
-- \(\Sigma\) given by \(v^{\gamma}=-g\), which has \(\#\tilde V+\#Z\) points over \(\mathbb F_p\), are irreducible over
-- \(\overline{\mathbb F}_p\): \(\Sigma\) and \(Z\) as smooth complete intersections of positive dimension, and \(Y\) as
-- follows. With \(w=\kappa\delta+\rho\), \(g\) is a form of degree \(\gamma w\), so \(Y\), cut out by \(h\) and
-- \(v^{\gamma}+g\), lies in the weighted projective space \(\mathbb P(1,\dots,1,w)\), with the weight \(w\) on \(v\),
-- and misses its vertex, where \(\xi=0\) and so \(v^{\gamma}=0\); off the vertex that space is covered by the affine
-- charts \(\xi_i\ne0\). There \(Y\) is smooth over \(\overline{\mathbb F}_p\), by the Jacobian of \((h,v^{\gamma}+g)\),
-- whose rows are \((dh,0)\) and \((dg,\gamma v^{\gamma-1})\): where \(v\ne0\), \(\gamma v^{\gamma-1}\ne0\), as a good
-- prime does not divide \(\gamma\), and \(dh\ne0\), as \(\Sigma\) is smooth; where \(v=0\), \(g=0\), so the point lies
-- over \(Z\), where \(dh\) and \(dg\) are independent. The map \(Y\to\Sigma\), \((\xi,v)\mapsto\xi\), is finite, as
-- \(v^{\gamma}+g\) is monic in \(v\), and totally ramified along \(\{v=0\}\), which it maps isomorphically onto \(Z\);
-- so the image of a connected component of \(Y\), closed and of dimension \(n-2\), is \(\Sigma\), and every connected
-- component of \(Y\) meets \(\{v=0\}\), which is connected as \(Z\) is. So \(Y\) is connected, and, smooth and
-- connected, it is irreducible. It is closed in \(\mathbb P(1,\dots,1,w)\), and a Veronese map defined over
-- \(\mathbb Z\) embeds that space in a projective space: the forms of degree \(w\) in \(\xi\) and the coordinate \(v\) map it
-- onto a closed subvariety of \(\mathbb P^{M'}\), \(M'=\binom{n-1+w}{n-1}\), and the image of \(Y\), cut out by \(h\)
-- and \(v^{\gamma}+g\) of
-- weighted degrees \(\delta\) and \(\gamma w\), has dimension \(n-2\) and degree \(\delta \gamma w^{n-2}\). By
-- \cite[Theorem~1]{LangWeil1954} a closed subvariety of \(\mathbb P^{M'}\) of dimension \(r\) and degree \(D'\),
-- irreducible over \(\overline{\mathbb F}_p\), has \(p^r+E\) points over \(\mathbb F_p\) with
-- \(E^2\le\big((D'-1)(D'-2)+A'\big)^2p^{2r-1}\), where \(A'\) depends only on \(M'\), \(r\) and \(D'\); for \(\Sigma\) and
-- \(Z\) in \(\mathbb P^{n-1}\), of degrees \(\delta\) and \(\delta(N+d)\), and for \(Y\) these do not depend on \(p\).
-- So there is a count \(C\), the same at every good prime, with \(E_1^2\le Cp^{2n-3}\) and \(E_2^2\le Cp^{2n-5}\) for
-- \(E_1=(p-1)\#\Sigma-p\#\tilde V\) and \(E_2=\#\Sigma-p\#Z\), and at every large prime \(\#\tilde V\ge p^{n-2}/2\) and
-- \(\#\Sigma\le2p^{n-2}\). Put \(x=p^{n-2}t^{M-k}/6\). Then \(\#\tilde VL-3x\) is \(0\) or such a quotient, as
-- \(L-t^{M-k}=[M-k]/[k]\); and at every large good prime \(x\) exceeds \(|E_1|\), as \(x^2=p^{2n-3}t^{M-2k}/36\), it
-- exceeds \(\#\Sigma L(\alpha_L)\), as \(1-L(\alpha_L)=t^M[a_L-M]/[a_L]\) and \(x\) exceeds \(2p^{n-2}\), and it
-- exceeds \(|E_2|Mt^{M-1}\), as \(x^2=p^{2n-4}t^{M-2}t^{M+2-2k}/36\), which is not below \(|E_2|L(\alpha_R)\), as
-- \(Mt^{M-1}-[M]\) and \([a_R]-1\) are \(0\) or such quotients. So minus the bracket of the value, \((\#\tilde
-- VL-3x)+(x-|E_1|)+(x-\#\Sigma
-- L(\alpha_L))+(x-|E_2|L(\alpha_R))+(|E_1|-E_1)+(|E_2|-E_2)L(\alpha_R)+\#Z\,L(\alpha_R)L\), exceeds \(0\).
--
-- Let now \(\delta=1\) and \(-2<s_S<-1\), so that \(k<M\). Then \(\Sigma\) is a hyperplane, and with
-- \(\pi_r=1+p+\dots+p^r\), the number of points of \(\mathbb P^r\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), \(\#\Sigma=\pi_{n-2}\); write
-- \(e_X=\#X-\pi_r\) for a variety \(X\) of dimension \(r\). By \cite[Theorem~8.1]{Deligne1974} a smooth complete
-- intersection \(X\) of dimension \(r\) over \(\mathbb F_p\) has \(e_X^2\le b^2p^r\), an inequality between integers,
-- where \(b\) is the primitive Betti number of a smooth complex complete intersection of the same dimension and
-- multidegree, read off its Euler characteristic \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966} as in (v); for \(Z\), \(b=b_Z\) and
-- \(r=n-3\). For \(a\) in \(\mathbb F_p^*\) let \(X'_a\) be cut out by \(h\) and \(au^{N+d}+g\) in the projective space
-- of dimension \(n\) with the further coordinate \(u\). At a good prime that does not divide \(N+d\) it is a smooth
-- complete intersection of dimension \(n-2\): where \(u=0\), on \(Z\), its two differentials are \(dh\) and \(dg\),
-- which are independent, and where \(u\ne0\) the derivative \((N+d)au^{N+d-1}\) in \(u\) is not zero, while \(dh\) is
-- not zero and has no part in \(du\); let \(b_X\) be its \(b\), which depends on \(n\) and \(N+d\) only. Let \(A(c)\) be the number
-- of \(\xi\) in \(\mathbb F_p^n\) with \(h(\xi)=0\) and \(g(\xi)=c\). The points of \(X'_a\) with \(u=0\) are those of
-- \(Z\), and those with \(u=1\) are \(A(-a)\) in number; and \((p-1)\#\tilde V\) is the number of pairs of a \(\xi\) in
-- \(\mathbb F_p^n\) with \(h(\xi)=0\) and \(g(\xi)\ne0\) and a \(v\) in \(\mathbb F_p\) with \(v^{\gamma}=-g(\xi)\), as
-- each point of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) has \(p-1\) representatives, and counted by \(v\), which is not \(0\), this
-- number is \(\sum_{v\ne0}A(-v^{\gamma})\). So \((p-1)(\#\tilde V+\#Z)=\sum_{v\in\mathbb F_p^*}\#X'_{v^{\gamma}}\), an identity
-- of counts; \(e_Y=\#\tilde V+\#Z-\pi_{n-2}\) is the mean of the \(e_{X'_{v^{\gamma}}}\) over \(v\in\mathbb F_p^*\), and
-- \(e_Y^2\le b_X^2p^{n-2}\), as the square of a mean is at most the mean of the squares. With \(\#Z=\pi_{n-3}+e_Z\) and
-- \(\pi_{n-2}=1+p\pi_{n-3}\), \(E_1=-1-pe_Y+pe_Z\) and \(E_2=1-pe_Z\), so \(E_1^2\le3(1+p^2e_Y^2+p^2e_Z^2)\le C_1p^n\)
-- and \(E_2^2\le2(1+p^2e_Z^2)\le C_2p^{n-1}\), with the counts \(C_1=3(1+b_X^2+b_Z^2)\) and \(C_2=2(1+b_Z^2)\), the
-- same at every good prime; and \(\#\tilde V=p^{n-2}+e_Y-e_Z\ge p^{n-2}/2\) once \(p^{n-2}\ge8(b_X^2+b_Z^2)\), as
-- \((e_Y-e_Z)^2\le2(e_Y^2+e_Z^2)\), while \(\#\Sigma\le2p^{n-2}\). Here \(M=\gamma\kappa(\kappa+\rho)\) and
-- \(k+M=n\kappa+\rho\), with \(\gamma\ge2\), so by (quarter) the counts \(a_1=(n-2)M-2k\) and \(a_3=(n-3)M+2-2k\) are at
-- least \(1\), and so is \(a_2=M-k\). With \(x\) as above, \(\#\tilde VL-3x\) is again \(0\) or such a quotient, as
-- \(k<M\); and at every large good prime \(x\) exceeds \(|E_1|\), as \(x^2=p^nt^{a_1}/36\) and \(t^{a_1}\) exceeds
-- \(36C_1\), it exceeds \(\#\Sigma L(\alpha_L)\), as \(x\) exceeds \(2p^{n-2}\) once \(t^{a_2}\) exceeds \(12\), and it
-- exceeds \(|E_2|Mt^{M-1}\), as \(x^2=p^{n-1}t^{2M-2}t^{a_3}/36\) and \(t^{a_3}\) exceeds \(36C_2M^2\). So the same sum
-- exceeds \(0\). This gives (vii).
--
-- \textit{The split and the chain.} The split is that of a cone of two rays, \cref{lem:cone}, with the whole of index
-- \(1\) and the parts of indices \(b\) and \(a\); a step of the chain is the same split, with the whole of index
-- \(m+1\) and the parts of indices \(m\) and \(1\). The second clause of (cell) is the circuit \(S+\rho k=\kappa
-- E+\rho R\) of the costs divided by \(ESR\), and the first is the second, then \cref{lem:cone}(apex) at \(\mu=\kappa\)
-- and \(X=SR\), then (countsplit) at \(R=k(1+s)+1\).
--
-- \textit{The square.} Over \(bxy\) the bottom half is \(nb(x+y)+X(b+nX)uw=(b+n)xy\), the sum of \(b\) times the tie
-- \(xy=nb+Xuw\) and \(n\) times \(xy+b^2=b(x+y)+X^2uw\), whose sides are both \(2b^2+bX(u+w)+X^2uw\) by the two
-- splits that share \(b\). Over \(xy\) the top half is \(nx+ny+PQuw=xy+n^2\), whose sides are both
-- \(2xy+Qxw+Puy+PQuw\) by the two splits that share \(n\). The rows (corner) and (qcorner) are calls of (square):
-- (corner) of its bottom half at \(n=X=P=Q=1\), where \(x+u=1\) and \(y+w=1\) are the top splits and \(x+y=1+b\)
-- gives the bottom splits \(x=b+w\), \(y=b+u\) and their tie, and (qcorner) at \(n=1\), \(P=XY\), \(Q=XW\), with the
-- splits of \cref{lem:qnumber}(attached) and the tie of \cref{lem:qnumber}(productstar).
proof thm:corner
  (chain) with[m / (e * (e + m * c)) + 1 / ((e + m * c) * (e + m * c + c)) = (m + 1) / (e * (e + m * c + c)) by lem:cone(countsplit)[b := m + 1, b1 := m, b2 := 1, c := e, d := e + m * c + c, w := e + m * c; (m + 1) * (e + m * c) = 1 * e + m * (e + m * c + c) by V]; V[1 * hw1]]
  (square) (V[(n / (x * y)) * h_1 | (n / (b * x)) * h_1, (n * X * w / (b * x * y)) * h_2, (1 / (x * y)) * h_3], V[(1 / x) * h_4 | (n / (x * y)) * h_4, (P * u / (x * y)) * h_5])
  (corner) with[x = b + 1 * w by V[1 * h_3 | 1 * h_2]; with[y = b + 1 * u by V[1 * h_3 | 1 * h_1]; with[x * y = 1 * b + 1 * u * w by V[y * h_1, 1 * h_3 | 1 * h_1, u * h_2]; with[1 = x + 1 * u by V[| 1 * h_1]; with[1 = y + 1 * w by V[| 1 * h_2]; with[1 / x + 1 / y + 1 * (b + 1 * 1) * u * w / (b * x * y) = 1 + 1 / b by thm:corner(square)[n := 1, x := x, y := y, b := b, u := u, w := w, X := 1, P := 1, Q := 1]; (V[1 * hw6], V[| 1 * hw2], V[| 1 * hw1])]]]]]]
  (qtie) V[1 * h_2, (g / A) * h_3 | (1 / A) * h_1, (c / A) * h_2]
  (qcorner) with[1 = x + X * Y * u by lem:qnumber(attached)[e := e, T := X * Y, U := W, u := x, w := u, v := 1; 1 * e + X * Y * W = 1 by V[1 * h_1]]; with[1 = y + X * W * w by lem:qnumber(attached)[e := e, T := X * W, U := Y, u := y, w := w, v := 1; 1 * e + X * W * Y = 1 by V[1 * h_1]]; with[y = b + X * u by lem:qnumber(attached)[e := e, T := X, U := W, u := b, w := u, v := y]; with[x = b + X * w by lem:qnumber(attached)[e := e, T := X, U := Y, u := b, w := w, v := x]; with[y * x = 1 * b + X * w * u by lem:qnumber(productstar)[e := e, T1 := X, T2 := W, T3 := Y, A := b, B := u, C := w, r := y, m1 := x, m := 1; 1 * e + X * W * Y = 1 by V[1 * h_1]]; with[x * y = 1 * b + X * u * w by V[1 * hw5]; with[1 / x + 1 / y + X * (b + 1 * X) * u * w / (b * x * y) = 1 + 1 / b by thm:corner(square)[n := 1, x := x, y := y, b := b, u := u, w := w, X := X, P := X * Y, Q := X * W]; with[1 / x + 1 / y + X * Y * (X * W) * u * w / (x * y) = 1 + 1 * 1 / (x * y) by thm:corner(square)[n := 1, x := x, y := y, b := b, u := u, w := w, X := X, P := X * Y, Q := X * W]; (V[1 * hw7], V[| 1 * hw3], V[| 1 * hw4], V[1 * hw8])]]]]]]]]
  (qcornersum) with[1 <= A + B by V[hp(A)]; with[(sum i in 1..A + B, t^i) = (sum i in 1..A, t^i) + t^A * (sum i in 1..B, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := A, m := B, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..A + C, t^i) = (sum i in 1..A, t^i) + t^A * (sum i in 1..C, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := A, m := C, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..A + B + C, t^i) = (sum i in 1..A + B, t^i) + t^(A + B) * (sum i in 1..C, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := A + B, m := C, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..B, t^i) + t^B * (sum i in 1..C, t^i) = (sum i in 1..C, t^i) + t^C * (sum i in 1..B, t^i) by lem:qsum(swap)[l := B, m := C, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..A, t^i) + t^A * (sum i in 1..B, t^i) = (sum i in 1..B, t^i) + t^B * (sum i in 1..A, t^i) by lem:qsum(swap)[l := A, m := B, t := t]; with[x = b + t^A * w by V[1 * hw2, 1 * h_4, t^A * h_6 | 1 * h_2]; with[y = b + t^A * u by V[1 * hw3, 1 * h_4, t^A * h_5 | 1 * h_3]; with[n = x + t^A * t^B * u by V[1 * hw4, 1 * h_2, (t^A * t^B) * h_5 | 1 * h_1]; with[w + t^B * u = u + t^C * w by V[1 * hw5, 1 * h_5, t^C * h_6 | 1 * h_6, t^B * h_5]; with[b + t^A * w = w + t^B * b by V[1 * hw6, 1 * h_6, t^B * h_4 | 1 * h_4, t^A * h_6]; with[x * y = n * b + t^A * u * w by V[y * hw7, b * hw8, (t^A * w) * hw8, (t^A * u) * hw11 | b * hw7, b * hw9]; with[n = y + t^A * t^C * w by V[1 * hw9, 1 * hw7, t^A * hw10 | 1 * hw8]; with[n / x + n / y + t^A * (b + n * t^A) * u * w / (b * x * y) = 1 + n / b by thm:corner(square)[n := n, x := x, y := y, b := b, u := u, w := w, X := t^A, P := t^A * t^B, Q := t^A * t^C]; with[n / x + n / y + t^A * t^B * (t^A * t^C) * u * w / (x * y) = 1 + n * n / (x * y) by thm:corner(square)[n := n, x := x, y := y, b := b, u := u, w := w, X := t^A, P := t^A * t^B, Q := t^A * t^C]; (V[1 * hw14], V[| 1 * hw8], V[| 1 * hw7], V[1 * hw15])]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
  (qcell) with[1 <= D + sigma by V[hp(D)]; with[(sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..D, t^i) + t^D * (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := D, m := sigma, t := t]; with[1 <= r + 1 by V; with[t^(D + sigma) * (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * (r + 1), t^i) = (sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) * (sum j in 1..r + 1, (t^(D + sigma))^j) by lem:qsum(scale)[a := D + sigma, b := r + 1, t := t]; with[1 <= e + 1 by V; with[t^(D + sigma) * (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * (e + 1), t^i) = (sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) * (sum j in 1..e + 1, (t^(D + sigma))^j) by lem:qsum(scale)[a := D + sigma, b := e + 1, t := t]; with[1 <= (D + sigma) * (r + 1) by V[(r + 1) * hp(D)]; with[1 <= (D + sigma) * e + D by V[1 * hp(D)]; with[(sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * (r + 1) + ((D + sigma) * e + D), t^i) = (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * (r + 1), t^i) + t^((D + sigma) * (r + 1)) * (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * e + D, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := (D + sigma) * (r + 1), m := (D + sigma) * e + D, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..(e + r + 1) * (D + sigma) + D, t^i) = (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * (r + 1) + ((D + sigma) * e + D), t^i) by sum; with[(sum i in 1..D + sigma + ((D + sigma) * e + D), t^i) = (sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) + t^(D + sigma) * (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * e + D, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := D + sigma, m := (D + sigma) * e + D, t := t]; with[1 <= (D + sigma) * (e + 1) by V[(e + 1) * hp(D)]; with[(sum i in 1..D + (D + sigma) * (e + 1), t^i) = (sum i in 1..D, t^i) + t^D * (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * (e + 1), t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := D, m := (D + sigma) * (e + 1), t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..D + sigma + ((D + sigma) * e + D), t^i) = (sum i in 1..D + (D + sigma) * (e + 1), t^i) by sum; V[((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma) * t^(r * (D + sigma))) * hw2, ((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma) * t^(r * (D + sigma))) * hw11 | ((sum i in 1..(e + r + 1) * (D + sigma) + D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma)) * hw2, (sum i in 1..D, t^i) * hw4, ((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^D * t^(r * (D + sigma))) * hw6, ((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma)) * hw9, ((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma)) * hw10, ((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma) * t^(r * (D + sigma))) * hw13, ((sum i in 1..D, t^i) * t^(D + sigma) * t^(r * (D + sigma))) * hw14]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
  (cornercount) with[A + B = A + 1 * B by V; with[A + C = A + 1 * C by V; with[(A + B) * (A + C) = (A + B + C) * A + 1 * C * B by V; with[A + B + C = A + B + 1 * C by V; with[A + B + C = A + C + 1 * B by V; (with[(A + B + C) / (A + B) + (A + B + C) / (A + C) + 1 * (A + (A + B + C) * 1) * C * B / (A * (A + B) * (A + C)) = 1 + (A + B + C) / A by thm:corner(square)[n := A + B + C, x := A + B, y := A + C, b := A, u := C, w := B, X := 1, P := 1, Q := 1]; V[1 * hw6]], with[(A + B + C) / (A + B) + (A + B + C) / (A + C) + 1 * 1 * C * B / ((A + B) * (A + C)) = 1 + (A + B + C) * (A + B + C) / ((A + B) * (A + C)) by thm:corner(square)[n := A + B + C, x := A + B, y := A + C, b := A, u := C, w := B, X := 1, P := 1, Q := 1]; V[1 * hw7]])]]]]]
  (odd) with[3 <= 2 * j + 1 by V[2 * hp(j)]; with[e^3 <= e^(2 * j + 1) by mono; with[(e + 1) * e < (e + 1) * b by V[1 * hw2, (e^2 + 2 * e + 4) * h_1 | 1 * h_2]; div[e + 1]]]]
  (even) with[2 <= 2 * j by V[2 * hp(j)]; with[e^2 <= e^(2 * j) by mono; with[(e + 1) * e <= (e + 1) * b by V[1 * hw2 | 1 * h_1]; div[e + 1]]]]
  (split) with[b / (l * K) + a / (K * m) = 1 / (l * m) by lem:cone(countsplit)[b := 1, b1 := b, b2 := a, c := l, d := m, w := K; 1 * K = a * l + b * m by V[1 * h_1]]; V[1 * hw1]]
  (cell) with[S + rho * k = kappa * E + rho * R by V[1 * h_1, rho * h_2]; with[kappa / (S * R) = kappa / ((1 + s) * (S * R)) + s * kappa / ((1 + s) * (S * R)) by lem:cone(apex)[mu := kappa, X := S * R, s := s, V := 1 + s; 1 + s = 1 + s by V]; with[k / (1 * R) + 1 / (R * (1 + s)) = 1 / (1 * (1 + s)) by lem:cone(countsplit)[b := 1, b1 := k, b2 := 1, c := 1, d := 1 + s, w := R; 1 * R = 1 * 1 + k * (1 + s) by V[1 * h_2]]; (V[(1 / (E * S * R)) * hw1, 1 * hw2, (kappa / S) * hw3], V[(1 / (E * S * R)) * hw1])]]]
  (edge) (V[((alpha_R * b * g + w) / (alpha_R * alpha_V * b)) * h_2, (1 / (alpha_R * alpha_V * b)) * h_3 | (1 / (alpha_R * b)) * h_3, (1 / (alpha_R * b * g)) * h_1], V[(1 / (alpha_R * alpha_V * b * g)) * h_4], V[ | (1 / (alpha_R * alpha_V)) * h_2])
  (wpos) with[g * g <= (m + 1) * (m + 1) by mono; with[c <= m * (m + 1) by V[1 * hw1 | 1 * h_2]; with[c * b <= m * (m + 1) * b by mono; with[m * ((m + 1) * b) < m * 1 by mono; V[1 * hw3, 1 * hw4]]]]]
  (qstep) (lem:qnumber(detached)[e := e, T := T, U := U, u := u, w := w, v := v], lem:qnumber(detached)[e := e, T := U, U := T, u := w, w := u, v := v; v * e + 1 = U * T by V[1 * h_3]], with[v = u + T * w by lem:qnumber(detached)[e := e, T := T, U := U, u := u, w := w, v := v]; V[(1 / (u * v)) * hw1]])
  (qincr) with[b1 = a1 + T * c by lem:qnumber(detached)[e := e, T := T, U := Tc, u := a1, w := c, v := b1]; with[b2 = c + Tc * a2 by lem:qnumber(detached)[e := e, T := Tc, U := T * TE, u := c, w := a2, v := b2; b2 * e + 1 = Tc * (T * TE) by V[1 * h_6]]; V[(T * b * (Tc * a2 + c) / (a1 * a2 * b1 * b2)) * hw1, (T * b / (a1 * a2 * b2)) * hw2]]]
  (qone) (V[(u * v * w) * h_1, (v * w) * h_1, (u * w) * h_1, (u * v) * h_1], V[u * h_1])
  (bz) with[c * (c + 2) * (2 * x) < c * (c + 2) * b by V[2 * hp(x), (c + 1) * hp(c) | 1 * h_1]; div[c * (c + 2)]]
  "core ratio" ind[x; with[a * b^1 = b * a^1 by eq; V[1 * hw1]]; chain[a * b^(x + 1) = b * (a * b^x) by eq; <= b * (b * a^x) by mono; <= a * (b * a^x) by mono; = b * a^(x + 1) by eq]]
  "core twice" ind[x; with[2^1 = 2 by eq; V[1 * hw1]]; with[2 * x <= 2^x by logic; V[2 * hw2, 2 * hp(x)]]]
  (sign) with[2 * e <= (k1 + 1) * (e + 1) by V[e * hp(k1)]; with[(k1 + 1) * (e + 1) * (2 * e)^m <= 2 * e * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^m by thm:corner(core ratio)[a := (k1 + 1) * (e + 1), b := 2 * e, x := m]; with[(k1 + 1) * (e + 1) * (2 * e)^e <= 2 * e * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^e by thm:corner(core ratio)[a := (k1 + 1) * (e + 1), b := 2 * e, x := e]; with[2 * m <= 2^m by thm:corner(core twice)[x := m]; with[2 * e <= 2^e by thm:corner(core twice)[x := e]; with[((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) * (2 * e)^m) * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) * (2 * e)^e) <= (2 * e * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^m) * (2 * e * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^e) by mono; with[(2 * m) * (2 * e) <= 2^m * 2^e by mono; with[(2 * m) * (2 * e) * (e^m * e^e) <= 2^m * 2^e * (e^m * e^e) by mono; with[(2 * e)^m = 2^m * e^m by eq; with[(2 * e)^e = 2^e * e^e by eq; with[e^(m + e + 1) = e^m * e^e * e by eq; with[((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) = ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^m * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^e * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1)) by eq; with[m * e^(m + e + 1) * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^3 <= e^2 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) by V[(1/4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))) * hw6, (1/4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^3) * hw8, (m * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^3) * hw11 | e^2 * hw12, (1/4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^3 * (2 * e)^e) * hw9, (1/4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^3 * 2^m * e^m) * hw10]; with[4 <= m + e + 1 by V[1 * h_1]; with[1 <= (k1 + 1) * (e + 1) by V; with[((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^4 <= ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) by mono; div[4 * m * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^4; V[(4 * m * (k1 * (e + 1) + 1) * (m * (e + 1) * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1)) * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 2) + ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 1)^2 * e * (e + 2))) * hw16, (4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 1) * (((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 1) * (k1 * (e + 1) + 1) + m * (e + 1)) * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))) * hw13, (4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) * m * ((k1 * (e + 1) + 1) * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 1)^2 * e * (e + 2) + ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 1) * (e + 1) * e^2 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1)))) * hp(m), (4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) * (m + 1) * (((k1 + 1) * (e + 1) + 1)^2 * (k1 * (e + 1) + 1) * e^2 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1)))) * hp(m), (4 * ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) * m^2 * ((e + 1) * (((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^3 * (2 * e + 1) + ((k1 + 1) * (e + 1))^2 * (e^2 + 3 * e + 1)))) * hp(k1)]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
  (rstar) (V[(1 / (g * m)) * h_1, (1 / m) * h_2], V[(D / (g * m * V)) * h_4, (D * nu / (g * m * V)) * h_3, (D * kappa / (g * m * V)) * h_1, (D * kappa / (m * V)) * h_2])
  (rneg) (V[(1 / (g * m)) * h_1, (1 / m) * h_2], V[(D / (g * m * W)) * h_4, (D * nu / (g * m * W)) * h_3, (D * kappa / (g * m * W)) * h_1, (D * kappa / (m * W)) * h_2])
  (phi) V[(kappa * delta + rho) * h_1]
  (chiz) with[e <= X by V[1 * h_1]; with[X * e^k <= e * X^k by thm:corner(core ratio)[a := X, b := e, x := k]; with[X^1 <= X^k by mono; with[e^(k + 2) = e^k * e^2 by eq; with[X^(k + 1) = X^k * X by eq; div[X; V[((X + 1) * e^2) * hw2, y * hw3, (X * (X + 1)) * hw4, (X * (e + 1)) * hw5, (X^k * y * (e + 1) * (X + 1)) * hp(e), (X^k * (X * e^2 + e^2)) * h_1 | (X^k * X) * h_1]]]]]]]
  "core ratio4" ind[x; with[a^4 * b^4 = b^4 * a^4 by eq; V[1 * hw1]]; chain[a^4 * b^(x + 1) = b * (a^4 * b^x) by eq; <= b * (b^4 * a^x) by mono; <= a * (b^4 * a^x) by mono; = b^4 * a^(x + 1) by eq]]
  "core sq" ind[x; with[2^(1 + 1) = 4 by eq; V[ | 1 * hw1]]; with[x^2 + x + 2 <= 2^(x + 1) by logic; with[2^(x + 1 + 1) = 2 * 2^(x + 1) by eq; V[2 * hw2, x * hp(x) | 1 * hw3]]]]
  (magn) with[4 <= m + e + 1 by V[1 * h_1]; with[e <= e + 1 by V; with[(e + 1)^4 * e^(m + e + 1) <= e^4 * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1) by thm:corner(core ratio4)[a := e + 1, b := e, x := m + e + 1]; with[1 <= e + 1 by V; with[(e + 1)^4 <= (e + 1)^(m + e + 1) by mono; with[1 <= m + e by V[1 * h_1]; with[(m + e)^2 + (m + e) + 2 <= 2^(m + e + 1) by thm:corner(core sq)[x := m + e]; with[2 * (2 * e + 3)^2 * (e^(m + e + 1) + (m + e + 1) * (e + 1)) < m * 2^(m + e + 1) * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1) * (e + 1) by div[(e + 1)^4; V[(m * (e + 1)^5 * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1)) * hw7, (2 * (2 * e + 3)^2) * hw3, (2 * (2 * e + 3)^2 * (m + e + 1) * (e + 1)) * hw5, ((32/27 * e * m^3 + 10/9 * m^3) * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1)) * hp(e), ((16 * e^5 * m + 10 * e^6 + 442/81 * e^4 * m + 28 * e^5 + 20 * e^4) * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1)) * hp(m), ((2 * e^5 + 40/27 * e^2 * m^2 + 442/81 * e^3 * m + 10 * e^4 + 137/27 * e * m^2 + 523/27 * e^2 * m + 24 * e^3 + 19/9 * m^2 + 181/9 * e * m + 32 * e^2 + 22/3 * m + 22 * e + 6) * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1)) * h_1, ((e^4 * m^2 + e^5 * m) * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1)) * h_1 * hp(e)]]; with[X^4 * (2 * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) <= (2 * (e + 1))^4 * X^(m + e + 1) by thm:corner(core ratio4)[a := X, b := 2 * (e + 1), x := m + e + 1]; with[(2 * (e + 1))^(m + e + 1) = 2^(m + e + 1) * (e + 1)^(m + e + 1) by eq; with[(X + 1) * (2 * (e + 1)) <= (2 * e + 3) * X by V[1 * h_2]; with[((X + 1) * (2 * (e + 1)))^2 <= ((2 * e + 3) * X)^2 by mono; div[4 * (e + 1)^2; V[(m * (e + 1) / 2) * hw9, (X^4 / 2) * hw8, (X^2 * (e^(m + e + 1) + (m + e + 1) * (e + 1))) * hw12 | (m * (e + 1) * X^4 / 2) * hw10]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
  (magnone) cases[2 <= k; with[4 <= k + 2 by V[1 * hc1]; with[1 <= X by V; with[X^4 <= X^(k + 2) by mono; V[2 * hw3, (2 * X^3 + 3 * X^2 + 3 * X + 3) * h_1]]]]; with[4 <= X by V[1 * h_2, 2 * hn1]; with[3 <= k + 2 by V[1 * hp(k)]; with[1 <= X by V; with[X^3 <= X^(k + 2) by mono; V[2 * hw7, (X^2 + X + 1) * hw4]]]]]]
  (chiodd) ind[n; V[(e^3 + 2 * e^2 + 4 * e + 4) * hp(e)]; with[n * (e + 1) < e^n + 1 by logic; with[e^(n + 1) = e^n * e by eq; V[e * hw1, (e * n + n) * hp(e), e * hp(n) | 1 * hw2]]]]
  (law) with[alpha_R * alpha_V = g * b + v * u by V[1 * h_2, alpha_R * h_3 | g * h_3, u * h_4]; (V[1 * hw1], V[(alpha_V * (1 + alpha_R)) * h_1 | alpha_R * hw1])]
  (three) V[(E * (kappa + c + 1)) * h_1, (2 * c) * h_2]
  (four) with[kappa * (j^2 + 4 * j + 6) < c * j * D by V[(c * kappa^2 * j + 5 * c * kappa^2 + c * kappa * rho) * hp(j), (5 * c * kappa) * hp(kappa), (kappa * j^2 * (c + 1) + 4 * kappa * j * (c + 1) + 6 * kappa) * hp(c), (c * kappa) * hp(rho) | (c * j) * h_3, (c * j * (kappa * (j + 4) + rho)) * h_1]; V[(g * D) * hw1, (D * (j^2 + 4 * j + 6)) * h_2]]
  "core bone" ind[p; V[0]; with[(e + 2)^2 * e^(p + 4) < (e + 1)^(p + 5) * e by logic; with[(e + 1)^(p + 1 + 5) = (e + 1)^(p + 5) * (e + 1) by eq; with[1 <= e + 1 by V; with[1 <= p + 5 by V; with[(e + 1)^1 <= (e + 1)^(p + 5) by mono; V[e * hw1, e * hw5 | e * hw2]]]]]]]
  "core bten" ind[p; V[(e^4 + 10 * e^3 + 60 * e^2 + 260 * e + 1105) * h_1]; with[10 * (p + 4) * (e + 2)^2 <= (e + 1)^(p + 4) by logic; with[(e + 1)^(p + 1 + 4) = (e + 1)^(p + 4) * (e + 1) by eq; V[(e + 1) * hw1, (40 * (e + 2)^2) * h_1 | 1 * hw2]]]]
  "core btwo" with[p^2 + p + 2 <= 2^(p + 1) by thm:corner(core sq)[x := p]; V[(10 * j) * hw1, 20 * h_1, 10 * hp(j), 10 * hp(p) * hp(p), (10 * p) * hp(j) * hp(p), 20 * hp(j) * hp(p)]]
  "core mzero" with[2 * (e + 1) <= 2 * e + 3 by V; with[(2 * (e + 1))^(p + 2) <= (2 * e + 3)^(p + 2) by mono; with[(2 * (e + 1))^(p + 2) = 2^(p + 2) * (e + 1)^(p + 2) by eq; with[(e + 2)^2 * e^(p + 4) < (e + 1)^(p + 5) * e by thm:corner(core bone)[p := p, e := e]; with[5 <= e by V[1 * hp(j), 1 * hp(p) | 1 * h_2]; with[10 * (p + 4) * (e + 2)^2 <= (e + 1)^(p + 4) by thm:corner(core bten)[e := e, p := p]; with[10 * (p + j + 3) + 1 <= 10 * 2^(p + 1) * j by thm:corner(core btwo)[p := p, j := j]; with[(e + 1)^(p + 5) = (e + 1)^(p + 2) * (e + 1)^3 by eq; with[(e + 1)^(p + 5) = (e + 1)^(p + 4) * (e + 1) by eq; with[2^(p + 2) = 2 * 2^(p + 1) by eq; V[(2 * (e + 1)^3 * j) * hw2, (4 * j * 2^(p + 1)) * hw8, (2/5 * (e + 1)^(p + 5)) * hw7, (4 * (e + 1)^(p + 5)) * h_2, 4 * hw4, (2/5 * (e + 1)) * hw6 | (2 * (e + 1)^3 * j) * hw3, (2 * (e + 1)^3 * j * (e + 1)^(p + 2)) * hw10, 2/5 * hw9]]]]]]]]]]]
  (mup) with[1 <= p + 1 by V; with[X * (2 * e + 3)^(p + 1) <= (2 * e + 3) * X^(p + 1) by thm:corner(core ratio)[a := X, b := 2 * e + 3, x := p + 1]; with[(X + 1) * (2 * e + 3) <= (2 * e + 4) * X by V[1 * h_3]; with[((X + 1) * (2 * e + 3))^2 <= ((2 * e + 4) * X)^2 by mono; with[(2 * e + 4)^2 * (e^(p + 4) + (p + 4) * (e + 1)) < 2 * (e + 1)^3 * j * (2 * e + 3)^(p + 2) by thm:corner(core mzero)[p := p, j := j, e := e]; with[(2 * e + 3)^(p + 2) = (2 * e + 3) * (2 * e + 3)^(p + 1) by eq; with[X^(p + 4) = X^3 * X^(p + 1) by eq; div[(2 * e + 3)^2; V[(X^2 * (e^(p + 4) + (p + 4) * (e + 1))) * hw4, (X^4) * hw5, (2 * (e + 1)^3 * j * X^4) * hw6, (2 * (e + 1)^3 * j * (2 * e + 3) * X^3) * hw2 | (2 * (e + 1)^3 * j * (2 * e + 3)^2) * hw7]]]]]]]]]
  (mupsix) V[(432 * X^4 + 2461 * X^3 + 25683 * X^2) * h_1]
  (quarter) with[4 * k + 3 * M <= n * M by V[4 * h_3, ((n + 1) * kappa * (kappa + rho)) * h_1, (2 * (n + 1) * kappa) * hp(kappa), (2 * n) * hp(kappa) * hp(rho), 2 * hp(n) * hp(rho), (2 * rho + 2) * hp(kappa) | (n + 1) * h_2]; (V[1 * hw1], V[1 * hw1], V[1 * hw1])]

-- Let \(\pi\) be an embedded resolution of \(f=0\) near the origin, with the components \(E_i\), their multiplicities
-- \(N_i\) and the sets \(E_i^\circ\) of \cref{fact:zetatop}, and let \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=\prod_q\det(1-tT_q)^{(-1)^q}\) be
-- the zeta function of the monodromy \(T_q\) of \(f\) at the origin on the cohomology of degree \(q\) of its Milnor
-- fibre. Then \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=\prod_i(1-t^{N_i})^{\chi(E_i^\circ)}\) \cite{ACampo1975}, as stated in
-- \cite[(2.1.2)]{Denef1991}. So the exponent of the cyclotomic polynomial \(\Phi_e\) in \(\zeta_{f,0}\) is
-- \(z_f(e)=\sum_{e\mid N_i}\chi(E_i^\circ)\), the sum of the shares \(\chi(E_i^\circ)[e\mid N_i]\) of the components, and when
-- it is not \(0\) a primitive root of unity of order \(e\) is a root of the characteristic polynomial of the monodromy
-- at the origin on the cohomology of some degree. At an isolated critical point the reduced cohomology of the Milnor
-- fibre lies in the degree \(n-1\) \cite{Milnor1968}, so that there \(z_f(e)=(-1)^{n-1}m_f(e)\) for \(e\ge2\), with
-- \(m_f(e)\) the multiplicity of a primitive root of unity of order \(e\) on that degree.
cit fact:acampo [the zeta function of the monodromy on a resolution]
  doi: 10.1007/BF02565748
  origin: ?{\text{cited: \cite{ACampo1975}, as stated in \cite[(2.1.2)]{Denef1991}, and \cite{Milnor1968} at an isolated critical point}}

-- Let \(s_0=-\nu/N\) be the candidate pole of a component, \(-\nu'/e\) in lowest terms, \(\varpi=\gcd(N,\nu)\), and \(u=t^\varpi\) its
-- primitive letter, so that the letter \(t\) with \(t^N=p\) of \cref{thm:corner}(iii) gives \(p=u^e\), and \(T=u^{\nu'}\)
-- at \(s_0\). A neighbour of cost \(\alpha\), with \(a=e\alpha\) whole, carries the mark
-- \(L(\alpha)=(p-1)/(t^{\alpha N}-1)=(u^e-1)/(u^a-1)\); for \(a\ge1\) it is the factor \(F\) of \(FS_a(u)=S_e(u)\), as in
-- \cref{lem:layer}(factor), and for \(a\le-1\) it is \(-u^{-a}\) times the factor of \(-\alpha\). Let \(\Phi_e\) be the
-- cyclotomic polynomial of order \(e\). If \(\alpha\) is not whole, with \(g=\gcd(a,e)\), \(a=ga'\), \(e=gn\) and
-- \(ba'=cn+1\), the factor is \(0\) in the layer of \(S_n(u^g)\) (off), whose non-zero roots are the \(u\) with \(u^e=1\)
-- and \(u^g\ne1\), the roots of \(\Phi_e\) among them; if \(\alpha=j\) is whole, the factor is \(1/j\) modulo the
-- binomial \(1-u^e\) (whole), which \(\Phi_e\) divides. So, modulo \(\Phi_e(u)\), a mark is \(0\) unless its cost is
-- whole, and then it is \(1/\alpha\); at \(u=1\) it is \(1/\alpha\). In the letter \(t\) the same layer is
-- \(\Phi_N(t)\), as \(u=t^\varpi\) has the order \(e\) when \(t\) has the order \(N\); \(\Phi_e(t)\) is that layer only when
-- \(\gcd(e,\nu)=1\). The cost \(\alpha_i=\nu_i-\nu N_i/N\) of a component \(E_i\) at \(s_0\) is whole exactly when \(e\mid N_i\),
-- as \(\nu'\) is prime to \(e\) (\cref{lem:frame}). So, with the Euler characteristics for the counts of points, the value
-- \(\mathcal V=\sum_{I\ni c}\chi(E_I^\circ)\prod_{i\in I\setminus\{c\}}L(\alpha_i)\) at a component \(c\) of the candidate, over the
-- strata through \(c\), is at \(u=1\) the sum of the \(\chi(E_I^\circ)\prod_{i\ne c}1/\alpha_i\), \(N\) times the residue
-- at \(s_0\) of their terms in \cref{fact:zetatop}, and modulo \(\Phi_e(u)\) it is the open part \(\chi(E_c^\circ)\) plus
-- the edges \(\chi(E_c\cap E_i^\circ)/\alpha_i\) to the neighbours of whole cost, \(N\) times the residue of their terms
-- in \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\), the sum over the strata all of whose components have multiplicities divisible by \(e\)
-- \cite[(4.3.1)]{Denef1991}: component by
-- component, the open part enters with the weight \(1\), which is its share \(\chi(E_c^\circ)[e\mid N_c]\) in the layer
-- \(\Phi_e\) of the product of \cref{fact:acampo}, and the value of the component is that share when no neighbour has a whole
-- cost. The rows: the factor of a cost that is not whole, \(0\) in the layer of \(S_n(u^g)\) (off), and that of a whole
-- cost, \(1/j\) modulo \(1-u^e\) (whole).
lemma lem:mark [the mark of a cost on the layer of its order]
  (off): g, a, n, b, c in NN>=1, u, F in QQ>0, b * a = c * n + 1, F * (sum i in 1..g * a, u^i) = (sum i in 1..g * n, u^i) : (u^g)^(a + n + 1) * F + (u^g)^(a + 1) * (sum j in 1..n, (u^g)^j) * (sum j in 1..c, ((u^g)^n)^j) * F = (u^g)^n * (sum j in 1..n, (u^g)^j) * (sum j in 1..b, ((u^g)^a)^j)
  fix (off) corner.markoff
  neqfix (off) corner.markoff.nounit
  (whole): k, e in NN>=1, u, F in QQ>0, F * (sum i in 1..e * k, u^i) = (sum i in 1..e, u^i) : k * u^e * F + u^e * F * (sum j in 1..k, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i)) = u^e * u^e + F * (sum j in 1..k, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i))
  fix (whole) corner.markwhole
  neqfix (whole) corner.markwhole.nofactor
  origin: ?{\text{elementary, proved here: \cref{lem:layer}(factor), (coprime) and (whole) read at the primitive letter of a candidate; the layer of the order of a component is its share in the product of \cref{fact:acampo}, and}\ Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\ \text{is that of \cite[(4.3.1)]{Denef1991}}}

-- (off) is \cref{lem:layer}(factor) at the letter \(u\), which gives \(FS_{a'}(u^g)=S_n(u^g)\), and then
-- \cref{lem:layer}(coprime) at the letter \(u^g\); (whole) is (factor) at \(g=e\), \(a=j\) and \(n=1\), which gives
-- \(FS_j(u^e)=u^e\), and then \cref{lem:layer}(whole) at the letter \(u^e\). At a root \(u\) of \(1-u^e\) (whole) reads
-- \(jF=1\).
proof lem:mark
  (off) with[F * (sum j in 1..a, (u^g)^j) = (sum j in 1..n, (u^g)^j) by lem:layer(factor)[g := g, a := a, n := n, t := u, F := F]; lem:layer(coprime)[a := a, n := n, u := b, v := c, x := u^g, F := F]]
  (whole) with[(sum i in 1..e * 1, u^i) = (sum i in 1..e, u^i) by sum; with[F * (sum j in 1..k, (u^e)^j) = (sum j in 1..1, (u^e)^j) by lem:layer(factor)[g := e, a := k, n := 1, t := u, F := F; F * (sum i in 1..e * k, u^i) = (sum i in 1..e * 1, u^i) by V[1 * h_1, 1 * hw1]]; with[(sum j in 1..1, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i)) = (sum i in 1..1, (u^e)^i) by sum; with[(sum i in 1..1, (u^e)^i) = (u^e)^1 by sum; with[k * u^e * F + (sum j in 1..1, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i)) + u^e * F * (sum j in 1..k, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i)) = 1 * u^e + u^e * (sum j in 1..1, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i)) + F * (sum j in 1..k, (sum i in 1..j, (u^e)^i)) by lem:layer(whole)[a := k, n := 1, x := u^e, F := F; F * (sum j in 1..k, (u^e)^j) = (sum j in 1..1, (u^e)^j) by V[1 * hw2]]; V[1 * hw5, 1 * hw3, 1 * hw4, u^e * hw3, u^e * hw4]]]]]]

-- For \(n\ge3\) and every \(\delta\ge1\), and for \(n=2\) and \(\delta\ge2\), the local monodromy conjecture holds for
-- \(f\) at the origin: for \(Z_{top,0}\), for \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\) for every count \(e\ge2\), and, at all but finitely
-- many primes, for \(Z_0(s,\omega)\) for every character \(\omega\) of \(\mathbb Z_p^\times\); for \(n=2\) and
-- \(\delta=1\) it holds for \(Z_{top,0}\), for \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\) and, at every good prime, for \(Z_0\). For
-- \(\delta\ge n\), \(n/N\) is the log canonical threshold of \(f\) at the origin and \(-n/N\) is the largest root of
-- the local Bernstein--Sato polynomial \(b_{f,0}\); and for \(\delta=n\) the strong form holds. At \(s_S\), for
-- \(e_S\ge2\), the root of order \(e_S\) is read on the layer \(\Phi_{e_S}\) of the value of \cref{thm:corner}(iii):
-- with the Euler characteristics for the counts of points, and read modulo \(\Phi_{e_S}\) in the primitive letter of
-- \(s_S\) as in \cref{lem:mark}, that value is \(N_S\) times the residue at
-- \(s_S\) of \(Z^{(e_S)}_{top,0}\) of (vi), \(\mathcal V=([\kappa=1]-\gamma)\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)+[e_S\mid k_0]\chi(\Sigma)/\alpha_L\),
-- and the exponent of \(\Phi_{e_S}\) in the product of \cref{fact:acampo} is
-- \(z(e_S)=\mathcal V+[e_S\mid N](n-\chi(\Sigma))-[e_S\mid k_0]\chi(\Sigma)/\alpha_L\); so when \(e_S\nmid N\) the two
-- are equal, and \(m(e_S)=(\gamma-[\kappa=1])B\). The reading on the layer is a tie over \(\mathbb Q\); that \(m(e_S)\) and
-- \(m(e_E)\) are not zero is a count, which rests on the primitive Betti numbers of \(\Sigma\) and \(Z\), \(B\ge1\) in
-- (v), read with \cite[\S22.1]{Hirzebruch1966}. The row: the whole left mark \(1/\alpha_L\) at \(s_S\) is the factor
-- \(\rho/\sigma_E(s_S)\) of (vi), the tie \(\sigma_E(s_S)=\rho\alpha_L\) written as one of two positive sums,
-- \(n+\rho \gamma\delta/D=NS+\rho \gamma n/D\) with \(D=N+d\) and \(S=-s_S\) (layer).
corollary cor:cornermonodromy [the monodromy conjecture at the formed corner]
  (layer): g, kappa, rho, delta, n in NN>=1, D, S in QQ>0, D = g * (kappa * delta + rho), S * (kappa * D) = n * kappa + rho : n + rho * g * delta / D = g * kappa * delta * S + rho * g * n / D
  fix (layer) corner.layer
  neqfix (layer) corner.layer.notie
  origin: ?{\text{the result, read by \cref{thm:corner}(i), (iii), (v), (vi) and (vii), \cref{lem:mark} and \cref{fact:acampo}; the classical inputs are cited inside it: the local variant of \cite[(2.3.2)]{Denef1991} and \cite[(4.3.2)]{Denef1991}; \cite[Proposition~8.5 and Theorem~10.6]{Kollar1997}; for}\ n=2\ \text{\cite{Loeser1988} as stated in \cite[p.~606]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}}}

-- By \cref{thm:corner}(i) and (vi) a pole of \(Z_{top,0}\) is \(-n/N\), \(s_S\) or \(-1\), a pole of \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\)
-- is \(-n/N\) or \(s_S\), and a pole of \(Z_0(s,\omega)\) is a zero of \(1-X_E\) or \(1-X_S\), or, for the trivial
-- character, of \(1-p^{-1}T\), which the conjecture reads by the classes \(-n/N\), \(s_S\) and \(-1\); the primes left
-- out are those that are not good and, for a character not trivial on \(1+p\Z_p\), those that divide \(N(N+d)\). By
-- (v), whose multiplicities are those of \cref{fact:acampo}, the roots of unity of the orders
-- \(e_E\) and \(e_S\) of the candidates \(-n/N\) and \(s_S\) (\cref{lem:frame}) are roots of the characteristic polynomial of the monodromy at the origin when these orders are
-- at least \(2\), and \(1\) is its root on the cohomology of degree \(0\) of the Milnor fibre. For \(n=2\) and \(\delta=1\), (v) gives this
-- for \(e_S\) only; there, by (vii), for \(n\ne N\) the number \(-n/N\) is a pole of neither \(Z_{top,0}\) nor
-- \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\) and no zero of \(1-X_E\) is a pole of \(Z_0\), while for \(n=N\) it is \(-1\). For \(n=2\) the
-- conjecture for \(Z_{top,0}\) is the theorem on plane curves of \cite{Loeser1988}, as stated in
-- \cite[p.~606]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, which the count here does not use. As the origin is an isolated
-- critical point, the monodromy at the other points of \(f=0\) near the origin has the root \(1\) only, so these are the local
-- variant of \cite[(2.3.2)]{Denef1991} for every character and the form of \cite[(4.3.2)]{Denef1991} for
-- \(Z^{(e)}_{top,0}\). For \(\delta\ge n\) the number \(n/N\) is the least ratio \(\nu/N\) on the components of the
-- resolution and the strict transform, so it is the log canonical threshold of \(f\) at the origin
-- \cite[Proposition~8.5]{Kollar1997}, and \(-n/N\) is the largest root of the local Bernstein--Sato polynomial
-- \(b_{f,0}\) \cite[Theorem~10.6]{Kollar1997}. For \(\delta=n\) it is the only pole other than \(-1\), so there the
-- strong form also holds, for every \(k_0\ge2\) and \(d\ge1\).
--
-- On the layer: at \(s_S\), with \(e_S\ge2\), the cost \(\alpha_V=1+s_S\) is not whole; \(\alpha_R=1/\kappa\) is whole
-- exactly when \(\kappa=1\), and its mark is then \(1\); and \(\alpha_L=(n-\delta)/(\kappa\delta+\rho)\) is whole
-- exactly when \(N+d\) divides \(\gamma\nu_S\), as \(\nu_S=\kappa(n-\delta)+\kappa\delta+\rho\) and \(\kappa\) is prime to
-- \(\kappa\delta+\rho\), that is, exactly when \(e_S\) divides \(k_0\). So by \cref{lem:mark}, with \(p=1\) on the layer,
-- \(\Lambda_r\) reads \(-r+[\kappa=1]+[e_S\mid k_0]/\alpha_L\) and \(\Lambda_Z\) reads \([e_S\mid k_0]/\alpha_L\), and with
-- \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\), \(\gamma\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\) and \(\chi(Z)\) for \(\#(\Sigma\setminus Z)\),
-- \(\#\tilde V\) and \(\#Z\) the value is \(V\). By (layer) its last term is \([e_S\mid k_0]\rho\chi(\Sigma)/\sigma_E(s_S)\),
-- and \(e_S\) divides \(N+d\) exactly when \(\kappa=1\), as \(\gcd(\kappa,\nu_S)=1\), so that \([e_S\mid
-- N+d]\kappa=[\kappa=1]\); so \(\mathcal V\) is \(N_S\) times the residue of (vi) at \(s_S\). The components with \(e_S\mid N_i\), those of integral cost at \(s_S\) (\cref{lem:frame}), are,
-- by the proof of (v), the corner, of \(\chi(E_i^\circ)=-(\gamma-[\kappa=1])\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\), and \(E\) when
-- \(e_S\mid N\); so the open part of the corner is its share, \(z(e_S)-\mathcal V=[e_S\mid N](n-\chi(\Sigma))-[e_S\mid
-- k_0]\chi(\Sigma)/\alpha_L\) is the open part of \(E\) less the edge to the left neighbour, and when
-- \(e_S\nmid N\) (v) gives \(m(e_S)=(\gamma-[\kappa=1])B\).
proof cor:cornermonodromy
  (layer) V[(n / D) * h_1, (g * delta / D) * h_2]

-- For \(\delta>n\) and \(d=1\), \(s_S\) is a root of \(b_{f,0}\) by the theorems of \cite{BudurShiZuo2026}, a preprint
-- of 2026 not yet refereed, so that, with the root \(-n/N\) of \cref{cor:cornermonodromy}, the strong form of the
-- monodromy conjecture holds there too; \cref{cor:cornermonodromy} does not rest on it. The number \(\nu_S/N_S\) is
-- remarkable in the sense of \cite[Definition~1.1]{BudurDeLorenzoPozaShiZuo2026}, witnessed by the corner and the
-- strict transform: every stratum whose least ratio is below \(\nu_S/N_S\) lies on the components of multiplicity
-- divisible by \(k_0\). Off the origin \(f=0\) is smooth, so the log pair \((\mathbb A^n,(\nu_S/N_S)\{f=0\})\) is log
-- canonical there, and so the divisor that \cite[Theorem~1.2]{BudurShiZuo2026} retains is centred at the origin, and
-- \(s_S\) is a root of \(b_{f,0}\) \cite[Theorems~1.1 and~1.3]{BudurShiZuo2026}.
remark rem:strongcorner [the strong form at the formed corner for \(\delta>n\) and \(d=1\)]
  origin: ?{\text{read on the corner of \cref{thm:corner} with \cite[Definition~1.1, Theorem~1.2 and Lemma~1.6]{BudurDeLorenzoPozaShiZuo2026} and \cite[Theorems~1.1 to~1.3]{BudurShiZuo2026}, a preprint not yet refereed; the root}\ -n/N\ \text{is that of \cref{cor:cornermonodromy}}}

-- For counts \(2\le a<b\), the germ \(x^a+y^b\) is the formed corner of \cref{thm:corner} at \(n=2\), with \(h=x\),
-- \(\delta=1\), \(k_0=a\), \(g=y^b\) and \(d=b-a\): \(\gamma=\gcd(a,b)\), \(N_S=\mathrm{lcm}(a,b)=l\),
-- \(s_S=-\frac1a-\frac1b\), and the costs of the corner are \(\alpha_L=\gamma/b\), \(\alpha_R=\gamma/a\) and
-- \(\alpha_V=1-\frac1a-\frac1b\) at each of its \(\gamma\) points. By \cref{thm:corner}(i) and (ii) the cusp \(x^2+y^3\)
-- has \(Z_{top,0}(s)=\frac{4s+5}{(s+1)(6s+5)}\), with the residue \(\frac{10}6=\frac53\) at \(-\frac56\), and its costs
-- tie as in \cref{lem:node}, \(\frac13+\frac12+\frac16=1\). By \cref{thm:corner}(v) the roots of an order \(e\ge2\) of
-- the characteristic polynomial of the monodromy on the cohomology of degree \(1\) have the multiplicity
-- \(\gamma[e\mid l]-[e\mid a]-[e\mid b]\), which is that of \((t^l-1)^{\gamma}(t-1)/((t^a-1)(t^b-1))\); for \(a\) and
-- \(b\) coprime it is \(1\) exactly at the divisors \(e\) of \(ab\) that divide neither \(a\) nor \(b\), and at the order
-- \(e_S=ab\) the marks of the three costs are read by \cref{lem:mark}, as in \cref{cor:cornermonodromy}. These zeta
-- functions of \(x^a+y^b\) are also the formula on the Newton polyhedron of \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992}, and the
-- conjecture for plane curves is \cite{Loeser1988}.
example rem:torus [the corner of \(x^a+y^b\) and its monodromy]
  origin: ?{\text{a classical case, read by the mechanism of \cref{thm:corner}: (i), (ii) and (v) at}\ n=2,\ \delta=1,\ k_0=a,\ d=b-a\text{, with \cref{lem:mark}; the zeta functions of}\ x^a+y^b\ \text{are the formula on the Newton polyhedron of \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992}, and the conjecture for plane curves is \cite{Loeser1988}}}

subsection sec:level One level of the tower

-- One blowing-up of the origin reads the zeta functions of a polynomial on those of its tangent cone \(H\), one
-- variable down, when the next form \(g\), of degree \(N+d\), cuts the hypersurface of \(H\) well. At a point of
-- \(\{H=0\}\) off \(g=0\) the germ \(t^N(H+t^dW)\) of the blowing-up gives, for each order of \(H\), three components:
-- the component \(E\) of the origin, of cost \(\sigma_E=n+Ns\), the level below, read at the argument
-- \(c'=(n+(N+d)s)/d\) and of cost \(-dc'\), and the strict transform, of cost \(1+s\), taken \(d\) times. The three
-- costs tie, \(\sigma_E-dc'+d(1+s)=d\), and their star, the sum of their inverses less \(1/d\), is minus a quotient of
-- positive masses for \(s>0\) and \(\sigma_E>d\) (stard); at \(d=1\) so is its q-form in the letter \(T\) (qstar), and
-- the shells of an order of \(H\) that \(d\) does not divide give quotients of positive masses in the letters \(T^r\)
-- (qshelld). At the points of \(g=0\) the components are those of \cref{thm:corner}.

-- Let \(n\ge2\), \(N\ge1\) and \(d\ge1\) be counts, \(D=N+d\), and \(f=H+g+f'\) a polynomial in \(n\) variables with
-- rational coefficients, where \(H=\prod_ih_i^{a_i}\) is a form of degree \(N\), with distinct irreducible forms
-- \(h_i\) and counts \(a_i\), \(g\) is a form of degree \(D\), and every term of \(f'\) has degree at least \(D+1\).
-- Put \(V=\{H=0\}\) in the projective space of dimension \(n-1\), \(Z=V\cap\{g=0\}\), \(Z_i\) the points of \(Z\) on
-- \(h_i=0\), \(g_i=\gcd(a_i,d)\), \(\sigma_E=n+Ns\) and \(c'=(n+Ds)/d\), so that \(dc'=\sigma_E+ds\), and write
-- \(c_a\) for \(c'\) at \(d=1\). For a stratum \(\mathcal S\) of \(V\) on which the germ \(H_{\mathcal S}\) of \(H\)
-- has one type let \(Z_{top,\mathcal S}(H;s')\) be the local topological zeta function of that germ, in \(n-1\)
-- variables, and, for a count \(e\), let its layer \(Z^{(e)}_{top,\mathcal S}(H;s')\) be the sum of its terms of
-- \cref{fact:zetatop} over the strata of an embedded resolution of the germ all of whose components have
-- multiplicities divisible by \(e\), so that \(Z^{(1)}_{top,\mathcal S}=Z_{top,\mathcal S}\). With the totient \(J_2\)
-- of Jordan, \(J_2(e)=e^2\prod_\ell(1-\ell^{-2})\) over the primes \(\ell\) dividing \(e\), so that \(\sum_{e\mid
-- m}J_2(e)=m^2\), put, for a germ \(\psi\) of \(H\) at a point,
-- \(\Theta_d(\psi)=\frac1{dc'}+\big(\frac1{\sigma_E}-\frac1{dc'}\big)Z_{top}(\psi;c')-\frac s{(1+s)d}\sum_{e\mid
-- d}J_2(e)Z^{(e)}_{top}(\psi;c')\). The hypothesis \((\mathrm T)\), of transversality, is that no point of \(g=0\)
-- lies on two of the hypersurfaces \(h_i=0\) or is a singular point of one, and that \(g\) cuts each of them
-- transversally. At a prime \(p\) at which the coefficients of \(f\) are \(p\)-integral write \(\#\) for the number of
-- points over \(\mathbb F_p\) of a reduction, put \(T=p^{-s}\), \(X_E=p^{-n}T^N\), \(Y=p^{-n}T^D\) and
-- \(F=(1-T)/(1-p^{-1}T)\), and for a point \(\xi\) of \(V\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), with a representative
-- \(\tilde\xi\), let \(Z_\xi(H;T')=\sum_m\mu_\xi(m)T'^m\) be the zeta function of Igusa of \(H\) on the residue
-- polydisc of \(\xi\), in \(n-1\) variables and in the variable \(T'\), where \(\mu_\xi(m)\) is the volume of the \(y\)
-- in \((p\mathbb Z_p)^{n-1}\) at which \(H(\tilde\xi+y)\) has the order \(m\), and, for a character \(\chi\) of
-- \(\mathbb Z_p^\times\) trivial on \(1+p\mathbb Z_p\), let \(\sum_mc_\xi(m,\chi)T'^m\) be the same with the factor
-- \(\chi(\mathrm{ac}\,H)\), \(\mathrm{ac}\,H=Hp^{-\mathrm{ord}\,H}\) \cite[(1.1.3)]{Denef1991}. For a count \(e\ge2\)
-- write \(m_f(e)\) for the multiplicity of a primitive root of unity of order \(e\) as a root of the characteristic
-- polynomial of the monodromy of \(f\) at the origin on the cohomology of degree \(n-1\) of its Milnor fibre, for a
-- rational \(x\) write \(o(x)\) for its order, the least count \(e\) with \(ex\) whole, and the root of unity of \(x\)
-- for the root of unity that the class of \(x\) in \(\mathbb Q/\mathbb Z\) gives, of the order \(o(x)\), and write
-- \([P]\) for \(1\) when \(P\) holds and \(0\) otherwise. Then:
-- (i) if \(p\) does not divide \(d\) and every point \(\xi\) of \(V\) over \(\mathbb F_p\) is of kind 1,
-- \(g(\xi)\ne0\) modulo \(p\), or of kind 2, a smooth point of one hypersurface \(h_i=0\) and of no other, of
-- multiplicity \(a(\xi)=a_i\), at which \(g\) vanishes transversally modulo \(p\), then
-- \(Z_0(f)=(1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\#(\mathbb P^{n-1}\setminus V)\frac{X_E}{1-X_E}+\sum_{\xi\text{ of kind
-- }1}\Phi_\xi+\sum_{\xi\text{ of kind }2}\tau_{a(\xi)}\), where, with
-- \(\beta_r=(1-p^{-1})\big(\frac1{1-Y}-\frac{T^{-r}}{1-X_E}\big)\) for \(0\le r<d\),
-- \(\Phi_\xi=(1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\frac Y{1-Y}-\sum_{r=0}^{d-1}\beta_r\Psi_{\xi,r}(Y)-p^{-1}F\,\Psi'_\xi(Y)\), for the
-- sections \(\Psi_{\xi,r}(X)=\sum_{q\ge1}\mu_\xi(dq-r)X^q\) of \(Z_\xi(H)\) by the residues modulo \(d\) and
-- \(\Psi'_\xi(X)=\sum_\chi\chi(-g(\tilde\xi))^{-1}\sum_{q\ge1}c_\xi(dq,\chi)X^q\), the sum over the characters
-- \(\chi\) trivial on \(1+p\mathbb Z_p\) with \(\chi^d=1\), and
-- \(\tau_a=(1-p^{-1})p^{2-n}\big((1-p^{-1})\frac{P_1}{1-X_E}+(1-F)P_2+(1-p^{-1})X_S\big)/(1-X_S)\), where \(X_S\),
-- \(P_1\), \(P_2\) are those of \cref{thm:corner} at \(k_0=a\), so that \(\gamma=\gcd(a,d)\), \(\kappa=a/\gamma\) and
-- \(\rho=d/\gamma\), with this \(N\) and this \(d\); no hypothesis is made on the germs of \(H\) at the points of kind
-- 1; for \(0<r<d\), with \([k]_T=1+T+\dots+T^{k-1}\),
-- \(-\beta_r=(1-p^{-1})(1-T)\big(T^{-r}[r]_T+X_E[d-r]_T\big)/\big((1-X_E)(1-Y)\big)\), a quotient of positive masses
-- when \(T<1\); and at \(d=1\), where \(\Psi'_\xi=\Psi_{\xi,0}=Z_\xi(H)\),
-- \(\Phi_\xi=(1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\frac Y{1-Y}-b_pZ_\xi(H;Y)\) with
-- \(b_p=\beta_0+p^{-1}F=\frac{(1-T)(p-Y)(1-pX_E)}{p(p-T)(1-X_E)(1-Y)}\), a quotient of positive masses when \(T<1\),
-- as then \(pX_E=p^{1-n}T^N<1\);
-- (ii) under \((\mathrm T)\),
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)=\frac{n-\chi(V)}{\sigma_E}+\sum_{\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S)\,\Theta_d(H_{\mathcal S})-\sum_i\chi(Z_i)\frac{(a_i-g_i^2)s}{(1+s)d(1+a_ic')}\),
-- the sum over the strata \(\mathcal S\) of \(V\); the coefficient of \(Z_{top}(\psi;c')\) in \(\Theta_d(\psi)\) is
-- \(B'=1/\sigma_E-1/(dc')-s/((1+s)d)\), written \(B\) at \(d=1\), where \(\Theta_1(\psi)=1/c_a+B\,Z_{top}(\psi;c_a)\);
-- and for \(s>0\), \(-B'=(\sigma_E-d)(1+c')s/(d\sigma_Ec'(1+s))\), a quotient of positive masses when \(\sigma_E>d\),
-- which holds for every \(s>0\) when \(d\le n\);
-- (iii) under \((\mathrm T)\) the origin is an isolated critical point of \(f\), and with
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=\prod_q\det(1-tT_q)^{(-1)^q}\) for the monodromy \(T_q\) on the cohomology of degree \(q\) of the
-- Milnor fibre, \(\zeta_{H,\mathcal S}\) the same for the germ of \(H\) on \(\mathcal S\), and
-- \(\zeta^{(d)}_{H,\mathcal S}(t)=\prod_q\det(1-tT_q^d)^{(-1)^q}\) the same for the \(d\)-th power of its monodromy,
-- which is \(\prod_m(1-t^{m/\gcd(m,d)})^{e_m\gcd(m,d)}\) when \(\zeta_{H,\mathcal S}(t)=\prod_m(1-t^m)^{e_m}\),
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=(1-t^N)^{n-\chi(V)}(1-t^D)^{\chi(V\setminus Z)}/\prod_{\mathcal S}\zeta^{(d)}_{H,\mathcal S}(t^D)^{\chi(\mathcal S)}\),
-- the product over the strata \(\mathcal S\) of \(V\setminus Z\); and for \(H=h^{k_0}\), \(k_0\ge1\), \(h\) of degree
-- \(\delta\) without square factors and \(\Sigma=\{h=0\}\) with finitely many singular points \(P\), each, as a germ
-- in \(n-1\) variables, of Milnor number \(\mu_P\) and with the multiplicity \(m_P(e)\), for every count \(e\ge1\), of a
-- primitive root of unity of order \(e\) as a root of the characteristic polynomial of its monodromy on the cohomology
-- of degree \(n-2\) of its Milnor fibre (so \(m_P(1)\) is the multiplicity of the root \(1\), which is \(r_P-1\) for a
-- plane curve germ with \(r_P\) branches, by \cref{fact:acampo} on a resolution whose exceptional curves form a tree of
-- rational curves), with \(\gamma=\gcd(k_0,d)\), \(\kappa=k_0/\gamma\), \(N_S=\kappa D\) and
-- \(m_P^{[k_0,d]}(e)=\sum_\mu m_P(e_\mu)\), the sum over the \(d\) roots \(\mu\) of \(\mu^d=\nu^D\) for a primitive root
-- of unity \(\nu\) of order \(e\), with \(e_\mu\) the order of \(\mu^{k_0}\),
-- \(m_f(e)=(-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))[e\mid N]+(-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\big(\gamma[e\mid N_S]-[e\mid
-- D]\big)+\sum_Pm^{[k_0,d]}_P(e)\), and the Milnor number of \(f\) is
-- \((-1)^{n-1}\big(N(n-\chi(\Sigma))-(k_0-1)D\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)-1\big)+k_0D\sum_P\mu_P\);
-- (iv) for \(H=h^{k_0}\) as in (iii), \(k_0\ge2\), \(\rho=d/\gamma\) and \(s_S=-(nk_0+d)/(k_0D)\), so that
-- \(N_Ss_S=-(n\kappa+\rho)\): (L1) the poles of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) lie among \(-1\), \(-n/N\), \(s_S\) and the numbers
-- \(s_0=-(n+d\theta/k_0)/D\) for \(-\theta\) a pole of the local topological zeta function of \(h\) at a singular point
-- \(P\) of \(\Sigma\) or of its layer \(Z^{(e)}_{top,P}(h^{k_0})\) for a count \(e\ge2\) dividing \(d\); (L2) for such an
-- \(s_0\), with \(-\theta\) a pole of \(Z_{top,P}(h)\), if \(o(s_0)\nmid N\), \(m_P(o(\theta))\ge1\) and either
-- \(n=3\) or \(\rho\theta\) is not whole, then \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge1\); at \(d=1\), \(\rho\theta\) is not whole exactly
-- when \(o(\theta)\ge2\); (L3) \(m_f(o(s_S))=(-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))[o(s_S)\mid N]+(-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z)\big(\gamma-[o(s_S)\mid D]\big)+\sum_Pm^{[k_0,d]}_P(o(s_S))\), which is at least \(1\) for \(n=3\); (L4) for
-- \(n\ne\delta\) and \(n\ne N\), with \(W(w)=n-\chi(\Sigma)+\sum_{\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S)Z_{top,\mathcal
-- S}(h;w-n/\delta)\), the sum over the strata \(\mathcal S\) of \(\Sigma\), which is \(\delta w\,Z_{top,0}(h;w-n/\delta)\)
-- for the topological zeta function of the cone \(h\) at the origin, \(Z_{top,0}(f)-B'\,W(k_0D(s+n/N)/d)\) has no pole at
-- \(-n/N\), where \(\sigma_EB'\) is \(1\), unless a component of an embedded resolution of the germ of \(h\) at a
-- singular point of \(\Sigma\), of multiplicity \(N_i\) and log discrepancy \(\nu_i\), has \(\nu_i/N_i=n/\delta\) and
-- \(\gcd(d,k_0N_i)\ge2\), which asks \(d\ge2\); so, but for such a component, \(-n/N\) is a pole of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\)
-- exactly when \(W\) has a pole at \(0\) or \(W(0)\ne0\), of the order one more than that of \(W\), and when \(W\) has no
-- pole at \(0\), \(N\) times the residue is \(W(0)\); that is, \(-n/N\) is a pole of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) exactly when
-- \(-n/\delta\) is a pole of \(Z_{top,0}(h)\), of the same order, and for a pole of order one \(N\) times the residue of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) is \(\delta\) times that of \(Z_{top,0}(h)\); as \(W\) depends neither on \(k_0\), nor on \(d\), nor
-- on \(g\), \(-n/N\) is then a pole of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) exactly when \(-n/\delta\) is one, of the same order, of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f_1)\) for \(f_1=h+g_1\), with \(g_1\) a form of degree \(\delta+1\) with \((\mathrm T)\); and
-- \(m_f(o(n/N))=(-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))+(-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\big(\gamma[\gamma\delta\mid nD]-[N\mid
-- nd]\big)+\sum_P\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}m_P\big(o(n/\delta+k_0j/d)\big)\), whose bracket is at least \(0\); so for
-- \(\delta\nmid n\) and \((-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\ge0\), which holds at \(n=3\),
-- \(m_f(o(n/N))\ge m_{f_1}(o(n/\delta))=(-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))+\sum_Pm_P(o(n/\delta))\), with equality at \(d=1\);
-- (v) at \(d=1\), for \(H=h^{k_0}\) as in (iv) with every singular point of \(\Sigma\) an ordinary double point, if
-- \((-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))\ge1\) and \((-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\ge1\), then every pole \(s_0\) of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) has \(o(s_0)=1\) or \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge1\); this clause is not stated for \(d\ge2\), where its count
-- is not redone.
-- The rows, one clause each: (stard) the star \(-B'\) of the three costs of one level is a quotient of positive masses
-- for \(s>0\) and \(\sigma_E>d\), read on \cref{thm:corner}(square) at the part \(1\) and the base \(d\); (qstar) its
-- q-form \(b_p\) at \(d=1\), in \(p\), \(X_E\), \(T\), read on \cref{thm:corner}(square) at the part \(1/q\);
-- (qshelld) for \(0<r<d\), \(-\beta_r\) in the letters \(T\), \(R=T^r\), \(U=T^{d-r}\) and \(X=X_E\), with the masses
-- \([r]_T\) and \([d-r]_T\) of \cref{lem:qnumber} in the frame \(1-T\), \(A\) with \(A(1-T)+R=1\) and \(B\) with
-- \(B(1-T)+U=1\); and (bracket) the bracket of (L4) is at least \(0\): \(N\mid nd\) gives \(\gamma\delta\mid nD\).
theorem thm:level [one level of the tower]
  (stard): E, s, c, m, d in QQ>0, c = E + d * s, E = d + m : 1 / c + s / (d * (1 + s)) = 1 / E + m * (d + c) * s / (d * E * c * (1 + s))
  fix (stard) corner.stard
  neqfix (stard) corner.stard.nocost
  (qstar): q, X, T, e, u, v, w, z, r in QQ>0, e + 1 = q, u + T = 1, v + X = 1, w + X * T = 1, z + T = q, r + q * X = 1 : u / z + e * X * T / (q * w) = e * X / (q * v) + u * (e + w) * r / (q * z * v * w)
  fix (qstar) corner.qstar
  neqfix (qstar) corner.qstar.nowall
  (qshelld): q, e, T, X, R, U, a, A, B, v, w in QQ>0, e + 1 = q, a + T = 1, A * a + R = 1, B * a + U = 1, v + X = 1, w + X * R * U = 1 : e / (q * R * v) = e / (q * w) + e * a * (A / R + X * B) / (q * v * w)
  fix (qshelld) corner.qshelld
  neqfix (qshelld) corner.qshelld.noshell
  (bracket): g, kappa, delta, n, d, u in NN>=1, g * kappa * delta * u = n * d : g * delta * (kappa * (n + u)) = n * (g * kappa * delta + d)
  fix (bracket) corner.bracket
  neqfix (bracket) corner.bracket.nodiv
  origin: ?{\text{the result, by the mechanism of \cref{thm:corner}: one level of the tower for every}\ n,\ k_0\ \text{and}\ d\ \text{and every germ of}\ H\ \text{at a point of}\ V\text{, read over each stratum: (i) at a good prime not dividing}\ d\ \text{with (qshelld) and, at}\ d=1\text{, (qstar), the term of a point of}\ Z\ \text{by \cref{thm:corner}(i) and \cref{lem:cone}(qapex), (ii) by \cref{thm:strata}(i) at the points of}\ Z\ \text{with (stard), and (iv) and (v) read off (ii) and (iii) with (bracket); the classical cases are cited inside it: off}\ Z\ \text{(ii) is \cite[Theorem~5.6, (Z1)]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026} at}\ k=d\text{, and at}\ d=1\ \text{\cite[Theorem~1.11]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, with the principle of strata \cite[1.2 and Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}; (iii) is \cite[Theorem~1, Corollary~1 and Lemma~1]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997}, on the formula of A'Campo \cite{ACampo1975}, with \cite[Proposition~8.3 and Lemma~8.6]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026} at isolated points, for}\ n=3\ \text{\cite[3.5]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997}, and the case of \cite[Theorem~B]{Nemethi1991}; the tools are standard: one blowing-up of the origin, read by \cref{fact:zetatop} and, at a good prime, by the order laws on the tubes and the lemma of Hensel; the rows are proved in Lean, and (i) to (v) are proved in the text from them}}

-- \textit{(i).} Blowing up the origin, a point \(x\) of order \(a\ge1\) is \(x=t(\tilde\xi+y)\), with \(t\) of order \(a\),
-- \(\xi\) the point of the projective space that \(x\) gives over \(\mathbb F_p\) and \(y\) in its residue polydisc, of
-- volume \(p^{1-n}\), and the measure of \(x\) is \(|t|^{n-1}\) times that of \(t\) and \(y\), so that the shell of the
-- \(t\) of order \(a\) has the measure \((1-p^{-1})p^{-na}\). Off \(V\), \(f=t^N\) times a unit, which gives the first
-- term. At a point of kind 1, \(f=t^N(H(\tilde\xi+y)+t^dG)\), with \(G\) a unit of residue \(g(\tilde\xi)\). Fix \(y\),
-- with \(m\ge1\) the order of \(H(\tilde\xi+y)\) and \(h_0\) its angular component modulo \(p\). On the shell of order
-- \(a\) the order of \(f\) is \(Na+da\) when \(da<m\) and \(Na+m\) when \(da>m\). When \(da=m\), with \(t=p^at_0\), it
-- is \(Na+m\) unless \(h_0+t_0^dg(\tilde\xi)\) vanishes modulo \(p\), which it does at \(\rho_d\) residues of \(t_0\),
-- the number of \(v\in\mathbb F_p\) with \(v^d=-h_0/g(\tilde\xi)\), each a simple root as \(p\nmid d\); over each of
-- them the order is \(Na+m+j\) on the relative volume \(p^{-j}\) for each \(j\ge1\), by the lemma of Hensel, which
-- gives \(p^{-1}(1-F)\) in place of \(p^{-1}\); so that shell gives \(\big((1-p^{-1})-\rho_dp^{-1}F\big)p^{-na}T^{Na+m}\).
-- So for \(m=dq-r\), with \(q\ge1\) and \(0\le r<d\), the shells give
-- \((1-p^{-1})\sum_{a<q}Y^a+(1-p^{-1})T^m\sum_{a\ge q}X_E^a-[r=0]\rho_dp^{-1}FY^q\), which is
-- \((1-p^{-1})\frac Y{1-Y}-\beta_rY^q-[r=0]\rho_dp^{-1}FY^q\), as \(T^{dq}X_E^q=Y^q\). Summed over \(y\), the first
-- term gives \((1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\frac Y{1-Y}\), the second \(\beta_r\Psi_{\xi,r}(Y)\), and the third
-- \(p^{-1}F\Psi'_\xi(Y)\), as \(\rho_d(c)=\sum_\chi\chi(c)\) over the characters with \(\chi^d=1\), which are trivial
-- on \(1+p\mathbb Z_p\), and \(\chi(-h_0/g(\tilde\xi))=\chi(\mathrm{ac}\,H(\tilde\xi+y))\chi(-g(\tilde\xi))^{-1}\).
-- For \(0<r<d\) the form of \(-\beta_r\) is (qshelld) at \(q=p\), \(X=X_E\), \(R=T^r\), \(U=T^{d-r}\), \(A=[r]_T\),
-- \(B=[d-r]_T\), \(v=1-X_E\) and \(w=1-Y\), as \(Y=X_ERU\). At \(d=1\), \(\rho_1=1\), so \(\Psi'_\xi=\Psi_{\xi,0}=Z_\xi(H)\),
-- and \(\beta_0+p^{-1}F=b_p\) is (qstar) at \(q=p\), \(X=X_E\) and the letter \(T\), with \(XT=Y\): there
-- \(u/z=p^{-1}F\), \(eXT/(qw)-eX/(qv)=\beta_0\) and \(u(e+w)r/(qzvw)=b_p\). The gaps of (qstar) are positive on the cone
-- \(q>1\), \(0<T<1\), \(0<X<1/q\) of rationals, and those of (qshelld) on the cone \(q>1\) and \(0<T,R,U,X<1\), each of
-- which holds a product of infinite sets of rationals, and a polynomial that is \(0\) on such a product is \(0\); so
-- (qstar) and (qshelld), cleared of their denominators, are identities of polynomials in their letters, and they hold
-- in the field of rational functions, as they are used above at \(q=p\), \(R=T^r\) and \(U=T^{d-r}\).
--
-- At a point of kind 2, \(H(\tilde\xi+y)=u^a\varepsilon\) with \(u=\tilde h_i\) and \(\varepsilon\) a unit, and
-- \(w=(g(\tilde\xi+y)+t(\dots))/\varepsilon\) is a coordinate with \(u\) modulo \(p\), by the transversality, so that
-- \(f\) is a unit times \(t^N(u^a+t^dw)\), and the order laws on the tube are those of a point of \(Z\) in the proof of
-- \cref{thm:corner}(i) at \(k_0=a\) and this \(d\), which use \(N\) only as the multiplicity of \(E\) and ask nothing
-- of \(p\) there; this gives \(\tau_a\). Fibre by fibre, for fixed \(t\) of order \(a\) and \(u\), with \(m'\) the order
-- of \(u^a\), the integral over \(w\) in \(p\mathbb Z_p\) is \(p^{-1}T^{m'}\) when \(m'\le da\), the base; and when
-- \(m'>da\), the apex, \(w\mapsto t_0^dw+p^{-da}u^a\) is a bijection of \(p\mathbb Z_p\) that keeps the measure, so
-- that the integral is \(p^{-1}T^{da}(1-F)\), where \(1-F=(p-1)T/(p-T)\) is the term \(eT/b\) of the edge of
-- \cref{lem:cone}(qapex) at \(q=p\) and \(b=p-T\).
--
-- \textit{(ii).} For \(s\) a positive rational the integrals of (i) are the sums of \cref{fact:zetatop}, and by the
-- principle of strata \cite[1.2 and Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002} \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) is
-- \(\chi(\mathbb P^{n-1}\setminus V)/\sigma_E\), with \(\chi(\mathbb P^{n-1}\setminus V)=n-\chi(V)\), plus the sum over
-- the strata of \(E\cap V\) of their Euler characteristics times the term of a point. At a point \(\xi\) of \(V\setminus
-- Z\) the blown-up \(f\) is \(t^N(H(\tilde\xi+y)+t^d\eta)\) with \(\eta\) a unit, and with \(t\) for \(t\eta^{1/d}\), a
-- root of the unit, it is a unit times \(t^N(t^d+H_\xi(y))\), for the form a unit times \(t^{n-1}dt\wedge dy\). Its term
-- is \cite[Theorem~5.6, (Z1)]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026} at \(m=N\), \(k=d\), \(\nu'=1\) and
-- \(\nu_z=n\), read for the germ \(z^m(z^k+f)\) at \(z=t\) and \(f=H_\xi\), where their \(r\) is our \(c'\), so that
-- \(\sigma_E=d(r-s)\), and their layers of the orders dividing \(d\) are ours; it is \(\Theta_d(H_\xi)\), as
-- \cref{fact:suspension} reads it at a monomial germ, and its proof uses only an embedded resolution of the germ of \(H\)
-- at the point, so the germ may be singular or not reduced. At a point of \(Z_i\), under \((\mathrm T)\),
-- \(H(\tilde\xi+y)=u^{a_i}\varepsilon\) with \(\varepsilon\) a unit, and \(w=(g(\tilde\xi+y)+t(\dots))/\varepsilon\) is
-- a coordinate with \(u\), by the transversality, so the germ is a unit times \(t^N(u^{a_i}+t^dw)\), the germ of \(U_J\)
-- of \cref{thm:strata} at one divisor, \(M=a_i\) and \(\nu=1\). By \cref{thm:strata}(i) its term is
-- \(U_J=P_c/\sigma_E+S_J/((1+s)d)\), with \(P_c=1/(1+a_ic')\) and \(S_J=a_i/(1+a_ic')\); and
-- \(\Theta_d(u^{a_i})\), with \(Z_{top}(u^{a_i};s')=1/(1+a_is')\), its layers \([e\mid a_i]/(1+a_is')\) and
-- \(\sum_{e\mid d}J_2(e)[e\mid a_i]=g_i^2\), is \(U_J+(a_i-g_i^2)s/((1+s)d(1+a_ic'))\). So the term of a point of
-- \(Z_i\) is that of a smooth point of \(h_i=0\) less \((a_i-g_i^2)s/((1+s)d(1+a_ic'))\). (stard) at \(E=\sigma_E\),
-- \(c=dc'\) and \(m=\sigma_E-d\), with \(dc'=\sigma_E+ds\) and \(d+c=d(1+c')\), is the form of \(-B'\); and
-- \(\sigma_E-d=n-d+Ns\) is positive for every \(s>0\) when \(d\le n\).
--
-- \textit{(iii).} The origin is an isolated critical point: a curve of critical points through it would lie in \(f=0\),
-- as \(f\) is constant on it \cite[\S3]{Milnor1968}, but near \(E\) and off it \(f\) is \(t^N\) times the equation of
-- the strict transform: \(H(\tilde\xi+y)+t^d\eta\) at a point of \(V\setminus Z\), whose derivative in \(t\),
-- \(t^{d-1}(d\eta+t\partial_t\eta)\), does not vanish for \(t\ne0\), and \(u^a\varepsilon+t^d\varepsilon w\) at a point
-- of \(Z\), whose derivative in \(w\) is \(t^d\varepsilon\). By \cite[Theorem~1 and Corollary~1]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997},
-- which rest on the formula of \cite{ACampo1975}, as the blowing-up is an isomorphism off the origin, the zeta
-- function is the product over the strata of \(E\) of the zeta functions of the germs of the blown-up \(f\) at their
-- points, to the powers of their Euler characteristics, and a unit factor leaves the zeta function of a germ unchanged,
-- as a resolution of one is a resolution of the other, with the same multiplicities: off \(V\) the component \(E\)
-- gives \((1-t^N)^{n-\chi(V)}\). At a point of \(V\setminus Z\) the germ is a unit times \(t^N(t^d+\varphi)\), with
-- \(t\) for \(t\eta^{1/d}\) and \(\varphi\) the germ of \(H\), which has the factor
-- \((1-t^D)/\zeta^{(d)}_\varphi(t^D)\): when \(\varphi\) has an isolated critical point this is
-- \cite[Proposition~8.3]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026} at \(k=d\), which restates
-- \cite[\S3.2]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997}; for every \(\varphi\), take the Milnor fibre of \(f\) over a tube of
-- \(\varphi\), the points of a small ball at which \(\varphi\) lies in a small disc, over the punctured disc of which
-- \(\varphi\) is a fibration with the Milnor fibre of \(\varphi\) as its fibre. Then \((t,y)\mapsto(t,\varphi(y))\)
-- maps the Milnor fibre of \(f\) onto the curve \(a^N(a^d+b)=\varepsilon\), a punctured line on which the monodromy acts
-- freely with the period \(D\), as \((a,b)\mapsto(\omega a,\omega^db)\) for the root of unity \(\omega\) of \(1/D\), with
-- \(D\) points at \(b=0\), over which the fibre is contractible, and elsewhere the Milnor fibre of \(\varphi\), turned by
-- the \(d\)-th power of its monodromy when the curve is turned \(D\) times, as \(b\) turns \(d\) times about \(0\); so
-- the Lefschetz numbers of the \(j\)-th powers of the monodromy are \(0\) off the multiples of \(D\) and
-- \(D(1-\Lambda(h_\varphi^{dj'}))\) at \(j=Dj'\), which is the factor, the roots of the characteristic polynomials of
-- \(h_\varphi^d\) being the \(d\)-th powers of those of \(h_\varphi\)
-- \cite[Lemma~8.6]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}. A point of \(Z\) gives \(1\): there the germ is a
-- unit times \(t^N(u^a+t^dw)\), and the formula of \cite{ACampo1975} on its toric resolution, that of the proof of
-- \cref{thm:corner}(v) at \(k_0=a\) and this \(d\), has over the point only parts of the Euler characteristic \(0\):
-- the rays of the vertices give tori of dimension two, those of the edge such tori cut by the strict transform along
-- tori of dimension one, and the other rays lie over other strata. This is the case of \cite[Theorem~B]{Nemethi1991},
-- for \(f=P(h,g)\), in which the factor of the discriminant is written in closed form, and for \(n=3\), \(d=1\) and \(g\)
-- without square factors it is \cite[3.5]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997} at \(k=1\).
--
-- For \(H=h^{k_0}\) the Milnor fibre of the germ at \(P\) is \(k_0\) copies of that of \(h\), permuted cyclically by
-- the monodromy, whose \(k_0\)-th power is that of \(h\); so a root \(\mu\) of the characteristic polynomial of the
-- monodromy of \(h^{k_0}\) at \(P\) on the cohomology of degree \(n-2\) has the multiplicity \(m_P(e_\mu)\), and the
-- roots for its \(d\)-th power are the \(\mu^d\). At a smooth point \(\zeta_{u^{k_0}}(t)=1-t^{k_0}\), so
-- \(\zeta^{(d)}_{u^{k_0}}(t^D)=(1-t^{N_S})^\gamma\), and at \(P\) the cohomology of degree \(0\) gives the same factor;
-- so \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=(1-t^N)^{n-\chi(\Sigma)}\big((1-t^D)/(1-t^{N_S})^\gamma\big)^{\chi(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z)}\prod_P\Delta_P(t^D)^{(-1)^{n-1}}\), with \(\Delta_P(t)=\det(1-tT^d)\) for the monodromy \(T\) of \(h^{k_0}\) at
-- \(P\) on the cohomology of degree \(n-2\), whose roots are the inverses of the \(\mu^d\), of the same orders; a root
-- of unity \(\nu\) of order \(e\) is a root of \(\Delta_P(t^D)\) of the multiplicity \(m^{[k_0,d]}_P(e)\), the
-- inverse of \(\nu^D\) being some \(\mu^d\) exactly when \(\nu^D\) is, of the same multiplicity. As
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)/(1-t)\) is the characteristic polynomial of the monodromy on the cohomology of degree \(n-1\) to the
-- power \((-1)^{n-1}\), \(m_f(e)\) is as said; the degree gives the Milnor number.
--
-- \textit{(iv).} (L1) is read off (ii): a pole of \(Z_{top,P}(h^{k_0};c')=Z_{top,P}(h;k_0c')\), or of a layer of
-- \(h^{k_0}\) at \(P\), at \(k_0c'=-\theta\) is \(s_0\); a smooth point gives \(1/(1+k_0c')\), its layers
-- \([e\mid k_0]/(1+k_0c')\) and the term of \(Z\) \(1/(1+k_0c')\), whose pole is \(s_S\); and \(c'=0\) is no pole, as
-- \(\Theta_d\) holds \(1/(dc')\) only in \((1-Z_{top,\mathcal S}(H;c'))/(dc')\), where \(Z_{top,\mathcal S}(H;0)=1\)
-- \cite[Remark~1.10]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}. (L2): put \(e=o(s_0)\); as \(-Ds_0=n+d\theta/k_0\), the root
-- of unity \(\nu_0\) of \(-s_0\), of order \(e\), has \(\nu_0^D=\mu_0^d\) for the root \(\mu_0\) of \(\theta/k_0\),
-- and \(\mu_0^{k_0}\) is the root of \(\theta\), of order \(o(\theta)\); so the term of \(P\) in (iii) is at least
-- \(m_P(o(\theta))\ge1\), and the other terms of \(P'\ne P\) are at least \(0\). The first term of (iii) is \(0\) as
-- \(e\nmid N\). If \(\rho\theta\) is not whole, \(e\nmid N_S\), as \(N_Ss_0=-\kappa n-\rho\theta\), and so \(e\nmid D\),
-- as \(D\mid N_S\), and the middle term is \(0\); and at \(n=3\), \(Z\) is \(\delta D\) points, so
-- \(-\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=\delta D-\chi(\Sigma)\), which is positive as \(\chi(\Sigma)\le2\delta\), \(\Sigma\) being a
-- plane curve of at most \(\delta\) components, whose normalization has the Euler number at most \(2\delta\) and maps
-- onto \(\Sigma\) gluing branches; and \(\gamma[e\mid N_S]\ge[e\mid D]\) as \(D\mid N_S\), so the middle term is at
-- least \(0\). (L3): \(N_Ss_S\) is whole, so \(o(s_S)\mid N_S\), which is (iii) at \(o(s_S)\). At \(n=3\),
-- \(\gamma-[o(s_S)\mid D]\ge1\), as \(o(s_S)\mid D\) asks \(Ds_S=-n-d/k_0\) whole, that is \(k_0\mid d\), and then
-- \(\gamma=k_0\ge2\); so \(m_f(o(s_S))\ge(3-\chi(\Sigma))[o(s_S)\mid N]+\delta D-\chi(\Sigma)\ge\delta(D-2)-(2\delta-3)[o(s_S)\mid
-- N]\), which is at least \(1\) as \(D\ge2\delta+1\).
--
-- (L4): by (ii), as \(\sum_{\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S)=\chi(\Sigma)\) and \(k_0c'=k_0D(s+n/N)/d-n/\delta\),
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)-B'\,W(k_0D(s+n/N)/d)=n/(dc')+(n-\chi(\Sigma))s/((1+s)d)-L-(k_0-\gamma^2)\chi(Z)s/((1+s)d(1+k_0c'))\),
-- with \(L=\frac s{(1+s)d}\sum_{2\le e\mid d}J_2(e)\sum_{\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S)Z^{(e)}_{top,\mathcal
-- S}(h^{k_0};c')\); at \(-n/N\), \(c'=-n/N\), \(1+s\) is not \(0\) as \(n\ne N\), \(1+k_0c'=1-n/\delta\) is not \(0\)
-- as \(n\ne\delta\), and a layer has a pole there only through a component of the germ at a singular point whose
-- multiplicity \(k_0N_i\) an \(e\ge2\) dividing \(d\) divides and with \(k_0c'=-\nu_i/N_i\), the case set aside, as at a
-- smooth point its pole is at \(1+k_0c'=0\); so this has no pole there; and there \(\sigma_E=N(s+n/N)\) is \(0\) and
-- \(\sigma_EB'=1-\sigma_E/(dc')-\sigma_Es/((1+s)d)\) is \(1\). So if \(W(w)\) is \(w^{-r}\) times a function with no
-- pole and no zero at \(0\), \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) has at \(-n/N\) a pole of order \(r+1\) when \(r\ge0\) and none when
-- \(r<0\), and for \(r\le0\) its residue is \(W(0)/N\).
--
-- \textit{The cone.} One blowing-up of the origin, read by \cref{fact:zetatop} as in (ii) with \(h\) in the place of \(f\), has
-- the component \(E\) of multiplicity \(\delta\) and log discrepancy \(n\); off \(\Sigma\) it is \(E\) alone, and over a
-- point \(\xi\) of a stratum \(\mathcal S\) of \(\Sigma\) the blown-up \(h\) is \(t^\delta h(\tilde\xi+y)\), whose components over
-- the point are \(E\) and those of an embedded resolution of the germ of \(h\) at \(\xi\) in the \(n-1\) variables
-- \(y\), each crossed with the line of \(t\); so, by the principle of strata \cite[1.2 and
-- Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002},
-- \(Z_{top,0}(h;s)=\big(n-\chi(\Sigma)+\sum_{\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S)Z_{top,\mathcal S}(h;s)\big)/(n+\delta s)\), which at \(s=w-n/\delta\),
-- where \(n+\delta s=\delta w\), is \(W(w)/(\delta w)\). So \(Z_{top,0}(h)\) has at \(-n/\delta\) a pole of order
-- \(r+1\) when \(r\ge0\) and none when \(r<0\), and for \(r=0\) its residue is \(W(0)/\delta\).
--
-- The same reading of (ii) for \(f_1\), at \(k_0=d=1\) with \(\delta\), \(\delta+1\) in the place of \(N\), \(D\), has
-- the same \(W\), so the same \(r\). For the multiplicities, \(e=o(n/N)\) divides \(N\); it divides \(N_S=\kappa D\)
-- exactly when \(\gamma\delta\mid nD\), as \(N_Sn/N=nD/(\gamma\delta)\), and \(D\) exactly when \(N\mid nd\), as
-- \(nD/N=n+nd/N\); for the root \(\nu\) of \(n/N\), \(\nu^D\) is the root of \(nd/N\), so the \(\mu\) with
-- \(\mu^d=\nu^D\) are the roots of \(n/N+j/d\), \(0\le j<d\), and \(\mu^{k_0}\) is the root of \(n/\delta+k_0j/d\); and
-- (iii) gives the closed form. Its bracket is at least \(0\), as \(N\mid nd\) gives \(\gamma\delta\mid nD\) by (bracket)
-- at \(g=\gamma\) and \(nd=Nu\), with the quotient \(\kappa(n+u)\). Keeping the term \(j=0\) of the last sum gives the
-- bound for \(\delta\nmid n\), where \(f_1\) has the closed form at \(k_0=d=1\), whose bracket is \([\delta\mid
-- n(\delta+1)]-[\delta\mid n]=0\); and at \(d=1\) and \(\delta\nmid n\) the bracket of \(f\) is \([\delta\mid
-- nD]-[N\mid n]=0\), as \(D=N+1\) is prime to \(\delta\), so the two are equal.
--
-- \textit{(v).} The germ \(\sum_{i<n}y_i^2\) of an ordinary double point in \(n-1\) variables has, on one blowing-up, the poles
-- \(-1\) and \(-(n-1)/2\) by \cref{fact:zetatop}, and its monodromy the one root \((-1)^{n-1}\), of order
-- \(o((n-1)/2)\), by \cite{ACampo1975}. At \(-1\) the root \(1\) is that of the cohomology of degree \(0\). At \(d=1\),
-- \(\gamma=1\), \(N_S=k_0D\) and \(m^{[k_0,1]}_P(e)=m_P(e/\gcd(e,k_0D))\). At \(-n/N\), by the closed form of (L4),
-- \(m_f\ge(-1)^{n-1}(n-\chi(\Sigma))\ge1\) for \(\delta\nmid n\), and for \(\delta\mid n\) and \(N\nmid n\) the bracket
-- is \(1\) and \(m_f\ge2\); at \(s_S\), \(m_f\ge(-1)^n\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\ge1\) by (L3), the bracket having the
-- sign of the first hypothesis. At the lift \(s_0\) of \(\theta=(n-1)/2\), \(o(s_0)/\gcd(o(s_0),k_0D)=o(\theta)\), every
-- node gives \(m_P(o(\theta))=1\), and the other two terms of (iii) are at least \(0\) by the two hypotheses, as
-- \([e\mid D]\le[e\mid k_0D]\); so \(m_f(o(s_0))\) is at least the number of nodes. The lift of \(-1\) is \(s_S\).
proof thm:level
  (stard) with[E = d + 1 * m by V[1 * h_2]; with[d * (1 + s) = d + 1 * (d * s) by V; with[E * (d * (1 + s)) = c * d + 1 * (d * s) * m by V[d * h_1, (d * s) * h_2]; with[c = E + 1 * (d * s) by V[1 * h_1]; with[c = d * (1 + s) + 1 * m by V[1 * h_1, 1 * h_2]; with[c / E + c / (d * (1 + s)) + 1 * (d + c * 1) * (d * s) * m / (d * E * (d * (1 + s))) = 1 + c / d by thm:corner(square)[n := c, x := E, y := d * (1 + s), b := d, u := d * s, w := m, X := 1, P := 1, Q := 1]; V[| (1 / c) * hw6]]]]]]]
  (qstar) with[q * v = e + 1 / q * (q * r) by V[q * h_3 | 1 * h_1, 1 * h_6]; with[z = e + 1 / q * (q * u) by V[1 * h_5 | 1 * h_1, 1 * h_2]; with[q * v * z = q * w * e + 1 / q * (q * u) * (q * r) by V[(q * v) * h_5, (q * u * v) * h_1, (q * e * X) * h_2, (q * q * u + q * e) * h_3 | (q * v + q * u) * h_1, (q * v) * h_2, (q * u * e) * h_3, (q * u) * h_6, (q * e) * h_4]; with[q * w = q * v + X * (q * u) by V[q * h_4 | q * h_3, (q * X) * h_2]; with[q * w = z + T / q * (q * r) by V[q * h_4 | 1 * h_5, T * h_6]; with[q * w / (q * v) + q * w / z + 1 / q * (e + q * w * (1 / q)) * (q * u) * (q * r) / (e * (q * v) * z) = 1 + q * w / e by thm:corner(square)[n := q * w, x := q * v, y := z, b := e, u := q * u, w := q * r, X := 1 / q, P := X, Q := T / q]; V[(e / (q * w)) * h_4, (1 / z) * h_1, (1 / z) * h_2 | (e / (q * w)) * hw6, (e / (q * v)) * h_3, (1 / z) * h_5]]]]]]]
  (qshelld) V[(e / (q * R * v * w)) * h_6, (e / (q * v * w)) * h_5, (e / (q * R * v * w)) * h_3, (e * X / (q * v * w)) * h_4]
  (bracket) V[1 * h_1]

-- For \(n=3\), every form \(h\) of degree \(\delta\) without square factors, every \(k_0\ge2\), every \(d\ge1\) and every
-- form \(g\) of degree \(k_0\delta+d\) with \((\mathrm T)\), the local monodromy conjecture holds for \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) at
-- \(-1\), at \(s_S\), at every lift \(s_0\) of a pole of the local topological zeta function of \(h\) at a singular point
-- of \(\Sigma\) with \(o(s_0)\nmid N\), and at \(-3/N\), unless a component of an embedded resolution of the germ of
-- \(h\) at a singular point of \(\Sigma\) with \(\nu_i/N_i=3/\delta\) and \(\gcd(d,k_0N_i)\ge2\) makes it a pole through
-- a layer alone, which asks \(d\ge2\) and \(\delta\ge4\). At \(d=1\) these are all the poles, so the conjecture holds for
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\); for \(d\ge2\) three cases are open: the lifts \(s_0\) with \(o(s_0)\mid N\), the lifts of the poles
-- of a layer alone, and \(-3/N\) through a layer alone. At \(k_0=1\) and \(d=1\), \(f\) is a superisolated singularity,
-- and the conjecture is \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}; at \(k_0=1\) and every \(d\), \((\mathrm
-- T)\) makes \(f\) a \(d\)-LYS singularity \cite[Definition~7.1]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}, and it
-- is \cite[Theorem~8.2]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}. For every \(n\ge3\) and \(k_0\ge2\) it holds at
-- \(d=1\) under the hypotheses of \cref{thm:level}(v), by \cref{thm:level}(v). For \(H=h^{k_0}\) as in
-- \cref{thm:level}(iii), with \((\mathrm T)\), every \(k_0\ge1\) and every \(d\ge1\), let \(\mathrm{lct}_P(h)\) be the
-- log canonical threshold of the germ of \(h\) at a singular point \(P\) of \(\Sigma\): if \(\delta\ge n\) and
-- \(\mathrm{lct}_P(h)\ge n/\delta\) at every \(P\), then \(n/N\) is the log canonical threshold of \(f\) at the origin
-- and \(-n/N\) is the largest root of the local Bernstein--Sato polynomial \(b_{f,0}\), so the strong form holds at
-- \(-n/N\) whenever it is a pole; if \(\delta\ge n\) and \(\theta\), the least \(\mathrm{lct}_P(h)\), is below
-- \(n/\delta\), the threshold is the lifted ratio \((n+d\theta/k_0)/D\), below \(n/N\). At the lift
-- \(s_0=-(n+\theta/k_0)/D\) of a pole \(-\theta\) of the germ of \(h\) at a singular point \(P\) of \(\Sigma\), at
-- \(d=1\) and with \(o(s_0)\nmid N\), the root of order \(o(s_0)\) is read on the layer of the same value: a component
-- \((N_i,\nu_i)\) of a resolution of the germ lifts to the component \((k_0N_iD,k_0nN_i+\nu_i)\) of \(f\), whose cost at
-- \(s_0\) is the cost \(\nu_i-\theta N_i\) of the germ at \(-\theta\) (liftd), so that by \cref{lem:mark} its mark
-- modulo \(\Phi_{o(s_0)}\) is the mark of the germ modulo \(\Phi_{o(\theta)}\); the star \(b_p\) of \cref{thm:level}(i)
-- is \(1\) on that layer; and the value of \(f\) at \(s_0\) on its layer is minus the sum of those of the germs on theirs,
-- as the exponents are in (L2), \(z_f(o(s_0))=-\sum_Pz_{h,P}(o(\theta))\). This reading is a tie over \(\mathbb Q\). At
-- \(d\ge2\) the component \((N_i,\nu_i)\) lifts to \((k_0N_iD,nk_0N_i+d\nu_i)/G_i\), with \(G_i=\gcd(k_0N_i,d)\), whose
-- cost at the lift \(s_0=-(n+d\theta/k_0)/D\) is \(d/G_i\) times that of the germ (liftd), and the reading on the layer
-- is not redone there. That the exponent is not zero is a count, which rests on cited inputs: the conjecture for plane
-- curves \cite{Loeser1988} at the lifts, the branches of the germs at \(s_S\) in (L3), and
-- \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002} for \(f_1\) at \(-3/N\) in (L4). The rows: the cost of a lifted
-- component at \(s_0\), written with \(x=-s_0\) as the tie \(\nu'+d\theta N_i=xN'+d\nu_i\) of two positive sums, with
-- \((N',\nu')=(k_0N_iD,k_0nN_i+d\nu_i)\) the lifted component before the division by \(G_i\) (liftd); and the lifted
-- ratio \(x\) at the least threshold \(\theta\) of the germs of \(h\), against \(n/N\): \(xNDk_0+nk_0d=nDk_0+d\theta
-- N\), so that \(x\ge n/N\) exactly when \(\theta\ge n/\delta\) (lct).
corollary cor:levelmonodromy [the monodromy conjecture over one level]
  (liftd): k, n, N, D, d, nu in NN>=1, r, x in QQ>0, x * D = n + d * r / k : k * n * N + d * nu + d * r * N = x * (k * N * D) + d * nu
  fix (liftd) corner.liftd
  neqfix (liftd) corner.liftd.nolift
  (lct): k, n, N, D, d in NN>=1, theta, x in QQ>0, D = N + d, x * D = n + d * theta / k : x * N * D * k + n * k * d = n * D * k + d * theta * N
  fix (lct) corner.lct
  neqfix (lct) corner.lct.nolift
  origin: ?{\text{the result: the case}\ k_0\ge2\text{, read by \cref{thm:level}(i), (iv) and (v), \cref{fact:zetatop}, \cref{lem:mark} and \cref{fact:acampo}, and the least ratio on the resolution of the tower; the classical cases are cited inside it:}\ k_0=1\ \text{and the germ}\ f_1\ \text{are \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, and}\ k_0=1\ \text{at every}\ d\ \text{is \cite[Theorem~8.2]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}, the plane germs of}\ \Sigma\ \text{are \cite{Loeser1988} as stated in \cite[p.~606]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, and the threshold and the root of}\ b_{f,0}\ \text{are \cite[Proposition~8.5 and Theorem~10.6]{Kollar1997}}}

-- For \(k_0\ge2\), by \cref{thm:level}(iv) a pole is \(-1\), whose root \(1\) is that of the cohomology of degree
-- \(0\), or \(-3/N\), or \(s_S\), or \(s_0=-(3+d\theta/k_0)/D\) for a pole \(-\theta\) of the germ of \(h\) at a
-- singular point \(P\) of \(\Sigma\) or of its layers, with \(0<\theta\le1\): by \cref{fact:zetatop} on the minimal
-- embedded resolution of the germ, \(\theta=\nu_i/N_i\) for one of its parts; a branch of the strict transform has
-- \(\nu_i=N_i=1\), as \(h\) has no square factors; the first blowing-up, if there is one, has \(\nu=2\) and \(N\) the
-- multiplicity of \(P\), at least \(2\); and each later centre lies on the strict transform, as the exceptional curves
-- cross normally, and on one or two exceptional curves, so that the new \(\nu\) is the sum of their \(\nu\), or that
-- plus \(1\), and the new \(N\) is the sum of their \(N\) plus the multiplicity of the strict transform at the centre,
-- at least \(1\); so \(\nu_i\le N_i\) for every part, and \(\theta=1\) gives \(s_S\). At a lift \(s_0\) of a pole
-- \(-\theta\) of \(Z_{top,P}(h)\) with \(o(s_0)\nmid N\) and \(\theta<1\), the conjecture for plane curves of
-- \cite{Loeser1988}, as stated in \cite[p.~606]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, gives \(m_P(o(\theta))\ge1\), and
-- (L2) at \(n=3\) gives \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge1\). At \(s_S\), (L3) gives \(m_f(o(s_S))\ge1\). At \(-3/N\): for \(\delta=1\),
-- \(\Sigma\) is a line, \(W(w)=w/(w-2)\) vanishes at \(0\), so by (L4) it is no pole, unless \(N=3\), where it is
-- \(-1\); for \(\delta=3\) it is \(s_S\), as \(D=3k_0+d\); otherwise \(\delta\nmid3\), and, but for the component set
-- aside, by (L4) it is a pole of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) exactly when \(-3/\delta\) is one of the superisolated \(f_1\), and
-- then \(m_f(o(3/N))\ge m_{f_1}(o(3/\delta))\) by (L4), as \(-\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)=\delta D-\chi(\Sigma)>0\) at
-- \(n=3\), and \(m_{f_1}(o(3/\delta))\ge1\) by \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, as \(o(3/\delta)\ge2\)
-- and the origin is the only singular point of \(f_1=0\). The component set aside has \(\nu_i/N_i=3/\delta\le1\), so
-- \(\delta\ge3\), and \(\delta=3\) is \(s_S\). At \(d=1\) there are no layers, and, as \(Ns_0=-\delta(3k_0+\theta)/D\)
-- and \(\delta(3k_0+\theta)=3(D-1)+\delta\theta\) with \(0<\delta\theta<D\), \(o(s_0)\) divides \(N\) only at
-- \(\delta\theta=3\), where \(s_0=-3/N\); so every pole is one of the cases above.
--
-- The least ratio. The tower is resolved by the blowing-up of the origin, then the product of the line of \(t\) with a
-- log resolution of \(V\), then the toric modifications of the germs of two monomials that these leave. Its divisors
-- over the origin and the strict transform have the ratios \(\nu/N\): \(n/N\) for \(E\); \(1\) for the strict
-- transform; over a divisor of the log resolution of \(V\), of multiplicity \(M_j\) and log discrepancy \(\nu_j\) for
-- \(H\), the rays \((M_j,de_j)/\gcd(M_j,d)\) of the edge \(\sum_jM_ja_j=da_t\), of the ratio
-- \((nM_j+d\nu_j)/(DM_j)=(n+d\theta_j)/D\) with \(\theta_j=\nu_j/M_j\), the lifted components of (liftd), which at
-- \(\theta_j=1/k_0\) are the corners; at the points of \(Z\) the rays \((0,e_j,M_j)\), of the ratio \(1+\theta_j>1\);
-- and divisors of multiplicity \(0\), which do not count. On each cone the multiplicity and the log discrepancy are
-- linear, so the least ratio of a cone is at one of its rays. So the log canonical threshold of \(f\) at the origin is
-- the least of \(1\), \(n/N\) and \((n+d\theta_{\min})/D\) \cite[Proposition~8.5]{Kollar1997}, with \(\theta_{\min}\)
-- the least \(\theta_j\), which for \(H=h^{k_0}\) is the least of \(1/k_0\), at the smooth points, and the
-- \(\mathrm{lct}_P(h)/k_0\). By (lct) at \(k=k_0\), with \(\theta=\mathrm{lct}_P(h)\), the lifted ratio \(x\) has
-- \(xNDk_0+nk_0d=nDk_0+d\theta N\), so \(x\ge n/N\) exactly when \(\theta N\ge k_0n\), that is \(\theta\ge n/\delta\),
-- with equality together; and \(1/k_0\ge n/N\) exactly when
-- \(\delta\ge n\), and then \(n/N\le1\). So for \(\delta\ge n\), \(n/N\) is the threshold exactly when every
-- \(\mathrm{lct}_P(h)\ge n/\delta\), and then \(-n/N\) is the largest root of \(b_{f,0}\)
-- \cite[Theorem~10.6]{Kollar1997}; otherwise the lifted ratio of the least \(\mathrm{lct}_P(h)\) is the threshold.
--
-- On the layer, at \(d=1\): the lifted component has \(k_0nN_i+\nu_i-x\,k_0N_iD=\nu_i-\theta N_i\) at
-- \(x=(n+\theta/k_0)/D\) ((liftd) at \(d=1\)), so its cost is whole exactly when the germ's is, and \(e=o(s_0)\) divides
-- \(k_0N_iD\) exactly when \(e/\gcd(e,k_0D)=o(\theta)\) divides \(N_i\), where \(o(s_0)\nmid k_0D\) as \(\theta\) is not
-- whole. On the layer \(\Phi_e\) of the primitive letter \(u\) of \(s_0\), \(p=1\) and the letters \(T\), \(X_E\) and
-- \(Y\) of \(b_p\) are \(u^{\nu'}\), \(u^{\nu'N}\) and \(u^{\nu'D}\), with \(\nu'\) prime to \(e\) and \(e\) dividing
-- neither \(N\) nor \(D\), so none of them is \(1\) and \(b_p=\frac{(1-T)(1-Y)(1-X_E)}{(1-T)(1-X_E)(1-Y)}=1\) there. So
-- the term \(-b_pZ_\xi(H;Y)\) of (i) gives the value of \(f\) at \(s_0\) on its layer as minus that of the germ at
-- \(-\theta\) on its own, component by component, and (iii) gives \(z_f(e)=-\sum_Pz_{h,P}(o(\theta))\). At \(d\ge2\),
-- (liftd) at \(r=\theta\) gives the cost \(k_0nN_i+d\nu_i-x\,k_0N_iD=d(\nu_i-\theta N_i)\) before the division by
-- \(G_i\), so a cost whole at the bottom is whole at the top, and not conversely. (lct) is the tie \(xD=n+d\theta/k\)
-- times \(Nk\), with \(nk(N+d)=nkD\).
proof cor:levelmonodromy
  (liftd) V[(k * N) * h_1]
  (lct) V[(N * k) * h_2 | (n * k) * h_1]

-- At \(d=1\), call a point of \(V\) special if it lies on two of the hypersurfaces \(h_i=0\) or is a singular point of one,
-- and let \((\mathrm T_0)\) ask that \(g\) vanish at no special point and cut transversally the \(h_i=0\) with
-- \(a_i\ge2\); for \(H=h^{k_0}\) with \(k_0\ge2\) it is \((\mathrm T)\). Then (ii) and (iii) of \cref{thm:level} hold
-- under \((\mathrm T_0)\), which asks less than \((\mathrm T)\); \((\mathrm T_0)\) is a condition on the germ of \(f\); when the
-- special points are finitely many, it holds for every form \(g\) of degree \(D\) off a proper algebraic subset of
-- the space of these forms; and for \(n\ge4\) it forces \(H=h^{k_0}\) with \(\Sigma\) irreducible and with
-- finitely many singular points. Let \(\mathcal P_2\) be the germs in two variables without square factors, and
-- for \(n\ge3\) let \(\mathcal P_n\) be the germs \(f\) with \((\mathrm T_0)\) whose tangent cone is \(h^{k_0}\),
-- \(k_0\ge1\), with \(h\) without square factors and \(\Sigma\) with finitely many singular points, at each of
-- which the germ of \(h\) lies in \(\mathcal P_{n-1}\). On \(\mathcal P_n\), (ii) and (iii) of \cref{thm:level}
-- read \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) and \(\zeta_{f,0}\) level by level down to the plane germs, a branch of the tower of \(f\)
-- having at most \(n-2\) levels above them, and the candidates of \(f\) are \(-1\) and the candidates of the first blowing-up and of the corner of
-- all levels, pushed up by the maps \(\theta\mapsto(n+\theta/k_0)/D\) of (L1), with the poles of the plane germs at
-- the bottom. For \(n=3\) and \(f\) in \(\mathcal P_3\) with \(N\ge2\), every candidate other than \(-1\) is not
-- whole. For \(n=4\), let \(f\) be in \(\mathcal P_4\) with \(k_0\ge2\) and \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\ge1\). Then
-- every pole \(s_0\) of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) with \(o(s_0)\nmid N\) has \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge1\), and if moreover
-- \(\chi(\Sigma)>4\), every pole \(s_0\) has \(o(s_0)=1\) or \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge1\). At every level the lift keeps
-- the costs of the components (tower), so that the root at a lifted pole is read on the layer, read by \cref{lem:mark},
-- of the value of the plane germ at the bottom, as in \cref{cor:levelmonodromy}; this reading is a tie over
-- \(\mathbb Q\), and that the exponent is not zero is a count, which rests on \cite{Loeser1988} and
-- \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002} at the bottom. The tower does not reach a germ whose next
-- form vanishes at a special point, or meets a multiple factor along a tangency, or whose special points form a
-- set of positive dimension, which for \(n\ge4\) takes in every tangent cone with two distinct factors; nor a germ
-- with a singular point of \(\Sigma\) whose germ is not in the class, where one level still holds by (ii) and
-- (iii) and the next does not; and the plane germs are in the class by definition. Where the next form passes
-- transversally through a normal crossing of the tangent cone, which the class excludes for \(n\ge4\), such a
-- point brings no new candidate, at every \(n\) and \(d\). The corollary is stated at \(d=1\): at \(d\ge2\) the lift
-- (liftd) of \cref{cor:levelmonodromy} multiplies the cost of a component by \(d/\gcd(k_0N_i,d)\), so a cost whole at
-- the bottom is whole at the top but not conversely, and the reading level by level is not redone there. The row: a
-- cost carried through two levels, the tie
-- \(\nu''+\theta N_i=x_2N''+\nu_i\) of two positive sums, with \((N'',\nu'')\) the component \((N_i,\nu_i)\) lifted twice
-- and \(-x_2\) the lift of the lift of \(-\theta\) (tower).
corollary cor:towerreach [the reach of the tower]
  (tower): k1, n1, D1, k2, n2, D2, N, nu in NN>=1, r, x1, x2 in QQ>0, x1 * D1 = n1 + r / k1, x2 * D2 = n2 + x1 / k2 : k2 * n2 * (k1 * N * D1) + (k1 * n1 * N + nu) + r * N = x2 * (k2 * (k1 * N * D1) * D2) + nu
  fix (tower) corner.tower
  neqfix (tower) corner.tower.nolift
  origin: ?{\text{the result: the class that the tower reaches and, at}\ n=3\ \text{and}\ n=4\text{, the conjecture on it, read off \cref{thm:level}, \cref{cor:levelmonodromy} and \cref{lem:mark} level by level, with \cite[Theorem~I.7.2]{Hartshorne1977}; the case}\ k_0=1\ \text{is \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}}}

-- On \(h_i=0\) with \(a_i=1\), at a point that is not special, the blowing-up gives \(t^N(u+tW)\) times a unit,
-- and \(u+tW\) is a coordinate whatever \(W\) is, so the components there are those of a point of \(V\setminus Z\); so
-- (ii) and (iii) of \cref{thm:level} hold under \((\mathrm T_0)\), the term of \(Z_i\) in (ii) having the factor
-- \(a_i-1=0\). \((\mathrm T_0)\) is a condition on the germ: a linear change of coordinates moves \(H\) and \(g\)
-- together, and a change \(x\mapsto x+q(x)\) with \(q\) of order at least \(2\) and a unit \(1+\ell+\dots\) with
-- \(\ell\) linear change \(g\) by \(\nabla H\cdot q_2+\ell H\), with \(q_2\) the part of degree \(2\) of \(q\),
-- which vanishes at the special points and along each \(h_i=0\) with \(a_i\ge2\), where \(\nabla H\) and \(H\) lie
-- in the ideal of \(h_i\); so neither the values of \(g\) at the special points nor its restrictions to those
-- hypersurfaces change. Moreover the change of \(g\) lies in the ideal \((h_i)\) when \(a_i\ge2\), and the differential
-- of \(h_ic\), for a polynomial \(c\), is \(c\,dh_i\) on \(h_i=0\), so \(dh_i\wedge dg\) is unchanged at every point of
-- \(h_i=0\), and with it the transversality of \((\mathrm T_0)\). When the special points are finitely many, \(g\)
-- avoids finitely many points, and at a smooth point \(x\) of one \(h_i=0\) the forms whose differential at \(x\)
-- vanishes on the tangent space of the cone of \(h_i=0\) are \(n-1\) independent linear conditions, so those tangent to
-- it somewhere fill a set of dimension one less than that of the forms. For \(n\ge4\), in the projective space of
-- dimension \(n-1\) two
-- hypersurfaces meet along a set of dimension at least \(n-3\ge1\), and a hypersurface meets every closed set of
-- positive dimension \cite[Theorem~I.7.2]{Hartshorne1977}.
--
-- Each level lowers the number of variables by one, so a branch of the tower of \(f\) has at most \(n-2\) levels
-- above the plane germs, and on \(\mathcal P_n\) (ii) and (iii) of \cref{thm:level} read \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) and
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}\) level by level down to them. The components compose: at a point of a component of masses \((N_i,\nu_i)\)
-- of a resolution of the germ of \(h\) at a singular point of \(\Sigma\), that germ is \(v^{N_i}\) times a unit
-- and the form has the factor \(v^{\nu_i-1}\), so the germ \(t^N(h^{k_0}+t)\) of the blowing-up is
-- \(t^N(v^{k_0N_i}+t)\) there, whose corner, the ray \((k_0N_i,1)\) in \((t,v)\), has the multiplicity \(k_0N_iD\)
-- and the log discrepancy \(k_0nN_i+\nu_i\); so \(\theta=\nu_i/N_i\) goes to \((n+\theta/k_0)/D\), the map of (L1),
-- and the candidates of \(f\) are \(-1\) and the candidates of the first blowing-up and of the corner of all levels, pushed up by these maps, with
-- the poles of the plane germs at the bottom. For \(n=3\) and \(N\ge2\), with \(0<\theta\le1\), \(3/N\) is whole
-- only at \(N=3\), where it is \(1\), and \((3k_0+\theta)/(k_0D)\), which is at most \(4/3\), is \(1\) only when
-- \(k_0(k_0\delta-2)=\theta\), that is \(\theta=k_0=1\) and \(\delta=3\). For \(n=4\), by \cref{thm:level}(iv) a pole
-- \(s_0\) with \(o(s_0)\nmid N\) is \(s_S\), the lift of \(-1\), or the lift \(s_0\) of a pole \(-\theta\ne-1\) of
-- the germ of \(h\) at a singular point \(P\) of \(\Sigma\), a germ of \(\mathcal P_3\) with \(N\ge2\), whose
-- candidates are as above; so \(\theta\) is not whole, \(m_P(o(\theta))\ge1\) by \cref{cor:levelmonodromy}, whose case
-- \(k_0=1\), \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, does not ask \(g\) to be transversal, and (L2)
-- gives \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge1\); at \(s_S\), (L3) gives \(m_f(o(s_S))\ge\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\ge1\). A pole with
-- \(o(s_0)\mid N\) and \(o(s_0)\ge2\), such as \(-4/N\), has \(m_f(o(s_0))\ge\chi(\Sigma)-4\) by (iii), whose
-- other terms are at least \(0\) here. Where the next form passes transversally through a normal crossing of the
-- tangent cone, the blown-up germ is a unit times \(t^N(\prod_ju_j^{a_j}+t^dw)\) once \(w\mapsto w+t(\dots)\) has
-- absorbed its terms of higher order, the germ of \(U_J\) in \cref{thm:strata}: its wall rays give no pole, and
-- its poles lie among \(-n/N\), \(-1\) and the corners \(1+a_jc'=0\) of the strata off \(Z\), with \(c'\) as
-- there, so such a point brings no new candidate, at every \(n\) and \(d\). (tower) is (liftd) of
-- \cref{cor:levelmonodromy} at \(d=1\), at two levels: the component lifted once is a component of the germ one level up, whose pole is
-- \(-x_1\), and the lift keeps its cost; so the costs of the plane germ at \(-\theta\) reach every level, a cost is
-- whole at the top exactly when it is at the bottom, and by \cref{lem:mark} the layer of a lifted pole reads the
-- marks of the plane germ, as in the proof of \cref{cor:levelmonodromy}.
proof cor:towerreach
  (tower) with[1 <= k1 * N * D1 by V[(N * D1) * hp(k1), D1 * hp(N), 1 * hp(D1)]; with[1 <= k1 * n1 * N + nu by V[1 * hp(nu)]; with[k1 * n1 * N + 1 * nu + 1 * r * N = x1 * (k1 * N * D1) + 1 * nu by cor:levelmonodromy(liftd)[k := k1, n := n1, N := N, D := D1, d := 1, nu := nu, r := r, x := x1; x1 * D1 = n1 + 1 * r / k1 by V[1 * h_1]]; with[k2 * n2 * (k1 * N * D1) + 1 * (k1 * n1 * N + nu) + 1 * x1 * (k1 * N * D1) = x2 * (k2 * (k1 * N * D1) * D2) + 1 * (k1 * n1 * N + nu) by cor:levelmonodromy(liftd)[k := k2, n := n2, N := k1 * N * D1, D := D2, d := 1, nu := k1 * n1 * N + nu, r := x1, x := x2; x2 * D2 = n2 + 1 * x1 / k2 by V[1 * h_2]]; V[1 * hw3, 1 * hw4]]]]]

-- At a point where the next form crosses the divisors of a log resolution of the tangent cone transversally, the
-- blowing-up of the origin gives a germ of two monomials, one of the divisors through the point and one of the next
-- form, whose fan on the Newton polyhedron has two vertices and one edge. Off the next form the term of the point is the
-- zeta function of a suspension. On it, read over the coordinates other than that of the next form, the edge is B1 for
-- that coordinate, and the cones through the wall rays, which the vertex \(A\) carries whole, the vertex \(B\) and the
-- edge take back in the proportions \(1/(1+s)\) and \(s/(1+s)\), by \cref{lem:cone}(apex), so that they cancel cone by
-- cone; the rest is a staircase, the tie \(y=\nu+Mc'\) of the cost of a divisor carried along the divisors. This holds at
-- every \(d\) and at every crossing, also where the next form passes through the crossing, which \((\mathrm T_0)\)
-- excludes for \(n\ge4\) (\cref{cor:towerreach}), and so, for a generic next form, at every point of a log resolution.

-- For counts \(n\), \(N\), \(d\) and, over a nonempty ordered finite set \(J\), counts \(M_j\) and \(\nu_j\), put
-- \(\sigma_E=n+Ns\) and \(c'=(n+(N+d)s)/d\), so that \(dc'=\sigma_E+ds\), and \(y_j=\nu_j+M_jc'\),
-- \(z_j=\nu_j+M_j(1+s)\), \(\varpi=\gcd(d,M_J)\), \(P_c=\prod_j1/y_j\) and the staircase
-- \(S_J=\sum_jM_j/(\prod_{i\le j}y_i\prod_{i\ge j}\nu_i)\).
-- Let \(T_J\) and \(U_J\) be the local topological zeta functions at the origin of \(t^N(\prod_jv_j^{M_j}+t^d)\) and
-- of \(t^N(\prod_jv_j^{M_j}+t^dw)\), for the forms \(t^{n-1}\prod_jv_j^{\nu_j-1}\,dt\wedge dv\) and
-- \(t^{n-1}\prod_jv_j^{\nu_j-1}\,dt\wedge dv\wedge dw\). Then
-- \(T_J+\frac s{1+s}\frac{\varpi^2}dP_c=\frac{P_c}{\sigma_E}+\frac{S_J}d\)
-- \cite[Theorem~5.6, (Z1)]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}. There (Z1) is stated for the germ
-- \(z^m(z^k+f)\) with \(r=((m+k)s+\nu_z)/k\); at \(z=t\), \(m=N\), \(k=d\), \(\nu_z=n\) and \(f=\prod_jv_j^{M_j}\), \(r\) is
-- \(c'\), \(Z_{top}(f,\omega,r)_0\) is \(P_c\), the layer \(Z^{(e)}\) of an order \(e\ne1\) dividing \(d\) is \(P_c\)
-- when \(e\) divides \(\varpi\) and \(0\) otherwise, and \(\sum_{e\mid \varpi}J_2(e)=\varpi^2\) for the totient \(J_2\) of Jordan;
-- the two forms agree by the tie \(r(r-s)+(1+s)(r+1)=(1+s)+r(r+1)\), where \(r-s=\sigma_E/d\).
cit fact:suspension [the zeta function of a suspension at a monomial germ]
  doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2604.24523
  stmt: Theorem 5.6, (Z1)
  origin: ?{\text{cited: \cite[Theorem~5.6, (Z1)]{ArtalGonzalezPerezGonzalezVillaLeonCardenal2026}, in its arXiv version 2604.24523v1, read at the monomial germ with}\ z=t,\ m=N,\ k=d,\ \nu_z=n\ \text{and}\ f=\prod_jv_j^{M_j}\text{, where}\ r=c'\ \text{and}\ Z_{top}(f,\omega,r)_0=P_c\text{, and with}\ \sum_{e\mid \varpi}J_2(e)=\varpi^2}

-- Let \(n\ge2\), \(N\ge1\) and \(d\ge1\) be counts and \(f=H+g+f'\) a polynomial in \(n\) variables with rational
-- coefficients, where \(H=\prod_ih_i^{a_i}\) is a form of degree \(N\), with distinct irreducible forms \(h_i\) and
-- counts \(a_i\), \(g\) is a form of degree \(N+d\), and every term of \(f'\) has degree at least \(N+d+1\). Put
-- \(V=\{H=0\}\) in the projective space of dimension \(n-1\) and \(Z=V\cap\{g=0\}\), and take \(\sigma_E\), \(c'\),
-- \(y_j\), \(\varpi\), \(P_c\), \(S_J\), \(T_J\) and \(U_J\) as in \cref{fact:suspension}. Then:
-- (i) \(\prod_j1/\nu_j=P_c+c'S_J\), \(U_J=\frac{P_c}{\sigma_E}+\frac{S_J}{(1+s)d}\), and
-- \(U_J+\frac s{1+s}\frac{S_J}d=T_J+\frac s{1+s}\frac{\varpi^2}dP_c\); the costs \(z_j\) of the wall rays \((0,e_j,M_j)\)
-- of the fan of the germ of \(U_J\) do not occur in \(U_J\), so they give no pole; and \(S_J/P_c\), which is
-- \(\big(\prod_j(1+M_jc'/\nu_j)-1\big)/c'\), is \(M_j/\nu_j\) at one divisor and, for \(|J|\ge2\), a polynomial in
-- \(c'\) of degree \(|J|-1\) with positive coefficients, so that \(U_J=T_J\) exactly when \(|J|=1\) and
-- \(M_j=\nu_j\varpi^2\);
-- (ii) let \(\rho\) be a log resolution of the pair of the projective space of dimension \(n-1\) and \(V\), with the
-- strata \(D_J^\circ\) of its divisors, the points on the divisors of \(J\) and on no other, and let
-- \(\rho^{-1}\{g=0\}\) be smooth and transversal to every \(D_J^\circ\), which holds for every \(g\) off a proper
-- algebraic subset of the forms of degree \(N+d\); for a germ \(\psi\) of \(H\) at a point put \(\Theta_d(\psi)\) as in
-- \cref{thm:level} and \(\Omega_d(\psi)=\frac{Z_{top}(\psi;c')}{\sigma_E}+\frac{1-Z_{top}(\psi;c')}{(1+s)dc'}\); then
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)=\frac{n-\chi(V)}{\sigma_E}+\sum_{\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S)\,\Theta_d(H_{\mathcal
-- S})+\sum_{\mathcal S'}\chi(\mathcal S')\,\Omega_d(H_{\mathcal S'})\), the sums over the strata \(\mathcal S\) of
-- \(V\setminus Z\) and \(\mathcal S'\) of \(Z\) on which the germ of \(H\) has one type, and
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=(1-t^N)^{n-\chi(V)}\prod_{\mathcal S}\big((1-t^D)/\zeta^{(d)}_{H,\mathcal S}(t^D)\big)^{\chi(\mathcal
-- S)}\), with \(D=N+d\) and \(\zeta^{(d)}_{H,\mathcal S}\) as in \cref{thm:level}(iii); where the hypersurfaces
-- \(h_i=0\) are smooth and cross normally, \(\rho\) is the identity, \(D_J^\circ\) is the set of the points on
-- \(h_j=0\) for \(j\in J\) and on no other, and the terms of a point of \(D_J^\circ\) are \(T_J\) off \(Z\) and \(U_J\)
-- on \(Z\), at \(M_j=a_j\) and \(\nu_j=1\); and at a prime \(p\) not dividing \(d\) at which \(\rho\), with
-- \(\rho^{-1}V\) and \(\rho^{-1}\{g=0\}\), has good reduction \cite[3.2]{Denef1991}, \(Z_0(f)\) is
-- \((1-p^{-1})p^{1-n}\#(\mathbb P^{n-1}\setminus V)\frac{X_E}{1-X_E}\) plus the terms \(\Phi_\xi\) of
-- \cref{thm:level}(i) at the points \(\xi\) of \(V\setminus Z\) over \(\mathbb F_p\) and, at each point of the
-- reduction of \(\rho^{-1}(Z)\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), the sum over the shells \(a\ge1\) of the \(t\) of
-- \(p^{-na}T^{Na}(1-p^{-1})p^{-1}\) times the sum over the orders \(m\le da\) of \(\prod_jv_j^{M_j}\) of their volumes
-- times \(T^m\), and \((1-F)T^{da}\) times the volume of the orders \(m>da\), the volumes taken in the residue polydisc
-- of the point on \(\rho\), off the coordinate of \(\rho^{-1}\{g=0\}\), for the pulled back form; in the coordinates of
-- the projective space this holds where \(H\) is a product along the stratum.
-- The row: a divisor added to the staircase, the tie \(y=x+Mc\) carried along (stair).
theorem thm:strata [the law of strata]
  (stair): X, Y, S, x, y, M, c in QQ>0, 1 / X = 1 / Y + c * S, y = x + M * c : 1 / (X * x) = 1 / (Y * y) + c * (S / x + M / (Y * x * y))
  fix (stair) corner.stair
  neqfix (stair) corner.stair.notie
  origin: ?{\text{the result: the law of strata at every}\ d\ \text{and at every crossing of the divisors of a log resolution of the tangent cone, read over the coordinates other than that of the next form, with the staircase (stair) and \cref{lem:cone}(apex); the classical cases are cited inside it: off}\ Z\ \text{the term of a point is \cref{fact:suspension}, the formula on the Newton polyhedron is \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992}, read by \cref{fact:zetatop} on a toroidal resolution \cite{KempfKnudsenMumfordSaintDonat1973}, the points are summed by the principle of strata \cite[1.2 and Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, the generic}\ g\ \text{is the theorem of Bertini \cite[Corollary~III.10.9]{Hartshorne1977}, and the good reduction is \cite[3.2]{Denef1991}; the row is proved in Lean, and (i) and (ii) are proved in the text from it and from \cref{lem:cone}(apex)}}

-- \textit{(i).} The germ \(t^N(\prod_jv_j^{M_j}+t^dw)\) has the two monomials \(A=(N,M_J,0)\) and \(B=(N+d,0,1)\) in
-- the orders of \((t,v_J,w)\), and it is nondegenerate, as its derivative in \(w\) is a monomial. A toroidal
-- resolution subordinate to its Newton polyhedron \cite{KempfKnudsenMumfordSaintDonat1973}, read by
-- \cref{fact:zetatop} with the form, gives the formula of \cite[Theorem~5.3(ii)]{DenefLoeser1992} with the cost
-- \(\ell_\tau(a)=na_t+\sum_j\nu_ja_j+a_w+s\langle a,\tau\rangle\) of a ray \(a\) on the normal cone of a face \(\tau\):
-- \(U_J\) is the sum of the terms of the normal cones of \(A\) and \(B\) less \(\frac s{1+s}\) times that of the edge
-- \(AB\), of lattice length \(1\), where the term of a cone is the sum, over the simplicial cones of a subdivision, of
-- the index of the cone over the product of the costs of its primitive rays, and the terms of two cones that meet
-- along a face add up. Read it over the coordinates \((a_t,a_J)\) other than \(a_w\), with \(u=\sum_jM_ja_j\): over
-- the cone \(u\le da_t\) only \(A\) is least, for every \(a_w\), and over the cone \(K\) of \(da_t\le u\) the line of
-- \(a_w\) is cut at the wall \(a_w=u-da_t\), above which \(A\) is least, on which the edge is, and below which \(B\) is.
-- Over the first, the normal cone of \(A\) is simplicial with the rays \(e_t\), \((M_j,de_j)/g_j\) for
-- \(g_j=\gcd(M_j,d)\), and \(e_w\), of the costs \(\sigma_E\), \(dy_j/g_j\) and \(1\), as \(dc'=\sigma_E+ds\), and of
-- the index \(\prod_jd/g_j\), so its term is \(P_c/\sigma_E\). Over \(K\) the edge is B1 for \(w\): its apex \(B\) has
-- \(w\) of weight \(1\), its base \(A\) is free of \(w\), and \(\ell_B-\ell_A=s(da_t-u+a_w)\) is \(0\) on the normal
-- cone of the edge and \(s\) on \(e_w\). So by \cref{lem:cone}(apex), a cone \(C'\) of the normal cone of the edge, of
-- index \(\mu\) and with the product \(X\) of the costs of its rays, spans with \(e_w\) a cone of the term \(\mu/X\) at
-- \(A\), where \(e_w\) costs \(1\), and \(\mu/(VX)\) at \(B\), where it costs \(V=1+s\), and the edge takes back
-- \(s\mu/(VX)\): these terms cancel, cone by cone, and the costs \(z_j\) of the wall rays, which are among those of
-- the rays of \(C'\), never enter. What is left of \(B\) is the term of the cone \(K\oplus e_w\) at \(\ell_B\), the
-- term of \(K\) at \(\ell_B\) over \(1+s\), the cost of \(e_w\) there. The orthant of \((a_t,a_J)\) is \(K\) and the
-- cone \(u\le da_t\), which meet along a face, and at \(\ell_B=dc'a_t+\sum_j\nu_ja_j\) their terms are
-- \(1/(dc'\prod_j\nu_j)\) and, with the rays above, \(P_c/(dc')\); so the term of \(K\) is
-- \(\big(\prod_j1/\nu_j-P_c\big)/(dc')=S_J/d\), by the first clause. So \(U_J=P_c/\sigma_E+S_J/((1+s)d)\). The first
-- clause: at one divisor \(1/\nu_1=1/y_1+c'M_1/(\nu_1y_1)\) is the tie \(y_1=\nu_1+M_1c'\) over \(\nu_1y_1\), and
-- (stair) at \(c=c'\), \(x=\nu_j\), \(y=y_j\), \(M=M_j\), \(X=\prod_{i<j}\nu_i\), \(Y=\prod_{i<j}y_i\) and \(S\) the
-- staircase of the \(i<j\) adds the divisors one by one; so \(\prod_j1/\nu_j=P_c+c'S_J\), and
-- \(S_J/P_c=\big(\prod_jy_j/\nu_j-1\big)/c'\) with \(y_j/\nu_j=1+M_jc'/\nu_j\), whose term of degree \(|J|-1\) in
-- \(c'\) is \(\prod_jM_j/\nu_j\). The relation of \(U_J\) and \(T_J\) is \cref{fact:suspension} with this form of
-- \(U_J\).
--
-- \textit{(ii).} The base point free system of the forms of degree \(N+d\), pulled back by \(\rho\), cuts every closed
-- stratum of \(\rho\) in a smooth hypersurface for a generic \(g\) \cite[Corollary~III.10.9]{Hartshorne1977}, so
-- \(\rho^{-1}\{g=0\}\) is smooth and transversal to every \(D_J^\circ\) off a proper algebraic subset of the forms. By
-- the principle of strata \cite[1.2 and Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}, as in the proof of
-- \cref{thm:level}(ii), \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) is \((n-\chi(V))/\sigma_E\) plus the sum over the strata of \(E\cap V\) of
-- their Euler characteristics times the term of a point. Off \(Z\) the term of a point \(\xi\) is \(\Theta_d(H_\xi)\),
-- by the proof of \cref{thm:level}(ii), which asks nothing of the germ. At a point of \(\rho^{-1}(\xi)\), for \(\xi\)
-- in \(Z\), on \(D_J^\circ\cap\rho^{-1}\{g=0\}\), the blown-up \(f\) is, on \(\rho\),
-- \(t^N\big(\varepsilon\prod_{j\in J}v_j^{M_j}+t^d(g+tW)\big)\), with \(\varepsilon\) a unit, the \(v_j\) among the
-- coordinates and \((M_j,\nu_j)\) the multiplicity and the log discrepancy of the divisor \(j\) for \(H\), and
-- \(w=(g+tW)/\varepsilon\) is a coordinate with the \(v_j\), as \(\rho^{-1}\{g=0\}\) is transversal to \(D_J^\circ\);
-- so the germ is a unit times that of \(U_J\), for the form \(t^{n-1}\prod_jv_j^{\nu_j-1}dt\wedge dv\wedge dw\), and by
-- (i) its term is \(P_c/\sigma_E+(\prod_j1/\nu_j-P_c)/((1+s)dc')\). Summed over \(\rho^{-1}(\xi)\) with the Euler
-- characteristics of the strata, the \(P_c\) give \(Z_{top}(H_\xi;c')\) and the \(\prod_j1/\nu_j\) give
-- \(Z_{top}(H_\xi;0)=1\), by \cref{fact:zetatop} on \(\rho\) and
-- \cite[Remark~1.10]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}; this is \(\Omega_d(H_\xi)\). The zeta function of the
-- monodromy is read as in the proof of \cref{thm:level}(iii): off \(Z\) the factor of a point is
-- \((1-t^D)/\zeta^{(d)}_{H_\xi}(t^D)\), and on \(Z\), at a point of \(\rho^{-1}(\xi)\), the germ is a unit times that
-- of \(U_J\), whose toric resolution has over the point only parts of the Euler characteristic \(0\), so the factor is
-- \(1\). Where the \(h_i=0\) are smooth and cross normally, the germ of \(H\) at a point of \(D_J^\circ\) is
-- \(\prod_{j\in J}v_j^{a_j}\) times a unit, of \(Z_{top}=P_c\) and layers \([e\mid a_J]P_c\) at \(\nu_j=1\), so that
-- \(\Theta_d\) of it is \(T_J\), by \cref{fact:suspension} and \(1-P_c=c'S_J\), and \(\Omega_d\) of it is \(U_J\). At a
-- good prime with \(p\nmid d\), \(\rho\) and \(g\) reduce with the same multiplicities and normal crossings, and \(w\)
-- is a coordinate over \(\mathbb Z_p\) at the points of the reduction of \(\rho^{-1}(Z)\); there, as at a point of kind 2
-- in the proof of \cref{thm:level}(i), for fixed \(t\) and \(v\) the integral over \(w\) is \(p^{-1}T^m\) for the order
-- \(m\le da\) of \(\prod_jv_j^{M_j}\), and \(p^{-1}T^{da}(1-F)\) for \(m>da\), by the translation of \(w\) and
-- \cref{lem:cone}(qapex); off \(Z\) the terms are those of \cref{thm:level}(i), which ask nothing of the germ. Where
-- \(H\) is a product along the stratum, the slice and \(w\) are coordinates of the projective space.
--
-- For \(H=h^{k_0}\) with \(\Sigma\) smooth, every stratum has one divisor, \(M=k_0\), \(\varpi=\gamma\) and
-- \(P_c=d/(\gamma\sigma_S)\), so \(T_J\) and \(U_J\) are the coefficients of \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z)\) and \(\chi(Z)\)
-- in \cref{thm:corner}(i), and the residue at \(s_S\) has the sign of \cref{thm:corner}(ii), set by \(\kappa\) against
-- \(\gamma\) and by the side of \(-1\), not one sign for \(d\ge2\).
proof thm:strata
  (stair) V[(1 / x) * h_1, (1 / (Y * x * y)) * h_2]

-- Let \(f\), \(V\), \(Z\), \(\rho\) and the generic \(g\) be as in \cref{thm:strata}(ii), and let \(s_0\) be a pole of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) other than \(-1\), \(-n/N\) and the corners \(-(n+d/a_i)/D\). By (ii) and the reading of the poles
-- of \cref{thm:level}(iv), \(s_0=-(n+d\theta)/D\) for a pole \(-\theta\) of \(Z_{top}\) or of a layer of the germ
-- \(H_{\mathcal S}\) of \(H\) at a singular stratum \(\mathcal S\) of \(V\); put \(e=o(s_0)\). Assume: (A) \(e\)
-- divides none of \(N\), \(D\) and the multiplicities \(a_iD/g_i\) of the corners; (B) \(-\theta\) is a pole of
-- \(Z_{top}(H_{\mathcal S})\) for some \(\mathcal S\), not only of a layer, and the root of unity of \(\theta\) is a
-- root of the characteristic polynomial of the monodromy of \(H_{\mathcal S}\), as the conjecture for the slice germ
-- asks when it has an isolated critical point and \(\theta\) is not whole; (C) the germs \(H_{\mathcal S}\) at the
-- singular strata have isolated critical points, \((-1)^{\dim\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S\setminus Z)\ge0\) for each of
-- them, and \((-1)^{\dim\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S\setminus Z)>0\) for a stratum of (B); (G) \(g\) is generic as in
-- \cref{thm:strata}(ii); and, for the poles of \(Z_0\), (P) the good reduction of \cref{thm:strata}(ii) and \(p\nmid
-- d\). Then \(m_f(e)\ge1\). A point stratum satisfies (C) when it is off \(Z\), and so does a curve \(C\) once \(\deg
-- C\cdot D>\chi(C^\circ)\), for \(C^\circ\) the stratum, as \(C\) meets \(\{g=0\}\) in \(\deg C\cdot D\) points for a
-- generic \(g\). The poles \(-1\), \(-n/N\) and the corners are read on the counts of the blowing-up and of the corner,
-- as in (L3) and (L4) of \cref{thm:level}, and a pole that comes from a layer alone is not reached. For \(n=3\) and
-- \(H=h^{k_0}\) this is \cref{cor:levelmonodromy}, where (A) reduces to \(e\nmid N\), as the middle term of \(m_f\) is at
-- least \(0\) there. For \(n=4\), it applies where the singular locus of \(V\) is finitely many points with isolated
-- surface germs that satisfy the conjecture, by \cite{LemahieuVanProeyen2011} for the non-degenerate ones and
-- \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002} for the superisolated ones, together with smooth closed curves
-- \(C_j\) along which \(V\) is locally the product of \(C_j\) with a fixed plane germ, covered by \cite{Loeser1988}: then
-- (C) holds, as \(D\ge3>\chi(C_j^\circ)\), and the root is there at every lifted pole with (A); for example
-- \(H=x^{a_1}y^{a_2}(x+y)^{a_3}\) in four variables, three planes through a line. Pinch points and the other points
-- whose germ has no isolated critical point are outside (C).
--
-- The reading. By the proof of \cref{thm:strata}(ii), \(\zeta_{f,0}\) is \((1-t^N)^{n-\chi(V)}\) times the factors
-- \(\big((1-t^D)/\zeta^{(d)}_{H,\mathcal S}(t^D)\big)^{\chi(\mathcal S\setminus Z)}\) of the strata off \(Z\). At a
-- singular stratum \(\mathcal S\) of the codimension \(c_{\mathcal S}=n-1-\dim\mathcal S\), whose slice germ has an
-- isolated critical point by (C), the factor of the cohomology of degree \(c_{\mathcal S}-1\) enters with the power
-- \((-1)^{c_{\mathcal S}}\chi(\mathcal S\setminus Z)\), and the cyclotomic polynomial \(\Phi_e\) enters it
-- \(m_{\mathcal S}(e)\ge0\) times, the number of the roots of order \(e\) of
-- \(\det(1-t^DT^d)\) for its monodromy \(T\) there; the other factors, of \(1-t^N\), \(1-t^D\) and of the cohomology of
-- degree \(0\) of the slices, whose roots have orders dividing \(N\), \(D\) or a multiplicity \(a_iD/g_i\), lack
-- \(\Phi_e\) by (A). So \(\Phi_e\) enters \(\zeta_{f,0}\) with the exponent \(\sum_{\mathcal S}(-1)^{c_{\mathcal
-- S}}\chi(\mathcal S\setminus Z)m_{\mathcal S}(e)\). As \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)/(1-t)\) is the characteristic polynomial of the
-- monodromy on the cohomology of degree \(n-1\) to the power \((-1)^{n-1}\), \(m_f(e)\) for \(e\ge2\) is
-- \((-1)^{n-1}\) times that exponent, and \((-1)^{n-1}(-1)^{c_{\mathcal S}}=(-1)^{\dim\mathcal S}\), as
-- \(c_{\mathcal S}=n-1-\dim\mathcal S\); so \(m_f(e)=\sum_{\mathcal S}(-1)^{\dim\mathcal S}\chi(\mathcal S\setminus
-- Z)m_{\mathcal S}(e)\). By (C) every term is at least \(0\), and by (B), as the root of unity \(\nu_0\) of \(-s_0\) has
-- \(\nu_0^D\) equal to the \(d\)-th power of the root of \(\theta\), \(m_{\mathcal S}(e)\ge1\) for the stratum of (B),
-- whose term is then positive. So \(m_f(e)\ge1\).
remark rem:reduction [the conjecture over one level, reduced to the strata]
  origin: ?{\text{a reading of \cref{thm:strata}(ii) and \cref{thm:level}(iii) and (iv) on the factors of the zeta function of the monodromy, with the conjecture for the slice germs as a hypothesis; the sources for the slices are \cite{Loeser1988}, \cite{LemahieuVanProeyen2011} and \cite[Theorem~3.1]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002}}}

subsection sec:contact Contacts and nodes of the base

-- When \(g=0\) touches the smooth base \(\Sigma\) of the corner, or passes through a node of it, the point brings one
-- new component, whose cost is a sum, with counts as coefficients, of \(c_a\), of the cost \(1+s\) of the strict transform
-- and of \(1\): \(\sigma_w=m(k_0-1)c_a+m(1+s)+1\) at a contact of order \(m\), and
-- \(\sigma_v=((m+1)k_0-m)c_a+m(1+s)+1\) at a node where \(g=0\) has a contact of order \(m\) with one branch. The fan
-- of the point sums to the term of a transversal point less one quotient at a contact (contactd), and plus one quotient
-- at a node (node). At every \(d\) prime to \(k_0\) a contact keeps this form when the next term \(t^{N+d+1}\) of the next
-- form vanishes at the point, or \(d\le k_0-1\), with two gcds in the quotient; when \(d\ge k_0\) and that term does not
-- vanish, it is a vertex, and the new component is the lift of the corner of that vertex and of \(u^{k_0}\), which is
-- itself no component (detached). At \(d=1\) and \(s_S\) the new component costs \(1+m\beta\), respectively \(m\beta\), and each point enters the
-- residue with the inverse of that cost, times \(m+1\) at a node. Over a nodal base at \(n=3\) the terms add up stratum
-- by stratum, and the residue at the candidate \(-3/N\) of the first blowing-up does not see \(g\). Over \(\mathbb F_p\) the quotient of a contact
-- keeps its form, each count and each cost read as a sum of powers of one letter, and at \(k_0=2\) the quadratic
-- character of the tangent cone adds one term on the same new component.

-- Let \(n\ge3\), \(k_0\ge2\), \(\delta\ge1\) and \(d\ge1\) be counts with \(\gcd(d,k_0)=1\), and \(f=h^{k_0}+g+f'\) as in
-- \cref{thm:level}, with \(h\) of degree \(\delta\), so that \(N=k_0\delta\) and \(D=N+d\); put \(\Sigma=\{h=0\}\),
-- \(Z=\Sigma\cap\{g=0\}\), \(c'\) as in \cref{thm:level}, \(\sigma_S=d(1+k_0c')=nk_0+d+k_0Ds\), \(N_S=k_0D\),
-- \(s_S=-(nk_0+d)/N_S\), \(\beta=(\delta-n)/D\), and, at \(d=1\), \(c_a=c'\) and \(M=(k_0-1)D+1\). Let \(\pi\)
-- be the blowing-up of the origin and \(E\) its component; the term of a point \(\xi\) of \(E\) is the local topological
-- zeta function of \(f\circ\pi\) at \(\xi\) for the form \(\pi^*(dx)\), and its zeta function is that of the monodromy
-- of the germ of \(f\circ\pi\) at \(\xi\); so \(\Theta_2=1/(dc')+dB'/\sigma_S\) and
-- \(\Theta_3=\Theta_2-(k_0-1)s/((1+s)\sigma_S)=d/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)+k_0/((1+s)\sigma_S)\), with \(B'\) as in
-- \cref{thm:level}(ii), are the terms of a point of \(\Sigma\setminus Z\) and of a point of \(Z\) in
-- \cref{thm:level}(ii). Let \(A_\star\) be that of \cref{thm:corner}(ii) at \(d=1\). Then:
-- (a) let \(\Sigma\) be smooth and the divisor of \(g\) on \(\Sigma\) be \(\sum_jm_j\mathcal T_j\), with the \(\mathcal T_j\) smooth and
-- disjoint, of union \(Z_{red}\), and at each point of a \(\mathcal T_j\) with \(m=m_j\ge2\) let
-- \(f\circ\pi=t^N\big(u^{k_0}\varepsilon+t^d(uA+y^mU+tC)\big)\) in local coordinates \(t,u,y\) and coordinates along
-- \(\mathcal T_j\) in which \(t=0\) is \(E\), \(u=0\) is the strict transform of \(\{h=0\}\) and \(t=u=y=0\) is \(\mathcal T_j\), with
-- \(\varepsilon\) and \(U\) units, under one of three hypotheses: (M) \(A(0)\ne0\) and \(C(0)=0\), the model; (T)
-- \(d<k_0-1\) and \(A(0)\ne0\), or \(d=k_0-1\) and the roots of \(\varepsilon(0)X^{k_0}+A(0)X+C(0)\) simple, which at
-- \(k_0=2\) is \(A(0)^2\ne4\varepsilon(0)C(0)\); or (Dt) \(d\ge k_0\) and \(C(0)\ne0\), the detached regime; put
-- \(g_1=\gcd(d,k_0-1)\), \(g_2=\gcd((k_0-1)m,d)\), \(M_d=(k_0-1)D+d\) and \(\sigma_w(m)=m((k_0-1)c'+1+s)+1\), whose
-- zero is \(s_w(m)=-\nu_w/N_w\) with \(N_w=mM_d\) and \(\nu_w=m(n(k_0-1)+d)+d\), and, under (Dt), \(\gamma_D=\gcd(k_0,d+1)\),
-- \(g=\gcd(k_0,(d+1)m)\), \(\sigma_{wD}(m)=nk_0m+(d+1)m+k_0+k_0m(D+1)s\), whose zero is
-- \(s_{wD}(m)=-(nk_0m+(d+1)m+k_0)/(k_0m(D+1))\), \(N_{wD}=k_0m(D+1)/g\) and \(N_{AD}=k_0(D+1)/\gamma_D\); then
-- (a1) the term of such a point is, under (M) or (T),
-- \(\Theta(m)=\Theta_3-\frac{s\big((g_1^2m-g_2^2)\sigma_S+d(k_0-1)(m-1)\big)}{(1+s)\sigma_S\,d\sigma_w(m)}\), and
-- under (Dt) \(\Theta_{det}(m)=\Theta_3-\frac{s\big((m\gamma_D^2-g^2)\sigma_S-(m-1)k_0(\sigma_S-k_0+1)\big)}{(1+s)\sigma_S\sigma_{wD}(m)}\),
-- where the corner \((k_0,d+1)\) of the edge of \(u^{k_0}\) and \(t^{d+1}C\) is not a ray of the fan, so that its cost
-- enters no denominator and only the factor of that edge in (a2); \(\Theta(1)=\Theta_{det}(1)=\Theta_3\), and at
-- \(d=1\), \(\Theta(m)=\Theta_3-\frac{(m-1)k_0s(c_a+1)}{(1+s)\sigma_S\sigma_w(m)}\);
-- (a2) its zeta function is \((1-t^{mM_d/g_2})^{g_2}/(1-t^{M_d/g_1})^{g_1}\) under (M) or (T), which is
-- \((1-t^{mM})/(1-t^M)\) at \(d=1\), and under (Dt)
-- \((1-t^{D+1})(1-t^{N_{wD}})^g/\big((1-t^{N_{AD}})^{\gamma_D}(1-t^{m(D+1)})\big)\);
-- (a3) \(Z_{top,0}(f)=\frac{n-\chi(\Sigma)}{\sigma_E}+\chi(\Sigma\setminus
-- Z_{red})\Theta_2+\sum_j\chi(\mathcal T_j)\Theta_j\) and
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=(1-t^N)^{n-\chi(\Sigma)}\big(\frac{1-t^D}{1-t^{N_S}}\big)^{\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red})}\prod_j\zeta_j(t)^{\chi(\mathcal T_j)}\),
-- with \(\Theta_j\) and \(\zeta_j\) the term of (a1) and the factor of (a2) at \(m=m_j\);
-- (a4) at \(d=1\), for \(\delta\ne n\), \(s_S\ne-1\) and \(1+m_j\beta\ne0\) for every \(j\), \(N_S\) times the residue of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) at \(s_S\) is \(A_\star\big(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red})+\sum_j\chi(\mathcal T_j)/(1+m_j\beta)\big)\), and
-- \(1+m\beta\) is the value of \(\sigma_w(m)\) at \(s_S\);
-- (a5) for \(m\ge2\), \(s_w(m)=s_S\) exactly when \(m(n-\delta)=N+d\), and so does \(s_{wD}(m)\) under (Dt); when
-- \(s_w(m)\) is none of \(-n/N\) and \(s_S\), \(N_w\) times the residue of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) at \(s_w(m)\) is
-- \(-\sum_{m_j=m}\chi(\mathcal T_j)\) times the value there of
-- \(\frac{s((g_1^2m-g_2^2)\sigma_S+d(k_0-1)(m-1))}{(1+s)\sigma_S}\), the sum over the \(\mathcal T_j\) of (M) or (T), and
-- under (Dt), when \(s_{wD}(m)\) is none of them, \(k_0m(D+1)\) times the residue at \(s_{wD}(m)\) is
-- \(-\sum_{m_j=m}\chi(\mathcal T_j)\) times the value there of
-- \(\frac{s((m\gamma_D^2-g^2)\sigma_S-(m-1)k_0(\sigma_S-k_0+1))}{(1+s)\sigma_S}\); and at \(d=1\), \(s_w(m)=-n/N\)
-- exactly when \(\delta(m+1)=nm\), \(s_w(m)\ne-1\), \(s_w(m)\) increases with \(m\), the value above is that of
-- \(\frac{(m-1)k_0s(c_a+1)}{(1+s)\sigma_S}\), and the residue at \(s_w(m)\) is zero exactly when
-- \(\sum_{m_j=m}\chi(\mathcal T_j)=0\), or \(k_0=2\) and \(m(n+1)=D\); at \(n=3\), where the \(\mathcal T_j\) are points, \(s_w(m)=s_S\)
-- exactly when \(\delta\le2\) and \(m=D/(3-\delta)\), \(s_w(m)=-3/N\) exactly when \(\delta=m=2\), and every
-- \(s_w(m_j)\) with \(m_j\ge2\) other than \(-3/N\) is a pole of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\), of order two when it is \(s_S\);
-- (b) let \(n=3\) and \(d=1\), and at a node of \(\Sigma\) through which \(g=0\) passes, smooth there, with a contact of order
-- \(m\ge1\) with one branch and transversal to the other, let
-- \(f\circ\pi=t^N\big((y_1y_2)^{k_0}\varepsilon+t(ay_1+by_2^m+tC)\big)\) in local coordinates \(t,y_1,y_2\) in which
-- \(t=0\) is \(E\) and \(y_1y_2=0\) is the strict transform of \(\{h=0\}\), with \(\varepsilon\), \(a\) and \(b\) units
-- and \(C\) a power series; put \(\lambda_m=(m+1)k_0-m\) and \(\sigma_v(m)=\lambda_mc_a+m(1+s)+1\), whose zero is
-- \(s_v(m)=-\nu_v(m)/N_v(m)\) with \(N_v(m)=\lambda_mD+m\) and \(\nu_v(m)=3\lambda_m+m+1\); then the term of the point
-- is \(\Theta_{node}(m)=\Theta_3+\frac{k_0s(\sigma_E-m-1)(c_a+1)}{(1+s)\sigma_E\sigma_S\sigma_v(m)}\), its zeta
-- function is \(1-t^{N_v(m)}\), and for \(\delta\ne3\), \(N_S\) times the residue of \(\Theta_{node}(m)\) at \(s_S\) is
-- \(\frac{m+1}{m\beta}A_\star\), as \(\sigma_v(m)=(m+1)\sigma_S-m\sigma_E\) is \(m\beta\) there;
-- (c) let \(n=3\), \(d=1\) and \(\Sigma\) be nodal, with \(r\) nodes, \(r_0\) of them off \(g=0\); let \(g=0\) meet the smooth
-- points of \(\Sigma\) at points \(q_j\) with contacts of orders \(m_j\ge1\), with the local form of (a) where
-- \(m_j\ge2\), and pass through each other node \(P\) as in (b), with a contact of order \(m_P\); and let
-- \(\Sigma^\circ\) be \(\Sigma\) less its nodes and the \(q_j\); then
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)=\frac{3-\chi(\Sigma)}{\sigma_E}+\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\Theta_2+r_0\Theta_0+\sum_j\Theta(m_j)+\sum_P\Theta_{node}(m_P)\),
-- with \(\Theta_0=\frac1{c_a}+\frac B{\sigma_S^2}\) and \(B\) as in \cref{thm:level}(ii), and
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)=(1-t^N)^{3-\chi(\Sigma)}\big(\frac{1-t^D}{1-t^{k_0D}}\big)^{\chi(\Sigma^\circ)}(1-t^D)^{r_0}\prod_j\frac{1-t^{m_jM}}{1-t^M}\prod_P\big(1-t^{N_v(m_P)}\big)\);
-- for \(\delta\ne3\), \(r_0=0\) and \(1+m_j\beta\ne0\) for every \(j\), \(s_S\) is at most a simple pole of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\), and \(N_S\) times its residue there is
-- \(A_\star\big(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)+\sum_j\frac1{1+m_j\beta}+\sum_P\frac{m_P+1}{m_P\beta}\big)\); and for
-- \(\delta\ne3\), \(N\ne3\) and no \(m_j=\delta=2\), \(-3/N\) is at most a simple pole of \(Z_{top,0}(f)\), and \(N\)
-- times its residue there is
-- \(\eta_\Sigma=3-\chi(\Sigma)+(\chi(\Sigma)-r)\frac\delta{\delta-3}+r\frac{\delta^2}{(\delta-3)^2}\), the value at \(0\)
-- of the function of \cref{thm:level}(iv), whatever the position of \(g=0\);
-- (d) let \(n=3\) and \(d=1\), let \(p\) be a prime at which \(f\) has \(p\)-integral coefficients, \(\Sigma\) is smooth modulo
-- \(p\) and \(g=0\) is smooth modulo \(p\) at the points of \(Z\), and let \(\zeta\) be a point of \(Z\) over \(\mathbb
-- F_p\) at which \(g=0\) has modulo \(p\) a contact of order \(m\ge2\) with \(\Sigma\) and the local form of (a) holds
-- over \(\Z_p\), with \(\varepsilon\) and \(U\) units, and \(A\) a unit when \(f'=0\) or \(k_0\ge3\); with \(X_E\),
-- \(X_S\), \(F\) and the term \(\tau\) of a
-- point of \(Z\) of \cref{thm:corner}(i) at \(d=1\), put \(Y=p^{-3}T^D\), \(X_M=p^{-(3k_0-2)}T^M\) and
-- \(X_w=p^{-1}X_M^m=p^{-\nu_w}T^{N_w}\), so that \(X_S=X_MX_E\), and
-- \(\Delta_p(m)=(1-p^{-1})p^{-2}F\,(1-p^{-1}Y)\frac{(1+Y+\dots+Y^{k_0-1})(X_M+X_M^2+\dots+X_M^{m-1})}{(1-X_S)(1-X_w)}\);
-- then the part of \(Z_0(f)\) on the tube of \(\zeta\), the \(x\) of \(p\Z_p^3\) whose class in \(\mathbb P^2(\mathbb
-- F_p)\) is \(\zeta\), is \(\tau-\Delta_p(m)\) when \(f'=0\), and when \(p\nmid m\) and \(k_0\ge3\); it is
-- \(\tau-\Delta_p(m)+(2-r)(1-p^{-1})p^{-2}F\frac{X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}}{1-X_w}\) when \(k_0=2\), \(p\nmid2m\) and
-- \(A^2\ne4\varepsilon C\) at \(\zeta\) modulo \(p\), with \(r\in\{0,2\}\) the number of roots of
-- \(\varepsilon\varphi^2+A\varphi+C\) at \(\zeta\) over \(\mathbb F_p\); in each case its product with
-- \((1-X_E)(1-X_S)(1-X_w)(1-p^{-1}T)\) is a polynomial in \(T\): by \cref{lem:cone}(apex) at a good prime, in its form
-- (qapex), the terms of the cones through the ray \(R'=(0,m,1)\), of the letter \(p^{-(m+1)}T^m\), which is \(X_E\) at
-- \(N=m=2\), add up to terms free of that letter in \(Z_0\), so that neither that ray nor the letter \(X_M\) of the edge
-- of normal \((k_0-1,1)\) in (a2) gives a factor of its denominator; and \(\Delta_p(m)\) is the quotient of (a1) read over \(\mathbb F_p\): each cost
-- \(\sigma=\nu+N's\) among \(c_a+1\), \(\sigma_S\) and \(\sigma_w(m)\) becomes \((1-p^{-\nu}T^{N'})/(1-p^{-1})\),
-- \(\frac s{1+s}\) becomes \(F\), the count \(k_0\) becomes \(1+Y+\dots+Y^{k_0-1}\) and \(m-1\) becomes
-- \(X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}\), with the factor \(p^{-2}\).
-- (e) let \(n=3\), let \(p\) be a prime not dividing \(k_0\) at which \(f\) has \(p\)-integral coefficients,
-- \(\Sigma\) is smooth modulo \(p\) and \(g=0\) is smooth modulo \(p\) at the points of \(Z\), and let \(\zeta\) be a
-- point of \(Z\) over \(\mathbb F_p\) at which the local form of (a) holds over \(\Z_p\), with a contact of order
-- \(m\ge2\); at the tie \(d=k_0-1\), with the roots of \(\varepsilon X^{k_0}+AX+C\) at \(\zeta\) simple and not zero
-- modulo \(p\), the part of \(Z_0(f)\) on the tube of \(\zeta\) is affine in their number \(r\) over \(\mathbb F_p\),
-- with the slope \(-(1-p^{-1})p^{-2}F\frac{X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}}{1-X_w}\), where \(X_M=p^{-4}T^{N+k_0}\) and
-- \(X_w=p^{-1}X_M^m\), so that it is its value at \(r=k_0\) plus \((k_0-r)(1-p^{-1})p^{-2}F\frac{X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}}{1-X_w}\),
-- which at \(k_0=2\) is the term \((2-r)\) of (d); under (Dt) the faces of the polyhedron of the vertices
-- \((N,k_0,0)\), \((N+d+1,0,0)\) and \((N+d,0,m)\) are nondegenerate modulo \(p\), as the edge \(u^{k_0}+Ct^{d+1}\) is
-- degenerate modulo \(p\) exactly when \(p\mid\gamma_D\) and the facet is nondegenerate when \(p\nmid m\), or when
-- \(p\nmid k_0\) and \(p\nmid\gamma_D\), and the part of \(Z_0(f)\) on the tube is the formula of
-- \cite[Theorem~4.2]{DenefHoornaert2001} on it, with the counts \((p-1)^2\) on the two edges through \((N+d,0,m)\),
-- \((p-1)^2r_D\) on the third, for the number \(r_D\) of the \(v\in\mathbb F_p^\times\) with \(v^{\gamma_D}=-C/\varepsilon\)
-- at \(\zeta\), and that of the trinomial on the facet, \((p-1)(p-2)\) when \(g=1\); at \(p=3\) and
-- \((N,k_0,d,m)=(3,3,5,3)\), where \(p\mid k_0\), the edge \(u^3+Ct^6\) is the cube of \(u+ct^2\) modulo \(3\) for
-- \(c^3=C\), and the formula fails.
-- The rows, one clause each: (wallray) in a fan through a ray of cost \(R=mV+1\), \(V=1+s\), between components of
-- costs \(S=kc+1\) and \(W=Lc+R\), the three quotients in \(R\) of its cones tie \(\frac{aL}{RW}\), two quotients free
-- of \(R\) and \(\frac{Ls}{VRW}\), by the flip of the tie \(kW+L=LS+kR\), then \cref{lem:cone}(apex) and (countsplit)
-- at \(R=mV+1\); (contactd) under (M) or (T), at every \(d\), the
-- fan of a contact of order \(m\) sums to \(\Theta_3\) less one quotient, with \(G=g_2^2\) and \(H=g_1^2\); (detached)
-- under (Dt) the fan sums to \(\Theta_3\) less one quotient, with \(G=g^2\) and \(H=\gamma_D^2\); (node) the fan of a node on \(g=0\) sums to
-- \(\Theta_3\) plus one quotient; (qcontact) over \(\mathbb F_p\), the two ties by which the integral over the base at a
-- contact keeps one letter, \(X_M\); (qslope) at the tie over \(\mathbb F_p\), the slope in \(r\) read on the open cone of
-- the edge of the roots and on its facet ray.
theorem thm:contact [contacts and nodes of the base]
  (wallray): a, L, k, m, s, c, V, S, W, R in QQ>0, V = 1 + s, R = m * V + 1, S = k * c + 1, W = L * c + R : a * L / (S * W * R) + a * k / (S * R) + (1 + m) * L / (R * W) = a * L / (R * W) + a * k / (W * S) + L / (V * W) + L * s / (V * R * W)
  fix (wallray) corner.wallray
  neqfix (wallray) corner.wallray.noray
  neqfix (wallray) corner.wallray.nocone
  (contactd): k, m, k1, m1, d in NN>=1, E, s, c, V, S, W, R, G, H in QQ>0, k = k1 + 1, m = m1 + 1, d * c = E + d * s, V = 1 + s, S = k * c + 1, W = m * (k1 * c + V) + 1, R = m * V + 1 : 1 / (E * S * W) + m / (E * W) + m * k1 / (d * R * W * S) + k / (d * R * S) + m^2 * k1 / (d * W * R) + G * s / (d * V * W) + 1 / (d * c * S) + k * s / (d * V * S) + s * (H * m * S + k1 * m1) / (d * V * S * W) = 1 / (d * c) + 1 / (E * S) + s / (d * V * W * S) + H * m * s / (d * V * W) + m * k1 * s / (d * V * R * W) + G * s / (d * V * W)
  fix (contactd) corner.contactd
  neqfix (contactd) corner.contactd.nocell
  (detached): n, N, k, d, m, k1, m1 in NN>=1, s, E, S, W, V, G, H in QQ>0, k = k1 + 1, m = m1 + 1, V = 1 + s, E = n + N * s, S = n * k + d + k * (N + d) * s, W = n * k * m + (d + 1) * m + k + k * m * (N + d + 1) * s : (d + 1) * m / (E * W) + k * d / (E * S * W) + k * m / W + k^2 / (S * W) + G * s / (V * W) + s * (H * m * S + m1 * k * k1) / (V * S * W) = d / (E * S) + k / (V * S) + s * (H * m + k / S + k) / (V * W) + s * (G * S + m1 * k * S) / (V * S * W)
  fix (detached) corner.detached
  neqfix (detached) corner.detached.nocorner
  (qtube): e, sigma, D, k1, m1 in NN>=1, t, SD, Ss, Se, Sa, Sv, SM, SS, Sw, SR, Sad, U, W, F, g, C in QQ>0, (sum i in 1..D, t^i) = SD, (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) = Ss, (sum i in 1..e, t^i) = Se, (sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) = Sa, (sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) = Sv, (sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma, t^i) = SM, (sum i in 1..(k1 + 1) * (e + sigma) + D, t^i) = SS, (sum i in 1..(m1 + 1) * (k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma) + D, t^i) = Sw, (sum i in 1..(m1 + 1) * (D + sigma) + D, t^i) = SR, (sum i in 1..e + sigma + D, t^i) = Sad, (sum j in 1..k1, (t^(e + sigma))^j) = U, (sum j in 1..m1, (t^(k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma))^j) = W, F * Sv = t^D * Ss, Sa * g + Sa * t^((m1 + 1) * (D + sigma) + D) = Sa + t^((m1 + 1) * (D + sigma) + D) * (sum i in 1..(e + sigma) * ((m1 + 1) * k1), t^i), Sa * C + SD^3 * t^(D + sigma) / (Sv * SR) + SD^2 * t^(e + sigma) / SM = SD^2 / Sv + SD^3 * t^(e + sigma) * t^(D + sigma + e * k1 + k1 * sigma) / (SM * Sw) : SD^3 / (Se * SS * Sw) + SD^2 * (1 + t^D * W) / (Se * Sw) + SD^3 * g / (Sw * SR * SS) + SD^2 * (1 + t^D * U) / (SR * SS) + C + 2 * F * SD / Sw + F * SD * (1 + t^D * U) / SS + F * SD * Sad * (1 + U) * t^D * W / (SS * Sw) + t^D * (SD * (1 + t^D * W) / Sw + SD^2 / (Sw * SS) + SD^2 * g / (Sw * SR)) + t^D * t^D * SD / Sw + SD / Sw + t^D * (SD^2 / (Se * Sw) + SD^2 / (SR * SS)) + SD / SS = SD^2 / (Se * SS) + SD * (1 + t^D * U) / SS + t^D * SD / SS + F * (SD * (1 + t^D * W) / Sw + SD^2 / (Sw * SS) + SD^2 * g / (Sw * SR)) + t^D * F * SD / Sw + (SD * (1 + t^D * W) / Sw + SD^2 / (Sw * SS) + SD^2 * g / (Sw * SR)) + 2 * t^D * SD / Sw + SD^2 / (Se * Sw) + SD^2 / (SR * SS)
  fix (qtube) corner.qtube
  neqfix (qtube) corner.qtube.nostair
  neqfix (qtube) corner.qtube.nogamma
  (node): k, m, k1 in NN>=1, E, s, c, V, S, W, R in QQ>0, k = k1 + 1, c = E + s, V = 1 + s, S = k * c + 1, W = (k + k1 * m) * c + m * V + 1, R = m * V + 1 : (m + 1) / (E * S * W) + (k + k1 * m) / (R * W) + k / (W * S) + (k + k1 * m) * m / (S * W * R) + k * m / (S * R) + s / (V * W) + 1 / (c * S) + k * s / (V * S) + k * s * (c + 1) * (m + 1) / (V * E * S * W) = 1 / c + 1 / (E * S) + (m + 1) * s / (V * S * W) + (k + k1 * m) * s / (V * R * W) + k * s * (c + 1) / (V * S * W)
  fix (node) corner.node
  neqfix (node) corner.node.nocell
  (qnode): e, sigma, D, k1, m in NN>=1, t, SD, Ss, Se, Sa, Sv, SM, SS, SV, SR, Sad, U, F, Sg, Rh, Lm, Q in QQ>0, (sum i in 1..D, t^i) = SD, (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) = Ss, (sum i in 1..e, t^i) = Se, (sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) = Sa, (sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) = Sv, (sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma, t^i) = SM, (sum i in 1..(k1 + 1) * (e + sigma) + D, t^i) = SS, (sum i in 1..(k1 + 1) * (e + sigma) + D + m * (k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma), t^i) = SV, (sum i in 1..m * (D + sigma) + D, t^i) = SR, (sum i in 1..e + sigma + D, t^i) = Sad, (sum j in 1..k1, (t^(e + sigma))^j) = U, F * Sv = t^D * Ss, SM * Sg + SM * t^((k1 + 1) * (e + sigma) + D) = SM + t^((k1 + 1) * (e + sigma) + D) * (sum i in 1..(k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma) * m, t^i), Sv * Rh + Sv * t^D = Sv + t^D * (sum i in 1..(D + sigma) * m, t^i), Sa * Lm + Sa * t^(m * (D + sigma) + D) = Sa + t^(m * (D + sigma) + D) * (sum i in 1..(e + sigma) * (k1 * (m + 1) + 1), t^i), Sa * Q + SD^3 * t^(e + sigma) * Sg / (SS * SV) = SD^2 * Rh / SR : SD^3 / (Se * SS * SV) + SD^3 * Sg / (Se * SS * SV) + SD^2 * (1 + t^D * U) / (SS * SV) + SD^2 * Lm / (SV * SR) + Q + 2 * F * SD / SV + F * SD * (1 + t^D * U) / SS + F * SD^2 * Sad * (1 + U) * (Sg + t^(D + e + sigma + e * k1 + k1 * sigma)) / (Se * SS * SV) + t^D * (SD^2 * Sg / (SV * SS) + SD^2 / (SV * SS) + SD^2 * Lm / (SV * SR)) + t^D * t^D * SD / SV + SD / SV + t^D * (SD^2 / (Se * SV) + SD / SV) + SD / SS = SD^2 / (Se * SS) + SD * (1 + t^D * U) / SS + t^D * SD / SS + F * (SD^2 * Sg / (SV * SS) + SD^2 / (SV * SS) + SD^2 * Lm / (SV * SR)) + t^D * F * SD / SV + (SD^2 * Sg / (SV * SS) + SD^2 / (SV * SS) + SD^2 * Lm / (SV * SR)) + 2 * t^D * SD / SV + F * SD * Sad * (1 + U) / (SS * SV) + SD^2 / (Se * SV) + SD / SV
  fix (qnode) corner.qnode
  neqfix (qnode) corner.qnode.noquotient
  neqfix (qnode) corner.qnode.nolambda
  (qcontact): q, X, T, L, e, u, v, w, z, r, a, g, h, b in QQ>0, e + 1 = q, u + T = 1, v + X = 1, w + X * T = 1, z + T = q, r + q * X = 1, a + L = 1, g + L * X = 1, h + q * L * X = 1, b + L = q : e * T / (w * z) + u * (e + w) / (z * v * w) = 1 / v, 1 / e + 1 / a + 1 / r = 1 / g + h * b * v / (e * a * g * r)
  fix (qcontact) corner.qcontact
  neqfix (qcontact) corner.qcontact.noshift
  (qslope): q, e, a, M, X, v in QQ>0, e + 1 = q, a * q = e, v + X = 1 : e^2 * (M + X) / (q * a * v) = e * M / v + e * X / v
  fix (qslope) corner.qslope
  neqfix (qslope) corner.qslope.noframe
  origin: ?{\text{the result, by the mechanism of \cref{thm:corner}: the contacts of every order and the nodes of the base, each a new component whose cost is a sum of the costs of the level with counts as coefficients, and whose fan ties as its quotients do (wallray), (contactd), (detached), (node), and at a prime (qcontact) and (qslope); the tools are standard: a change of coordinate by the implicit function theorem, the Newton polygon of the roots \cite[Section~3]{GarciaBarrosoGonzalezPerezPopescuPampu2019}, the formula of \cite[Theorem~5.3]{DenefLoeser1992} on the toric resolution of a nondegenerate germ \cite{Varchenko1976} as \cref{fact:zetatop}, the formula of \cite{Varchenko1976Zeta}, the principle of strata \cite[1.2 and Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002} and \cite[Corollary~1]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997}, with \cref{thm:level} and \cref{thm:corner}(ii); at a prime the order laws on the tubes, with \cref{thm:corner}(i) and (qstar) of \cref{thm:level}, and the formula of \cite[Theorem~4.2]{DenefHoornaert2001}; the rows are proved in Lean, and (a) to (d) are proved in the text from them; (qtube) and (qnode): in the sums \(S_x=\sum_{i=1}^xt^i\) of one letter \(t\), the tube of a contact of order \(m=m_1+1\) over a component of \(k_0=k_1+1\) steps: with \(S_D,\dots,S_{c_a+D}\) the sums at \(D,\sigma,e,c_a=e+\sigma,D+\sigma,c_M=k_1c_a+D+\sigma,c_S=k_0c_a+D,c_w=mc_M+D,c_R=m(D+\sigma)+D,c_a+D\), \(U=\sum_{j=1}^{k_1}t^{jc_a}\), \(W=\sum_{j=1}^{m_1}t^{jc_M}\), \(F=t^DS_\sigma/S_{D+\sigma}\), the far cone \(g\) by its sum and the staircase \(C\) by its tie, the cones tie at every \(t\), and at \(t=1\) the tie is (contact) in counts (qtube); and the tube of a node, for every \(m\ge1\): with \(c_V=c_S+mc_M\), the box sums \(\sigma_m\), \(\rho_m\) and \(\lambda\) of its cones by their sums at \(mc_M\), \(m(D+\sigma)\) and \((k_1(m+1)+1)c_a\), and the open cone \(Q\) of the vertex by its tie, the cones tie at every \(t\), and at \(t=1\) the tie is (node) in counts (qnode)}}

-- \textit{(a1).} Under (T) put \(u=u'+t\varphi\). The restriction of \(f\circ\pi\) to \(u'=0\) is
-- \(t^{N+d}\big(y^mU+t(C+\varphi A+t^{k_0-d-1}\varphi^{k_0}\varepsilon)\big)\), with \(A\), \(U\), \(C\), \(\varepsilon\)
-- read at \(u=t\varphi\), and the equation \(C+\varphi A+t^{k_0-d-1}\varphi^{k_0}\varepsilon=0\) has a solution
-- \(\varphi\), a power series in \(t\), \(y\) and the coordinates along \(\mathcal T_j\), by the implicit function theorem:
-- for \(d<k_0-1\) as \(A(0)\ne0\), and for \(d=k_0-1\) at a simple root of \(\varepsilon(0)X^{k_0}+A(0)X+C(0)\). The
-- change keeps \(t\), the component \(E\) and the form, the restriction to \(u'=0\) is then \(t^{N+d}y^m\) times a
-- unit, the coefficient of \(u'\) is \(t^d\) times a unit, by the hypothesis on \(A(0)\) for \(d<k_0-1\) and at a simple
-- root for \(d=k_0-1\), and the new terms \(t^{N+i}u'^{k_0-i}\) lie on or above the edge from \((N,k_0,0)\) to
-- \((N+d,1,0)\) as \(d\le k_0-1\). So under (M) and (T), in the letters \(t,u',y\), the Newton polyhedron is that of
-- \(A=(N,k_0,0)\), \(B=(N+d,1,0)\) and \(Y=(N+d,0,m)\), every other monomial lying above its bounded faces, each
-- bounded face polynomial has a variable whose derivative is a monomial on the torus, the germ is nondegenerate, and
-- the coordinates along \(\mathcal T_j\) do not occur in it. The toric modification of a regular refinement of its fan
-- is an embedded resolution near the point \cite{Varchenko1976}, and \cref{fact:zetatop} on it, with the weights
-- \(n,1,1\) of the form \(t^{n-1}dt\,du'\,dy\) and the factor \(1\) of the coordinates along \(\mathcal T_j\), is the
-- formula of \cite[Theorem~5.3(ii)]{DenefLoeser1992}. The rays are \(e_t,e_u,e_y\), \(S=(k_0,d,0)\), primitive as
-- \(\gcd(d,k_0)=1\), \(w=((k_0-1)m,dm,d)/g_2\), normal to the bounded facet, of normalized area \(g_2\), and
-- \(R'=(0,m,1)\), of costs \(\sigma_E\), \(1\), \(1\), \(\sigma_S\), \(d\sigma_w(m)/g_2\) and \(R=m(1+s)+1\); the
-- edge \(AB\) has the length \(g_1\) and the edges \(BY\) and \(AY\) the length \(1\). The cones of the three vertices,
-- \((e_t,S,w)\), \((e_t,e_y,w)\), \((S,R',w)\), \((S,R',e_u)\) and \((e_y,R',w)\), of the indices \(d^2/g_2\),
-- \(dm/g_2\), \(d(k_0-1)m/g_2\), \(k_0\) and \(m^2(k_0-1)/g_2\), give, with \(\sigma_S=dS\) and \(\sigma_w=W\),
-- \(\frac1{\sigma_ESW}+\frac m{\sigma_EW}+\frac{m(k_0-1)}{dRWS}+\frac{k_0}{dRS}+\frac{m^2(k_0-1)}{dWR}\), and the facet
-- and the three bounded edges, of the normal cones \((S,w)\), \((e_y,w)\) and \((R',w)\), of the indices \(d/g_2\),
-- \(mg_1/g_2\) and \((k_0-1)m/g_2\), give \(\frac s{1+s}\frac1d\big(\frac{g_2^2}W-\frac1{SW}-\frac{mg_1^2}W-\frac{m(k_0-1)}{RW}\big)\).
-- (contactd) at \(E=\sigma_E\), \(c=c'\), \(V=1+s\), \(S=\sigma_S/d\), \(W=\sigma_w(m)\), \(R\), \(G=g_2^2\) and
-- \(H=g_1^2\), with \(\Theta_3=\frac1{dc'}+\frac1{\sigma_ES}-\frac1{dc'S}-\frac{k_0s}{d(1+s)S}\), says that this sum is
-- \(\Theta(m)\); by (wallray) at \(a=1\) and \(L=m(k_0-1)\), that is by \cref{lem:cone}(apex), the terms of the cones
-- through the ray \(R'\) add up to terms free of \(R\) in \(Z_{top,0}\). Under (Dt) the Newton polyhedron is that of
-- \(A\), \(D=(N+d+1,0,0)\) and \(Y\): \(B\) lies above it, as \(dk_0>(d+1)(k_0-1)\), and so does every other monomial;
-- the face polynomials \(u^{k_0}+Ct^{d+1}\), \(u^{k_0}+t^dy^m\), \(Ct^{d+1}+t^dy^m\) and their sum have each a variable
-- whose derivative is a monomial on the torus, so the germ is nondegenerate. Its rays are \(e_t,e_u,e_y\), \(S\) and
-- \(w_D=(k_0m,(d+1)m,k_0)/g\), normal to the facet \(ADY\), of normalized area \(g\) and of cost \(\sigma_{wD}(m)/g\); the
-- edge \(AD\) has the length \(\gamma_D\), and \(AY\) and \(DY\) the length \(1\). The corner \((k_0,d+1,0)\) of \(AD\) is
-- not a ray: \(Y\) lies below its plane, as \(k_0(N+d)<k_0(N+d+1)\), and \(w_D\) is its lift. The cones of the vertices,
-- \((e_t,e_y,w_D)\), \((e_t,S,w_D)\), \((e_y,e_u,w_D)\) and \((e_u,S,w_D)\), of the indices \((d+1)m/g\), \(k_0d/g\),
-- \(k_0m/g\) and \(k_0^2/g\), and the edges \(AD\), \(AY\), \(DY\) and the facet, of the normal cones \((e_y,w_D)\),
-- \((S,w_D)\), \((e_u,w_D)\) and the ray \(w_D\), give the fan of (detached) at \(E=\sigma_E\), \(S=\sigma_S\),
-- \(W=\sigma_{wD}(m)\), \(G=g^2\) and \(H=\gamma_D^2\), which says that the sum is \(\Theta_{det}(m)\); there is no ray
-- \(R'\). No change of \(u\) brings back the apex \(t^{N+d}u\) for \(d\ge k_0\): the roots of
-- \(\psi^{k_0}\varepsilon+t^dA\psi+t^{d+1}C\) have the order \((d+1)/k_0>1\) in \(t\), the slope of its Newton polygon
-- \cite[Section~3]{GarciaBarrosoGonzalezPerezPopescuPampu2019}, as the point \((1,d)\) lies above its edge from
-- \((0,d+1)\) to \((k_0,0)\). At \(m=1\) the germ is that of a point of \(Z\). At \(d=1\), \(g_1=g_2=1\), \(\sigma_S=1+k_0c_a\) and
-- \((m-1)\sigma_S+(k_0-1)(m-1)=(m-1)k_0(c_a+1)\).
--
-- \textit{(a2).} By the formula of \cite{Varchenko1976Zeta} on the same polyhedra: under (M) and (T) the bounded edge
-- \(AB\) in the plane \(y=0\) has the normal \((k_0-1,d)/g_1\), the value \(M_d/g_1\) and the length \(g_1\), and gives
-- \((1-t^{M_d/g_1})^{-g_1}\); the facet has the value \(mM_d/g_2\) and the area \(g_2\), and gives
-- \((1-t^{mM_d/g_2})^{g_2}\); the polyhedron meets no other coordinate plane in an edge and no axis. Under (Dt) the
-- vertex \(D\) on the axis of \(t\) gives \(1-t^{D+1}\), the facet \((1-t^{N_{wD}})^g\), the edge \(AD\) in the plane
-- \(y=0\), of normal \((k_0,d+1)/\gamma_D\), \((1-t^{N_{AD}})^{-\gamma_D}\), and the edge \(DY\) in the plane \(u=0\)
-- \((1-t^{m(D+1)})^{-1}\); so the cost of the corner of \(AD\) enters the monodromy and not \(Z_{top,0}\).
--
-- \textit{(a3).} By the principle of strata \cite[1.2 and Example~1.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2002} and by
-- \cite[Corollary~1]{GuseinZadeLuengoMelle1997}, as \(\pi\) is an isomorphism off the origin, summed over
-- \(E\setminus\Sigma\), where the germ is \(t^N\), of term \(1/\sigma_E\) and zeta function \(1-t^N\), over
-- \(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red}\), with the term and the zeta function of \cref{thm:level}(ii) and (iii), and over the
-- \(\mathcal T_j\), on each of which the germ has one polyhedron.
--
-- \textit{(a4).} At \(d=1\) and \(s_S\), \(c_a=-1/k_0\), so \(\sigma_w(m)=m(1/k_0+s_S)+1=1+m\beta\), as \(1/k_0+s_S=(N-nk_0)/N_S=\beta\),
-- and \(k_0(c_a+1)=k_0-1\). By \cref{thm:corner}(ii), \(N_S\) times the residue at \(s_S\) of \(\Theta_2\) is
-- \(A_\star\), and that of \(\Theta_3\) is \(A_Z=1/\alpha_L+1/(\alpha_R\alpha_V)\), with \(\alpha_L=-\beta\),
-- \(\alpha_R=1/k_0\), \(\alpha_V=1+s_S\) and \(\alpha_L+\alpha_R+\alpha_V=1\); so
-- \(A_\star-(1+\beta)A_Z=1/\alpha_R+1/\alpha_V-(1+\beta)/(\alpha_R\alpha_V)=0\) and \(\beta
-- A_Z=-1+k_0\beta/(1+s_S)=(k_0-1)s_S/(1+s_S)\). Then by (a1) \(N_S\) times the residue of \(\Theta(m)\) is
-- \(A_Z-(m-1)\beta A_Z/(1+m\beta)=A_Z(1+\beta)/(1+m\beta)=A_\star/(1+m\beta)\), and \(1/\sigma_E\) has no pole at
-- \(s_S\) for \(\delta\ne n\).
--
-- \textit{(a5).} At every \(d\), \(s_S\), \(s_w(m)\) and \(s_{wD}(m)\) are the zeros of \(\sigma_S\), \(d\sigma_w(m)\) and
-- \(\sigma_{wD}(m)\), and with \(N=k_0\delta\) the numerators of \(s_w(m)-s_S\) and \(s_{wD}(m)-s_S\) are \(dk_0\) and \(k_0\)
-- times \(N+d-m(n-\delta)\); \(\Theta_2\) and \(\Theta_3\) have no pole at \(s_w(m)\) or \(s_{wD}(m)\) off \(-n/N\) and
-- \(s_S\), and the quotient of (a1) has there the residue of its numerator over \((1+s)\sigma_S\) divided by \(N_w\),
-- respectively \(k_0m(D+1)\). At \(d=1\), \(\sigma_w(m)\) at \(s_S\) is \(1+m\beta\); at \(-n/N\), where \(c_a=-n/N\), it is \(m(1-n/\delta)+1\); at
-- \(-1\) it is \(m(k_0-1)(n-N-1)+1\), not zero for \(m\ge2\); and \(s_w(m)=-(n(k_0-1)+1)/M-1/(mM)\). \(\Theta_2\) and
-- \(\Theta_3\) have their poles among \(-n/N\), \(s_S\) and \(-1\), as
-- \(\frac1{c_a}-\frac1{c_a\sigma_S}=\frac{k_0}{\sigma_S}\), so at another \(s_w(m)\) the residue is that of the
-- quotient of (a1), times \(\sum_{m_j=m}\chi(\mathcal T_j)\); its factors \(m-1\), \(k_0\), \(s\), \(1+s\), \(\sigma_S\) do not
-- vanish there, and \(c_a=-1\) and \(\sigma_w(m)=0\) give \(D(1+m(2-k_0))=m(n+1)\), impossible for \(k_0\ge3\) and
-- \(m\ge2\). At \(n=3\), \(m(3-\delta)=D\) needs \(\delta\le2\), \(\delta(m+1)=3m\) has the one solution
-- \(\delta=m=2\), \(\sum_{m_j=m}\chi(\mathcal T_j)\) is a number of points, and \(4m=2\delta+1\) has no solution; when
-- \(s_w(m)=s_S\), each point of order \(m\) gives \(\Theta(m)\) the double pole of \(1/(\sigma_S\sigma_w(m))\) with the
-- coefficient \(-(m-1)k_0s(c_a+1)/(1+s)\) at \(s_S\), not zero, and the other terms have simple poles there.
--
-- \textit{(b).} Put \(y_1=y_1'+t\psi\), where the power series \(\psi\) in \(t\) and \(y_2\) solves
-- \(a\psi+C+t^{k_0-2}\psi^{k_0}y_2^{k_0}\varepsilon=0\), with \(a\), \(C\), \(\varepsilon\) read at \(y_1=t\psi\), by
-- the implicit function theorem, as its derivative in \(\psi\) at the origin is \(a(0)\ne0\), the last term having the
-- factor \(y_2^{k_0}\). The change keeps \(t\), the component \(E\) and the form, and the restriction of \(f\circ\pi\) to
-- \(y_1'=0\) is then \(t^{N+1}by_2^m\), with \(b\) read at \(y_1=t\psi\). Every other monomial of \(f\circ\pi\) in
-- \(t,y_1',y_2\) has the factor \(y_1'\): it lies above \(t^N(y_1'y_2)^{k_0}\) or \(t^{N+1}y_1'\), or it is
-- \(t^{N+k_0-i}y_1'^iy_2^{k_0}\) with \(0<i<k_0\), from \(((y_1'+t\psi)y_2)^{k_0}\), which lies above the point of the
-- segment from \((N,k_0,k_0)\) to \((N+1,1,0)\) at the parameter \((k_0-i)/(k_0-1)\). So the Newton polyhedron in
-- \(t,y_1',y_2\) is that of \(t^N(y_1'y_2)^{k_0}\), \(t^{N+1}y_1'\) and \(t^{N+1}y_2^m\), whose bounded facet has the
-- normal \(v=(\lambda_m,m,1)\) and the value \(N_v(m)\), and every other monomial lies strictly above that facet, as
-- \(v\) has positive entries and \(t^{N+k_0-i}y_1'^iy_2^{k_0}\) has the value \(N_v(m)+(\lambda_m-m)(k_0-i)\), with
-- \(\lambda_m-m=(k_0-2)m+k_0>0\); so the germ is nondegenerate for it as in (a1). Its rays are \(e_t,e_1,e_2\),
-- \(S_1=(k_0,1,0)\), \(S_2=(k_0,0,1)\), \(v\), normal to the bounded facet, of area \(1\) as \(N_v(m)\) is the
-- determinant of its three vertices, and \(R''=(0,m,1)\), of costs \(\sigma_E\), \(1\), \(1\), \(\sigma_S\),
-- \(\sigma_S\), \(\sigma_v(m)\) and \(R=m(1+s)+1\). The cones of the three vertices, cut in two along the planes
-- through \(e_t\) and \(v\), through \(e_1\) and \(v\), and through \(S_2\) and \(R''\), give
-- \(\frac{m+1}{\sigma_E\sigma_S\sigma_v(m)}+\frac{\lambda_m}{R\sigma_v(m)}+\frac{k_0}{\sigma_v(m)\sigma_S}+\frac{\lambda_mm}{\sigma_S\sigma_v(m)R}+\frac{k_0m}{\sigma_SR}\),
-- the facet and the bounded edges, of length \(1\), whose cones have the multiplicities \(1\), \(m\) and \(\lambda_m\),
-- give \(\frac s{1+s}\big(\frac1{\sigma_v(m)}-\frac{m+1}{\sigma_S\sigma_v(m)}-\frac{\lambda_m}{R\sigma_v(m)}\big)\),
-- and (node) at \(E=\sigma_E\), \(c=c_a\), \(V=1+s\), \(S=\sigma_S\), \(W=\sigma_v(m)\) says that the sum is
-- \(\Theta_{node}(m)\): by (wallray) at \(a=m\) and \(L=\lambda_m\), that is by \cref{lem:cone}(apex), the terms of the
-- cones through the ray \(R''\), of cost \(R=m(1+s)+1\), add up to terms free of \(R\) in \(Z_{top,0}\), its quotient
-- \(\frac{Ls}{VRW}\) against the last term of the bounded edges. By
-- \cite{Varchenko1976Zeta}, the facet has the value \(N_v(m)\) and the area \(1\), and the
-- polyhedron meets no coordinate plane in an edge and no axis. At \(s_S\), \(\sigma_S=0\) and
-- \(\sigma_E=n+Ns_S=-\beta\), so \(\sigma_v(m)=m\beta\), and \(N_S\) times the residue of \(\Theta_{node}(m)\) is
-- \(A_Z+\beta A_Z(\sigma_E-m-1)/(m\beta\sigma_E)=A_Z(m+1)(1+\beta)/(m\beta)=\frac{m+1}{m\beta}A_\star\), with \(A_Z\)
-- and \(\beta A_Z=(k_0-1)s_S/(1+s_S)\) as in (a4).
--
-- \textit{(c).} By the principle of strata as in (a3): over \(E\setminus\Sigma\), of Euler characteristic \(3-\chi(\Sigma)\),
-- the term is \(1/\sigma_E\) and the zeta function \(1-t^N\); over \(\Sigma^\circ\) and at the \(q_j\) they are those
-- of \cref{thm:level}(ii) and (iii) and of (a), and at the nodes on \(g=0\) those of (b); at a node off \(g=0\) they
-- are those of \cref{thm:level}(ii) and (iii) at a point where \(g\ne0\), and the germ of \(H\) is \((y_1y_2)^{k_0}\)
-- times a unit, whose local topological zeta function is \(1/(1+k_0s')^2\) by \cref{fact:zetatop}, and whose monodromy
-- zeta function is \(1\) by \cite{ACampo1975}, as \(y_1y_2=0\) has normal crossings and the origin lies on both of its
-- components. At \(s_S\), \(\sigma_S\) vanishes to the order one, and \(\sigma_E\), the \(\sigma_w(m_j)\) and the
-- \(\sigma_v(m_P)\) do not vanish, so the residue is the sum of those of (a4) and (b). At \(-3/N\), \(c_a=-3/N\),
-- \(1+s\ne0\) as \(N\ne3\), \(\sigma_S=(\delta-3)/\delta\), \(\sigma_w(m)=m(\delta-3)/\delta+1\), which vanishes only
-- at \(m=\delta=2\), and \(\sigma_v(m)=(m+1)\sigma_S\), so only \(\sigma_E\) vanishes there, to the order one, and
-- \(N\) times the residue of \(B\) is \(1\); so \(N\) times the residue of \(\Theta_2\), of \(\Theta_3\) and of
-- \(\Theta(m)\) is \(1/\sigma_S\), that of \(\Theta_0\) is \(1/\sigma_S^2\), and that of \(\Theta_{node}(m)\) is
-- \(\frac1{\sigma_S}+\frac3\delta\frac{m+1}{\sigma_S\sigma_v(m)}=\frac1{\sigma_S^2}\), as
-- \(k_0s(c_a+1)/(1+s)=-3/\delta\) there; and \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\) plus the number of the \(q_j\) is
-- \(\chi(\Sigma)-r\).
--
-- \textit{(d).} Let \(u=h\) and \(y\) be coordinates of the residue polydisc of \(\zeta\) in \(E\), so that
-- \(|dx|=|t|^2|dt|\,|du|\,|dy|\) on the tube, \(g=uA+y^mU\), and, for \(f'=0\), \(f\circ\pi=t^N(H+tg)\) with
-- \(H=u^{k_0}\). Fix \(u\) and \(y\), with \(m'=\mathrm{ord}\,H\) and \(c'=\mathrm{ord}\,g\). The shell
-- \(\mathrm{ord}\,t=i\) has the measure \((1-p^{-1})p^{-3i}\) and \(|t^N|^s=T^{Ni}\), and \(\mathrm{ord}(H+tg)\) is
-- \(i+c'\) for \(i<m'-c'\), \(m'\) for \(i>m'-c'\), and \(m'+j\) for \(i=m'-c'\), where \(j=0\) off one of the \(p-1\)
-- classes of \(\mathrm{ac}\,t\), on which \(H+tg\) runs once over \(p^{m'+1}\Z_p\); so \(j\) has the weight
-- \(w_0=1-\frac1{p-1}\) at \(0\) and \(w_j=p^{-j}\) at \(j\ge1\). Summing the three geometric series, with (qstar) of
-- \cref{thm:level} for \(b_p\), the integral over \(t\) is \(I(m',c')=T^{c'}\big((1-p^{-1})\frac
-- Y{1-Y}-Y^{m'-c'}b_p\big)\) for \(m'>c'\) and \((1-p^{-1})T^{m'}\frac{X_E}{1-X_E}\) for \(m'\le c'\). For fixed \(y\)
-- of order \(b\ge1\), \(u\mapsto uA+y^mU\) is a bijection of \(p\Z_p\) that keeps the measure, and
-- \(\mathrm{ord}(uA)=\mathrm{ord}\,u\); so with \(a=\mathrm{ord}\,u\) and \(c=mb\), \(m'=k_0a\) and \(c'\) is \(a\) for
-- \(a<c\), \(c\) for \(a>c\), and \(c+j\) for \(a=c\), with the same weights \(w_j\). The integral over \(u\) is then
-- \(\Phi(c)=\sum_{a\ne c}(1-p^{-1})p^{-a}I(k_0a,\min(a,c))+(1-p^{-1})p^{-c}\sum_{j\ge0}w_jI(k_0c,c+j)\),
-- whose sums are geometric in \(a\) and \(j\); they give \(\Phi(c)=\alpha'+\beta' X_M^c\), with \(\alpha'\) and \(\beta'\)
-- free of \(c\), and \(X_M=p^{-1}TY^{k_0-1}\): the terms in \((T/p)^c\) cancel, as
-- \(\frac{T/p-1}{1-T/p}+\frac1{p-1}+w_0=0\); those in \((T/p)^{k_0c}\) cancel, as
-- \(\frac{(1-p^{-1})Y}{(1-Y)(1-p^{-1}T)}+b_p\frac{pX_E}{1-pX_E}=\frac{X_E}{1-X_E}\), which is \(X_E\) times the first
-- clause of (qcontact) at \(q=p\), \(X=X_E\), \(e=p-1\), \(u=1-T\), \(v=1-X_E\), \(w=1-Y\), \(z=p-T\) and
-- \(b_p=u(e+w)r/(pzvw)\); and the terms in \(X_M^c\) have the coefficient
-- \(\beta'=(1-p^{-1})b_p\big(\frac1{p-1}+\frac1{1-X_M}+\frac1{1-pX_E}-\frac1{1-X_S}\big)=b_p\frac{(1-pX_S)(1-p^{-1}X_M)(1-X_E)}{(1-X_M)(1-X_S)(1-pX_E)}\),
-- by the second clause at \(L=X_M\), as \(X_S=X_MX_E\). The gaps of (qcontact) are positive on the cone \(q>1\),
-- \(0<T<1\), \(0<L<1\), \(0<X<1/q\) of rationals, which holds the product of the infinite sets \(1<q<2\), \(0<T<1\),
-- \(0<L<1\) and \(0<X<\frac12\); so, as (qstar) of \cref{thm:level}, (qcontact) is an identity of rational functions in
-- \(q\), \(X\), \(T\), \(L\), as it is used here. The integral over \(y\) is
-- \(\sum_{b\ge1}(1-p^{-1})p^{-b}\Phi(mb)=\alpha'+(1-p^{-1})\beta'\frac{X_w}{1-X_w}\); at \(m=1\) the germ is that of a
-- point of \(Z\), with the coordinate \(uA+yU\) in place of \(y\), and this is \(\tau\). So at \(m\) it is
-- \(\tau+(1-p^{-1})\beta'\big(\frac{X_w}{1-X_w}-\frac{p^{-1}X_M}{1-p^{-1}X_M}\big)=\tau-(1-p^{-1})p^{-1}\beta'\frac{(1-X_M)(X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1})}{(1-X_w)(1-p^{-1}X_M)}\),
-- which is \(\tau-\Delta_p(m)\) by the product form of \(b_p\) in \cref{thm:level}(i) and
-- \(1-pX_S=1-Y^{k_0}=(1-Y)(1+Y+\dots+Y^{k_0-1})\).
--
-- For \(f'\ne0\) and \(p\nmid m\), the integral is read by the formula
-- of \cite[Theorem~4.2]{DenefHoornaert2001}, as restated in \cite{BoriesVeys2016}, with the weights \((3,1,1)\) of the
-- form: \(\sum_\tau S_\tau p^{-3}\big((p-1)^3-N_\tau F\big)\) over the bounded faces \(\tau\) of the Newton polyhedron
-- of \(f\circ\pi\) in \(t,u,y\), where \(N_\tau\) is the number of zeros in the torus over \(\mathbb F_p\) of the
-- polynomial of \(\tau\) and \(S_\tau\) the sum of \(p^{-(3k_t+k_u+k_y)}T^{\mu(k)}\) over the \(k\) of the open cone of
-- \(\tau\), \(\mu(k)\) the least value of \(k\) on the polyhedron; its proof, the sum over the orders \(k\) of
-- \((t,u,y)\), on each of which \(f\circ\pi\) is \(p^{\mu(k)}\) times the polynomial of \(\tau\) plus \(p\) times a
-- series, holds as it stands for a power series over \(\Z_p\) whose face polynomials have no singular zero in the
-- torus. When \(k_0\ge3\), or \(k_0=2\) and \(r=2\), the change \(u=u'+t\varphi\) of (a1) is defined over \(\Z_p\), by
-- Hensel's lemma at a simple root modulo \(p\), and keeps the measure of the tube; after it the polyhedron is that of
-- \(t^Nu'^{k_0}\), \(t^{N+1}u'\) and \(t^{N+1}y^m\), the face polynomials are those of
-- \(u'^{k_0}\varepsilon+t(u'A'+y^mU)\) at \(\zeta\), with \(A'=A\) or \(A+2\varphi\varepsilon\), a unit: \(A\) is one for
-- \(k_0\ge3\), and \(A+2\varphi\varepsilon\) is the derivative of \(\varepsilon\varphi^2+A\varphi+C\) at a simple root, so
-- a unit whether \(A\) is or not; and as
-- \(p\nmid m\) they have no singular zero, and their numbers of zeros do not depend on the units; so the integral is
-- that of \(f'=0\).
--
-- When \(k_0=2\) and \(r=0\), \(C\) is a unit at \(\zeta\), and the polyhedron has the further vertex
-- \((N+2,0,0)\): the edge from \((N,2,0)\) to \((N+2,0,0)\) carries \(\varepsilon u^2+Atu+Ct^2\), with
-- \(N_\tau=r(p-1)^2\), and the triangle with the vertex \((N+1,0,m)\) carries \(Q+Bty^m\), \(Q=\varepsilon
-- u^2+Atu+Ct^2\), with \(N_\tau=(p-1)(p-1-r)\), since the number of \(y\) with \(y^m=-Q/(Bt)\) is
-- \(\sum_{\chi^m=1}\chi(-Q/(Bt))\) over the characters \(\chi\) of \(\mathbb F_p^\times\), and with \(u=tw\),
-- \(Q/t=tQ(1,w)\), so that the sum over \(t\) of a \(\chi\ne1\) is \(0\); the other faces carry binomials and
-- monomials, whose numbers of zeros do not depend on \(r\). So the formula is affine in \(r\). The cone of the edge is
-- \(\{(a,a,c):mc>a\}\) and that of the triangle \(\{j(m,m,1)\}\), so
-- \(S_{edge}=\frac{p^{-1}}{1-p^{-1}}\big(\frac{1+X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}}{1-X_w}-1\big)\) and
-- \(S_{tri}=\frac{X_w}{1-X_w}\), with \(X_M=p^{-4}T^{N+2}\) at \(k_0=2\), and the derivative in \(r\) is
-- \(-p^{-3}F\big((p-1)^2S_{edge}-(p-1)S_{tri}\big)=-(1-p^{-1})p^{-2}F\frac{X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}}{1-X_w}\); at \(r=2\)
-- the value is \(\tau-\Delta_p(m)\), as above, read on a germ with \(r=2\) and \(C\) a unit, that is with two roots
-- in \(\mathbb F_p^\times\). Such a germ exists at every \(p\nmid2m\): at \(p=3\), where \(\mathbb F_3^\times=\{1,2\}\),
-- the roots are \(1\) and \(2\), whose sum is \(0\) modulo \(3\), so \(A\) is not a unit there, which (d) allows at
-- \(k_0=2\). The denominators of \(\tau\), \(F\) and \(\Delta_p(m)\) divide
-- \((1-X_E)(1-X_S)(1-X_w)(1-p^{-1}T)\).
--
-- \textit{(e).} At the tie the polyhedron at \(\zeta\) has the vertices \(A=(N,k_0,0)\), \(D=(N+k_0,0,0)\) and
-- \(Y=(N+k_0-1,0,m)\), and \((N+k_0-1,1,0)\) on the edge \(AD\), whose polynomial in \(u=tX\) is \(t^{k_0}\) times
-- \(\varepsilon X^{k_0}+AX+C\); with \(p\nmid k_0\) and simple roots not zero modulo \(p\) every face is nondegenerate
-- modulo \(p\), and in \cite[Theorem~4.2]{DenefHoornaert2001} only the counts \((p-1)^2r\) of \(AD\) and
-- \((p-1)(p-1-r)\) of \(ADY\) depend on \(r\), counted with \(u=tX\). The open cone of \(AD\), spanned by \(e_y\) and the
-- facet ray \(w=(m,m,1)\), has the multiplicity \(m\), its box points are \((j,j,1)\) for \(0<j<m\) and \((m,m,2)\), of the
-- letters \(p^{-1}X_M^j\) and \(p^{-1}X_w\), and \(w\) has the letter \(X_w\); so its sum is
-- \(p^{-1}(X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}+X_w)/((1-p^{-1})(1-X_w))\) and that of the ray \(X_w/(1-X_w)\), and (qslope) at \(q=p\),
-- \(e=p-1\), \(a=1-p^{-1}\), \(M=X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1}\), \(X=X_w\) and \(v=1-X_w\) gives
-- \((p-1)^2\) times the first less \((p-1)\) times the second as \((p-1)(X_M+\dots+X_M^{m-1})/(1-X_w)\); times the factor
-- \(-p^{-3}F\) of the counts this is the slope, which at \(k_0=2\) is that of the proof of (d). Under (Dt) the faces
-- \(u^{k_0}+Ct^{d+1}\) and its sum with \(t^dy^m\) have, for \(p\nmid k_0\), the derivative in \(u\) a monomial times
-- \(k_0\), a unit, at the zeros on the torus, and the formula of \cite[Theorem~4.2]{DenefHoornaert2001} applies with the
-- counts said: \(u^{k_0}t^{-(d+1)}\) is the \(\gamma_D\)-th power of a primitive monomial, a coordinate of the torus of
-- \((t,u)\) over \(\mathbb F_p\), so \(u^{k_0}+Ct^{d+1}\) has \((p-1)r_D\) zeros there, and \((p-1)^2r_D\) with \(y\).
--
-- The row (wallray) is \(a\) times the flip, the tie \(kW+L=LS+kR\) divided by \(SWR\), then \cref{lem:cone}(apex) at
-- \(\mu=L\) and \(X=RW\), then (countsplit) at \(R=mV+1\) times \(L/W\); the tie \((1+m)V=R+s\) of the ray is that
-- (countsplit) after (apex).
proof thm:contact
  (wallray) with[k * W + L = L * S + k * R by V[k * h_4 | L * h_3]; with[L / (R * W) = L / (V * (R * W)) + s * L / (V * (R * W)) by lem:cone(apex)[mu := L, X := R * W, s := s, V := V]; with[m / (1 * R) + 1 / (R * V) = 1 / (1 * V) by lem:cone(countsplit)[b := 1, b1 := m, b2 := 1, c := 1, d := V, w := R; 1 * R = 1 * 1 + m * V by V[1 * h_2]]; V[(a / (S * W * R)) * hw1, 1 * hw2, (L / W) * hw3]]]]
  (contactd) with[1 * (m * k1) / (S * W * R) + 1 * k / (S * R) + (1 + m) * (m * k1) / (R * W) = 1 * (m * k1) / (R * W) + 1 * k / (W * S) + m * k1 / (V * W) + m * k1 * s / (V * R * W) by thm:contact(wallray)[a := 1, L := m * k1, k := k, m := m, s := s, c := c, V := V, S := S, W := W, R := R; W = m * k1 * c + R by V[1 * h_6 | 1 * h_7]]; with[d * (m * S + 1) = d * W + m * E by V[(d * m * c) * h_1, (d * m) * h_4, m * h_3, (d * m) * h_5, d * h_6]; with[m * c * V + k * c * V + m * k1 * c * S + V * W + k * s * c * W + s * k1 * m1 * c = S * V * W + s * c by V[(V) * h_6, (S * V) * h_6, (c * k * s) * h_6, (V) * h_5, (V^2 * m) * h_5, (c * k1 * m) * h_5, (V * c * k1 * m) * h_5, (c * m) * h_4, (c * k * m) * h_4, (V * c * k * m) * h_4, (c^2 * k * k1 * m) * h_4, (c) * h_2, (c * k1) * h_2, (c * s) * h_2, (c * k) * h_2, (c * k * s) * h_2, (c) * h_1, (c * m1) * h_1, (c * m1 * s) * h_1]; V[(1 / d) * hw1, (1 / (d * E * S * W)) * hw2, (1 / (d * c * V * S * W)) * hw3]]]]
  (detached) with[(d + 1) * m * S * V + k * d * V + k * m * E * S * V + k^2 * E * V + s * E * m1 * k * k1 = d * W * V + k * E * W + s * E * S * k + s * E * k + s * E * m1 * k * S by V[(E * k) * h_6, (V * d) * h_6, (V * m) * h_5, (V * d * m) * h_5, (E * k * s) * h_5, (E * V * k * m) * h_5, (E * k * m1 * s) * h_5, (k^2) * h_4, (V * k^2) * h_4, (k * m) * h_4, (k * s) * h_4, (N * k^2 * s^2) * h_4, (d * k * m) * h_4, (d * k * s) * h_4, (d * k^2 * s^2) * h_4, (k^2 * m * n) * h_4, (k^2 * m * s) * h_4, (k^2 * n * s) * h_4, (V * d * k * m) * h_4, (V * k^2 * m * n) * h_4, (k * k1 * m1 * s) * h_4, (N * k^2 * m * s) * h_4, (N * k^2 * m1 * s^2) * h_4, (d * k * m1 * s) * h_4, (d * k^2 * m * s) * h_4, (d * k^2 * m1 * s^2) * h_4, (k^2 * m1 * n * s) * h_4, (N * V * k^2 * m * s) * h_4, (V * d * k^2 * m * s) * h_4, (k^2 * n) * h_3, (N * k^2 * s) * h_3, (k * m * n) * h_3, (k^2 * m * n^2) * h_3, (N * k * m * s) * h_3, (d * k * m * n) * h_3, (N^2 * k^2 * m * s^2) * h_3, (N * d * k * m * s) * h_3, (N * d * k^2 * m * s^2) * h_3, (d * k^2 * m * n * s) * h_3, (2 * N * k^2 * m * n * s) * h_3, (N * k * s^2) * h_2, (k * n * s) * h_2, (k^2 * n^2 * s) * h_2, (N^2 * k^2 * s^3) * h_2, (N * k^2 * s^2) * h_2, (k^2 * n * s) * h_2, (N * d * k * s^2) * h_2, (N * d * k^2 * s^3) * h_2, (d * k * n * s) * h_2, (d * k^2 * n * s^2) * h_2, (2 * N * k^2 * n * s^2) * h_2, (N * k * m1 * s^2) * h_1, (k * m1 * n * s) * h_1]; V[(1 / (E * S * W * V)) * hw1]]
  (qtube) with[1 <= e + sigma by V[hp(e)]; with[(sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..e, t^i) + t^e * (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := e, m := sigma, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..e, t^i) + t^e * (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) + t^sigma * (sum i in 1..e, t^i) by lem:qsum(swap)[l := e, m := sigma, t := t]; with[1 <= D + sigma by V[hp(D)]; with[(sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..D, t^i) + t^D * (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := D, m := sigma, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..D, t^i) + t^D * (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..sigma, t^i) + t^sigma * (sum i in 1..D, t^i) by lem:qsum(swap)[l := D, m := sigma, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..e + sigma + D, t^i) = (sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) + t^(e + sigma) * (sum i in 1..D, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := e + sigma, m := D, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..D, t^i) + t^D * (sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) + t^(e + sigma) * (sum i in 1..D, t^i) by lem:qsum(swap)[l := D, m := e + sigma, t := t]; with[1 <= k1 * (e + sigma) by V[(e + sigma) * hp(k1), 1 * hp(e)]; with[t^(e + sigma) * (sum i in 1..(e + sigma) * k1, t^i) = (sum i in 1..e + sigma, t^i) * (sum j in 1..k1, (t^(e + sigma))^j) by lem:qsum(scale)[a := e + sigma, b := k1, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..(e + sigma) * k1, t^i) = (sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma), t^i) by sum; with[(sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + (D + sigma), t^i) = (sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma), t^i) + t^(k1 * (e + sigma)) * (sum i in 1..D + sigma, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := k1 * (e + sigma), m := D + sigma, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma, t^i) = (sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + (D + sigma), t^i) by sum; with[1 <= k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma by V[1 * hp(D)]; with[(sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma + e, t^i) = (sum i in 1..k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma, t^i) + t^(k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma) * (sum i in 1..e, t^i) by lem:qsum(add)[l := k1 * (e + sigma) + D + sigma, m := e, t := t]; with[(sum i in 1..(k1 + 1) * (e