-- At the tie \(d=k_0-1\) the simple roots of \cref{thm:contact}(a) are a proved boundary: the multiplicities of the
-- roots of \(\varepsilon(0)X^{k_0}+A(0)X+C(0)\) do not determine \(Z_{top,0}\). At \(k_0=3\) and the double root of
-- \(X^3-3X+2=(X-1)^2(X+2)\), the shift at the double root gives, up to units, the germ
-- \(t^N(u'^3+3tu'^2+t^2y^m+\beta t^4)\); for \(\beta\ne0\) the next level carries the cusp \(u'^2+t^3\), and at
-- \((n,N,m)=(3,3,2)\) the poles of \(Z_{top,0}\) are \(-1\), \(-11/14\), \(-3/4\) and \(-11/15\), where a simple root gives
-- \(-1\), \(-3/4\) and \(-11/15\); the values for \(\beta\ne0\), for \(\beta=0\) and for a simple root differ at
-- \((n,N,m)=(3,3,2)\), \((3,3,3)\) and \((4,2,2)\). For \(\beta=0\) the germ is singular along the axis of \(t\), and the
-- factor \((1+s)^2\) can appear: at \(m=2\), through the ray \((0,1,1)\) of cost \(2(1+s)\), and not at \(m=3\), where the
-- facet through \((N+1,2,0)\) and \((N+2,0,m)\) has the normal \((0,3,2)\). The value at a multiple root is read at the next
-- step of the Newton--Puiseux iteration, its level in the tree of Eggers and Wall
-- \cite[Section~3]{GarciaBarrosoGonzalezPerezPopescuPampu2019}, which is not done here.
remark rem:multiroot [a multiple root at the tie]
  origin: ?{\text{a boundary of \cref{thm:contact}(a) at}\ d=k_0-1\text{, by exact counterexamples on the formula of \cite[Theorem~5.3(ii)]{DenefLoeser1992}; the next step is \cite[Section~3]{GarciaBarrosoGonzalezPerezPopescuPampu2019}}}

-- For \(n=3\), every \(k_0\ge2\), \(\delta\ge1\), every \(d\ge1\) prime to \(k_0\) and every list of contact orders
-- as in \cref{thm:contact}(a), with \(r\) the number of points of \(Z_{red}\), every pole \(s_0\ne-1\) of
-- \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) has \(z_f(o(s_0))\ge1\), where \(z_f(e)\) is the exponent of the cyclotomic polynomial \(\Phi_e\) in
-- \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)\) and \(o\) is as in \cref{thm:level}: at \(d=1\) always, and for \(d\ge2\) when \(\delta\ge3\) or
-- \(r\ge3\), and at every pole but \(s_S\) when \(\delta\le2\) and \(r=2\). For \(d\ge2\), \(\delta\le2\) and \(r=1\), and at
-- \(s_S\) for \(r=2\), it is open: on every split of \(\delta D\) into one or two points, for \(k_0\le5\) and \(d\le8\),
-- the \(956\) configurations have \(2388\) poles, each with its root, and the \(874\) candidates whose exponent is \(0\)
-- are no poles, as (a4) shows at \(d=1\), which is not redone for \(d\ge2\). The origin is an isolated critical point
-- of \(f\), so \(\zeta_{f,0}(t)/(1-t)\) is the characteristic polynomial of the monodromy at the origin on the cohomology
-- of degree \(2\) of the Milnor fibre: a primitive root of unity of order \(e\) is then its root, of the multiplicity
-- \(z_f(e)\), and the root \(1\) at \(-1\) is that of the cohomology of degree \(0\), so the local monodromy conjecture
-- holds for \(Z_{top,0}(f)\) at the origin. The same holds at \(d=1\) over a nodal \(\Sigma\) as in
-- \cref{thm:contact}(c), with a root on the cohomology of some degree. There, with \(\Sigma^\circ\) as in (c), \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\ge0\) only when \(\Sigma\) is
-- smooth, when \(\Sigma\) is two lines, and when \(\Sigma\) is a nodal cubic, a line and a conic, or three lines and
-- \(g=0\) meets it at its nodes only, where \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=0\); in every other case each term of the exponent
-- of a root in the zeta function of the monodromy of (c) is at least \(0\).
--
-- At \(s_w(m)\), \(m\ge2\), the root of the order \(e_w\) of \(s_w(m)\) is read component by component on the layer \(\Phi_{e_w}\): by \cref{lem:mark} the open part of the contact component, of Euler characteristic \(1\), enters the
-- value there with the weight \(1\), and that is its share in the product of \cref{thm:contact}(a3), the factor
-- \((1-t^{mM})/(1-t^M)\) of (a2), whose exponent of \(\Phi_{e_w}\) is \(1\). At \(s_S\), for \(\delta\le2\) and
-- one point of \(Z_{red}\), of order \(m=\delta D\), the contact component has the whole cost \(1+m\beta=-1\) (wedge),
-- so that by \cref{lem:mark} its mark keeps the value \(1/(1+m\beta)\) on the layer \(\Phi_{e_S}\) that it has in
-- the residue of (a4), \(A_\star(1+1/(1+m\beta))=0\): \(s_S\) is no pole, and \(z_f(e_S)=0\), the share \(-1\) of
-- the corner over \(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red}\) against the share \(1\) of the contact component. Candidates can be lost
-- with their classes: \(x^2+y^3+z^4\) is of (a) at \((k_0,\delta)=(2,1)\) with one point of order \(3\), and its
-- poles are \(-1\) and \(s_w(3)=-13/12\), while \(s_S=-7/6\) and \(-3/2\) are not poles, and \(z_f(6)=z_f(2)=0\).
-- The reading on a layer is a tie over \(\mathbb Q\); that \(z_f(o(s_0))\) is not zero at a pole is a count, read
-- on (a3) and (c), which rest on \cref{fact:acampo} and, at a contact, on the formula of \cite{Varchenko1976Zeta}
-- in (a2).
--
-- After \(\pi\), the strict transform of \(\{f=0\}\) meets \(E\) along all of \(\Sigma\) with the multiplicity
-- \(k_0\ge2\), which no blowing up of points separates; and in the coordinates \(x\) the face of degree \(N\) of
-- the Newton polyhedron of \(f\) carries \(h^{k_0}\), singular on the torus unless \(h\) is a monomial, so that
-- \(f\) is then degenerate. Over \(\mathbb F_p\), by \cref{thm:contact}(d), the part of \(Z_0(f)\) at a tangency
-- has its poles among \(-3/N\), \(s_S\), \(s_w(m)\) and \(-1\), for every \(p\) when \(f'=0\): the component \(R'\)
-- gives no candidate there either. The row: the cost \(1+m\beta\) of the contact component at \(s_S\), with
-- \(\beta=-c/D\), \(c=3-\delta\) and \(mc=2D\), is \(-1\), written as \(a+1=mc/D\) with \(a=1\) (wedge); and the degree
-- of the factor of a detached point in \cref{thm:contact}(a2), its share \((k_0-1)(m-1)(D+1)\) of the Milnor number, a tie
-- of counts (zdeg).
corollary cor:contactmonodromy [the monodromy conjecture over a contact]
  (wedge): c, m, D in NN>=1, b, a in QQ>0, b * D = c, m * c = 2 * D, a + 1 = m * b : a = 1
  fix (wedge) corner.wedge
  neqfix (wedge) corner.wedge.nopoint
  (zdeg): k, k1, m, m1, D, g, gD, Nw, Nad in NN>=1, k = k1 + 1, m = m1 + 1, g * Nw = k * m * (D + 1), gD * Nad = k * (D + 1) : (D + 1) + g * Nw = gD * Nad + m * (D + 1) + k1 * m1 * (D + 1)
  fix (zdeg) corner.zdeg
  neqfix (zdeg) corner.zdeg.nofacet
  origin: ?{\text{the result: the conjecture for these germs, read by \cref{thm:contact}(a2) to (a5), (c) and (d), \cref{lem:mark} and \cref{fact:acampo}}}

-- At every \(d\), by \cref{thm:contact}(a3), with \(\chi^\circ=\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red})=\delta(3-\delta)-r\),
-- \(z_f(e)=(3-\chi(\Sigma))[e\mid N]+\chi^\circ\big([e\mid D]-[e\mid k_0D]\big)+\sum_{j}\big(g_2[e\mid m_jM_d/g_2]-g_1[e\mid
-- M_d/g_1]\big)+\sum_{j'}\big([e\mid D+1]+g[e\mid N_{wD}]-\gamma_D[e\mid N_{AD}]-[e\mid m_{j'}(D+1)]\big)\), the first sum over
-- the points of (M) or (T) and the second over those of (Dt), with \(g_2\), \(g\) and \(N_{wD}\) at \(m_j\) and
-- \(m_{j'}\); at \(d=1\) it is the formula below. Its terms: \(g_2\) divides \(\gcd((k_0-1)m,dm)=mg_1\) and \(g_1\) divides
-- \(g_2\), so \(M_d/g_1\) divides \(mM_d/g_2\) and a term of the first sum is at least \(0\); \(g\) divides \(m\gamma_D\),
-- \(\gamma_D\) divides \(g\), and \(D+1\) divides \(N_{AD}\), which divides \(N_{wD}\), as does \(m(D+1)\), with the quotients
-- \(m\gamma_D/g\) and \(k_0/g\); so a term of the second sum is \(g-\gamma_D\ge0\) when \(e\mid D+1\), and when
-- \(e\nmid D+1\) but \(e\) divides \(N_{AD}\) and \(m(D+1)\), at a prime with \(v_p(e)>v_p(D+1)\) the first gives
-- \(v_p(k_0)>v_p(\gamma_D)\) and the second \(p\mid m\), so \(g\ge2\gamma_D\) and the term is at least \(\gamma_D-1\ge0\); the
-- other cases are immediate. The term of \(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red}\) is \(-\chi^\circ\) at the \(e\) dividing \(k_0D\) and not
-- \(D\), and \(0\) at the others, so it is at least \(0\) when \(\chi^\circ\le0\), that is \(\delta\ge3\), or \(\delta\le2\)
-- and \(r\ge2\). At \(-3/N\) the term of \(E\) is \(3-\chi(\Sigma)\ge1\). At \(s_S\), whose order \(e_S\) divides \(k_0D\) and
-- not \(D\), as \(k_0\nmid D\), the term of \(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red}\) is \(-\chi^\circ\ge1\) when \(\delta\ge3\) or \(r\ge3\). At
-- \(s_w(m)\) the own term of a point of order \(m\) is \(g_2-g_1[e\mid M_d/g_1]\ge1\), as \(e\mid N_w\): it is \(0\) only if
-- \(m\) divides \(\gcd(\nu_w,N_w)\), and as \(\nu_w=m(3(k_0-1)+d)+d\) that asks \(m\mid d\), \(g_2=g_1\) and \(g_1\mid d/m\),
-- while at a prime \(p\mid m\), \(g_2=g_1\) forces \(v_p(d)\le v_p(k_0-1)\), so \(v_p(g_1)=v_p(d)\) and \(g_1\nmid d/m\). At
-- \(s_{wD}(m)\) the own term \([e\mid D+1]+g-\gamma_D[e\mid N_{AD}]-[e\mid m(D+1)]\) is at least \(1\): \(e\mid m(D+1)\)
-- exactly when \(k_0\mid(d+1)m\), that is \(g=k_0\); \(e\mid N_{AD}\) with \(\gamma_D=1\) and \(g=2\) would ask \(k_0=2\) and \(d\)
-- even; \(e\nmid m(D+1)\) with \(g=\gamma_D\) and \(e\mid N_{AD}\) would ask \(m\gamma_D\) to divide \(nk_0m+(d+1)m+k_0\), whose
-- \(p\)-adic order at \(p\mid m\) is that of \(k_0\), below that of \(m\gamma_D\); and \(e\mid D+1\) asks \(k_0\mid(d+1)m\) and
-- \(m\mid k_0\), where \(g=\gamma_D\) forces \(m=1\). So every term is at least \(0\) when \(\chi^\circ\le0\), and each candidate
-- has a positive term of its own, which gives the class said. The degree of the factor of a detached point is
-- \((D+1)+gN_{wD}-\gamma_DN_{AD}-m(D+1)=(k_0-1)(m-1)(D+1)\) (zdeg), as \(gN_{wD}=k_0m(D+1)\) and \(\gamma_DN_{AD}=k_0(D+1)\),
-- and that of a point of (M) or (T) is \((m-1)M_d\).
--
-- The origin is isolated: else a curve of critical points through it would lie in \(f=0\) \cite[\S3]{Milnor1968}, and its
-- strict transform would meet \(E\) at a point \(\xi\). At every point of \(E\) the germ of the strict transform is
-- nondegenerate, at the points of \(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red}\), at the transversal points and at the contacts of (M), (T) and
-- (Dt), by the proof of \cref{thm:contact}(a1), so the toric modification at \(\xi\) is an embedded resolution near the
-- fibre over \(\xi\). Off \(t=0\) the only face of the boundary that it subdivides is the cone of \(e_u\) and \(e_y\), whose
-- orbit is the axis of \(t\), and only under (M) and (T), through the ray \(R'\); on that axis, after the shift, the strict
-- transform contains the axis and its derivative in \(u'\) is \(t^d\) times a unit. So the strict transform is smooth at
-- every point with \(t\ne0\) near \(\xi\), which is a contradiction.
--
-- At \(d=1\), by \cref{thm:contact}(a3), \(z_f(e)=(3-\chi(\Sigma))[e\mid N]+\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red})\big([e\mid D]-[e\mid
-- k_0D]\big)+\sum_j\big([e\mid m_jM]-[e\mid M]\big)\), with \(\chi(\Sigma)=\delta(3-\delta)\); every term is at
-- least \(0\) except the second when \(\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red})=1\), that is \(\delta\le2\) and \(Z_{red}\)
-- one point, of order \(m=\delta D\). The poles lie among \(-1\), \(-3/N\), \(s_S\) and the \(s_w(m_j)\). At
-- \(s_w(m)\), \(m\ge2\): as \(\nu_w\) is prime to \(m\), its order \(e_w\) (\cref{lem:frame}) is \(mM/\gcd(\nu_w,M)\), which divides
-- \(mM\) and not \(M\), so each point of order \(m\) gives \(1\) to the last sum; for \(\delta=1\) and one point,
-- \(M=k_0^2\), \(\nu_w\equiv k_0-1\) modulo \(k_0^2\) and \(e_w=(k_0+1)k_0^2\), which does not divide
-- \(k_0D=k_0(k_0+1)\); for \(\delta=2\) and one point, \(m\) is even and \(\nu_w\) odd, so the \(2\)-adic order of
-- \(e_w\) exceeds that of \(M=k_0(2k_0-1)\), which is that of \(k_0D\). At \(s_S\) for \(\delta\ne3\): its order
-- is \(k_0q_S\) with \(q_S=D/\gcd(3k_0+1,D)>1\), as \(D\) divides \(3k_0+1\) only at \(\delta=3\); \(k_0\) divides
-- \(M=k_0D-N\), \(M\equiv1\) modulo \(q_S\), and \(q_S\) is prime to \(k_0\), so
-- \(z_f(k_0q_S)=(3-\chi(\Sigma))[k_0q_S\mid N]+\#Z_{red}-\chi(\Sigma)+\#\{j:q_S\mid m_j\}\), at least \(1\) unless
-- \(\delta\le2\) and \(\#Z_{red}\le2\); there, as \(s_S\ne-1\) at \(n=3\), if \(1+m_j\beta=0\) for some \(j\),
-- \(s_S=s_w(m_j)\), and otherwise \(s_S\) is a simple candidate whose residue is \(A_\star\) times \(1+1/(1+\delta
-- D\beta)=0\) for one point, or \(1/(1+m_1\beta)+1/(1+m_2\beta)=0\) for two, as \(\delta
-- D\beta=\delta(\delta-3)=-2\), by \cref{thm:contact}(a4); at \(\delta=3\), \(s_S=-3/N=-1/k_0\) and
-- \(z_f(k_0)=3+\#Z_{red}\).
--
-- At \(-3/N\): every point of \(\Sigma\) gives \(\Theta_2\), \(\Theta_3\) or \(\Theta(m)\) the term
-- \(1/(\sigma_E\sigma_S)\), and the quotient of (a1) has no pole there unless \(\delta=m=2\), so for \(\delta=1\),
-- \(N\) times the residue is \(3-\chi(\Sigma)+\chi(\Sigma)/(1-3/\delta)=1-1=0\), and \(-3/N\) is no pole unless
-- \(N=3\), where it is \(-1\); for \(\delta\ge3\), \(z_f(o(3/N))\ge3-\chi(\Sigma)\ge3\); for \(\delta=2\),
-- \(o(3/N)=2k_0/\gcd(3,2k_0)\) has a \(2\)-adic order above that of \(k_0D\), so
-- \(z_f(o(3/N))\ge3-\chi(\Sigma)=1\).
--
-- By (c), \(z_f(e)=(3-\chi(\Sigma))[e\mid N]+\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\big([e\mid D]-[e\mid k_0D]\big)+r_0[e\mid
-- D]+\sum_j\big([e\mid m_jM]-[e\mid M]\big)+\sum_P[e\mid N_v(m_P)]\), and the poles lie among \(-1\), \(-3/N\),
-- \(s_S\), the \(s_w(m_j)\) and the \(s_v(m_P)\), as
-- \(\frac1{c_a}-\frac1{c_a\sigma_S^2}=\frac{k_0(\sigma_S+1)}{\sigma_S^2}\), and \(\sigma_v(m)\) at \(-1\) is
-- \(1-\lambda_m(N-2)\ne0\).
--
-- Let \(C_i\) be the components of \(\Sigma\), \(r'\) of them, of geometric genera \(g_i\); two of them meet at
-- \(\deg C_i\deg C_k\) nodes, so \(\chi(\Sigma)=\sum_i(2-2g_i)-r\le2r'-r'(r'-1)/2\le3\), with equality only for
-- two lines and for three lines not through one point; and \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=\sum_i(2-2g_i-b_i-z_i)\), with
-- \(b_i\ge\sum_{k\ne i}\deg C_i\deg C_k\) the branches at nodes on \(C_i\) and \(z_i\) the number of the \(q_j\)
-- on \(C_i\), where \(z_i\ge1\) unless \(g=0\) meets \(C_i\) at nodes only, and the contacts of \(g=0\) along
-- \(C_i\) add up to \(D\deg C_i\) by B\'ezout. Hence \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\ge0\) only when \(\Sigma\) is smooth, done
-- above; when \(\delta=3\), \(\Sigma\) is a nodal cubic, a line and a conic, or three lines, and \(g=0\) meets
-- \(\Sigma\) at nodes only, where \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=0\); and when \(\Sigma\) is two lines, where
-- \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=2-z_1-z_2\), with \(z_2\ge1\) when the node lies on \(g=0\) with its contact of order \(m\)
-- along the first line, and \(z_1=0\) exactly when \(m=D\). When \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\le0\), every term of
-- \(z_f(e)\) is at least \(0\), and a pole \(s_0\ne-1\) has its class: at \(s_v(m)\), whose order divides
-- \(N_v(m)\), by its term; at \(s_w(m)\) as above; at \(s_S\), whose order \(e_S\) divides \(k_0D\) and not \(D\),
-- as \(k_0\nmid D\), by \(-\chi(\Sigma^\circ)\ge1\) when \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)<0\); and at \(-3/N\) by
-- \(3-\chi(\Sigma)\ge1\), except for three lines, where \(\delta=3\) and \(-3/N=s_S\), and for two lines, where
-- \(\delta=2\), \(N=2k_0\ne3\) and \(\eta_\Sigma=0+2(-2)+4=0\), so that by (c) \(-3/N\) is no pole unless some
-- \(m_j=2\), and then it is \(s_w(2)\).
--
-- There remain the pole \(s_S\) when \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=0\) and \(\Sigma\) is not smooth, and every pole when
-- \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=1\) and \(\Sigma\) is two lines. For \(\delta=3\), \(e_S=k_0\) divides every
-- \(N_v(m)\equiv-mN\) modulo \(k_0\), and \(g=0\) passes through a node, so \(z_f(k_0)\ge1\).
--
-- For two lines, \(\delta=2\) and \(\beta=-1/D\), and there are four cases: (1) the node off \(g=0\) and one
-- \(q_j\) of order \(D\) on each line: \(1+D\beta=0\), so \(s_S=s_w(D)\) and \(z_f(e_S)\ge2\); (2) the node with
-- \(m<D\) and one \(q_j\) on each line, of orders \(D-m\) and \(D-1\): no \(1+m_j\beta\) and no \(\sigma_v(m)\)
-- vanishes at \(s_S\), and by (c) the residue there is \(A_\star(\frac Dm+D-\frac{(m+1)D}m)/N_S=0\), so \(s_S\) is
-- no pole; (3) the node with \(m=D\) and two \(q_j\) on the second line: \(k_0D\) divides
-- \(N_v(D)=D((D+1)k_0-N)\), so \(z_f(e_S)\ge1\); (4) the node with \(m=D\) and one \(q_j\), of order \(D-1\),
-- where \(\chi(\Sigma^\circ)=1\): \(s_S\) is no pole, its residue being \(A_\star(1+D-(D+1))/N_S=0\) by (c);
-- \(-3/N\) is no pole, as \(\eta_\Sigma=0\) and \(D-1\ne2\); \(N_v(D)=k_0D(D-1)\) and \(\nu_v(D)\equiv-1\) modulo
-- \(D-1=2k_0\), so the order of \(s_v(D)\) does not divide \(k_0D\), the only negative term vanishes there and
-- \(z_f\ge1\) by the term of the node; and at \(s_w(D-1)\) the term \([e\mid N_v(D)]\) is at least \([e\mid
-- k_0D]-[e\mid D]\), so \(z_f\ge1\) by the term of \(q_j\).
--
-- For (wedge): \(1+m\beta\) is the value of \(\sigma_w(m)\) at \(s_S\) by (a4), and \(\beta=(\delta-3)/D\); one
-- point of order \(m=\delta D\) with \(\delta\le2\) gives \(m(3-\delta)=\delta(3-\delta)D=2D\). The order
-- \(e_S=k_0q_S\) divides \(mM\) and not \(M\), as \(q_S\) divides \(D\), hence \(m\), and \(M\equiv1\) modulo
-- \(q_S\), while \(k_0\) divides \(M=k_0D-N\); it divides \(k_0D\) and not \(D\), and not \(N\), as
-- \(q_S\nmid\delta\) for \(\delta=1\) and \(q_S\) is odd for \(\delta=2\); so in (a3)
-- \(z_f(e_S)=-\chi(\Sigma\setminus Z_{red})+1=0\). At \(s_w(m)\) the order \(e_w\) divides \(mM\) and not \(M\),
-- as shown above, so the exponent of \(\Phi_{e_w}\) in the factor of (a2) is \(1\).
proof cor:contactmonodromy
  (wedge) V[1 * h_3, (m / D) * h_1, (1 / D) * h_2]
  (zdeg) V[1 * h_3, 1 * h_4, (D * m) * h_1, m * h_1, D * h_1, 1 * h_1, (D * k1) * h_2, k1 * h_2]

-- For \(f=h^{k_0}+g\) with \(k_0\ge2\) and \(f'=0\), the singular points of \(\{f=0\}\) are the cone over
-- \(\Sigma\cap\mathrm{Sing}\{g=0\}\): at such a point \(H+g=0\), and Euler's relations give \(NH+Dg=x\cdot\nabla f=0\),
-- so \(H=g=0\), then \(\nabla H=k_0h^{k_0-1}\nabla h=0\) and \(\nabla g=0\); conversely, on that cone \(h=0\), so
-- \(\nabla H=0\), and \(g=0\) and \(\nabla g=0\). So, for \(f'=0\), the origin is an isolated singular point of
-- \(\{f=0\}\) exactly when \(g=0\) is smooth at the points of \(Z\). In \cref{thm:contact}(a), \(g\) is \(uA+y^mU\) at
-- \(t=0\) with \(m\ge2\), so \(g=0\) is smooth at a point of a \(\mathcal T_j\) exactly when \(A(0)\ne0\): this is the hypothesis
-- of (a) for \(k_0\ge3\), and for \(k_0=2\) with \(f'=0\), where \(C(0)=0\) as \(t^2C\) is then a multiple of \(u^{k_0}\).
-- For \(k_0=2\) and \(f'\ne0\) the hypothesis \(A(0)^2\ne4\varepsilon(0)C(0)\) allows \(A(0)=0\), and the criterion does
-- not extend: \(x^2+y^3+z^4\), of (a) with \(g=y^3\), \(f'=z^4\), \(A=0\) and \(U=C=1\), has an isolated singular point
-- at the origin while \(g=0\) is singular at the point of \(Z\). If moreover \(\Sigma\) is smooth and \(g=0\) is smooth at the points of \(Z\), then \(g=0\) is
-- tangent to \(\Sigma\) at finitely many points: on a complete curve \(C\) of such points \(\nabla g=A\nabla h\) with
-- \(\nabla h\ne0\), so \(A\) is a section of \(\mathcal O(D-\delta)\) on \(C\), of positive degree
-- \(D-\delta=(k_0-1)\delta+1\), and, as a section of a line bundle of positive degree on a complete curve has a zero
-- \cite[Section~II.6]{Hartshorne1977}, it vanishes at some point of \(C\), where \(\nabla g=0\), which is excluded. So at
-- every \(n\) a tangency of \(g=0\) with a smooth \(\Sigma\) along a set of positive dimension needs \(f'\ne0\).
remark rem:isolated [where the germ has an isolated singular point]
  origin: ?{\text{a reading of the hypotheses of \cref{thm:contact} by standard tools, for}\ f'=0\text{, with the case}\ k_0=2,\ f'\ne0\ \text{of (a) where it does not extend: Euler's relation for forms, and a section of a line bundle of positive degree on a complete curve has a zero \cite[Section~II.6]{Hartshorne1977}}}

subsection sec:qo Quasi-ordinary germs

-- A germ \(f\) in \(d+1\) variables is quasi-ordinary when its discriminant in \(z\) is a monomial times a unit; an
-- irreducible one has characteristic exponents \(\lambda_1<\dots<\lambda_g\) in \(\mathbb Q^d\), with the orders
-- \(n_j\ge2\). Its local topological zeta function is a sum over \(3g+1\) simplicial cones \(\theta\) of a fan in the
-- orders of \(x\) and of the semi-roots of \(f\) along an arc, the term \(J_\theta\) of a cone being its index times
-- the product of \(1/(\xi_j(v)+\eta(v)s)\) over its rays \(v\): \(Z_{top}(f,s)_0=J_{\sigma_{g+1}}+\sum_{j=1}^g
-- (J_{\sigma_j^+}-J_{\rho_j}+J_{\sigma_j^-})\) \cite[Theorem~3.7]{GonzalezPerezGonzalezVilla2014}. Every pole is
-- \(-1\) or \(-\xi_j(v)/\eta(v)\) for a ray \(v\) of \(\rho_j\) that is not special
-- \cite[Corollary~3.17]{GonzalezPerezGonzalezVilla2014}: at a special ray, of data \((n_j+1,n_jR)\) with
-- \(R=n_{j+1}\cdots n_g\), four terms of its level add up to \((\mathbb L-1)^{d+1}S_{\rho_j}\) times
-- \(1+\frac A{1-A}+\frac B{1-B}=\frac{1-AB}{(1-A)(1-B)}\), with \(A=\mathbb L^{-n_j}T^{(n_j-1)R}\) and
-- \(B=\mathbb L^{-1}T^R\), and the factor \(1-AB\) of the special ray in \(S_{\rho_j}\) cancels
-- \cite[Proposition~9.15]{GonzalezPerezGonzalezVilla2014}. On a line: the data of the special ray are the sums of
-- those of two marks, \((n_j+1,n_jR)=(n_j,(n_j-1)R)+(1,R)\); at \(e=\mathbb L-1\) and the letters \(1/A\) and
-- \(1/B\) the identity is the mediant of \cref{lem:qline}, in which the mass of the product letter \(1/(AB)\) is the
-- factor of the special ray; and on its candidate, where \(AB=1\) and the costs of the two marks are \(1/n_j\) and
-- \(-1/n_j\), it is the wall of \cref{lem:qline}.
cit fact:qozeta [the zeta functions of an irreducible quasi-ordinary germ]
  doi: 10.1515/crelle-2012-0049
  stmt: Theorem 3.7, Corollary 3.17 and Proposition 9.15, of arXiv:1105.2480v1
  origin: ?{\text{cited: \cite[Theorem~3.7, Corollary~3.17 and Proposition~9.15]{GonzalezPerezGonzalezVilla2014}; the identity of the proof of Proposition~9.15 is \cref{lem:qline}(mediant) at}\ e=\mathbb L-1\ \text{and the letters}\ 1/A\ \text{and}\ 1/B\text{, and on the candidate of the special ray it is \cref{lem:qline}(wall)}}

-- For an irreducible quasi-ordinary germ \(f\), \(h=x^Nf\) and the form \(\omega=x^{\nu-1}dx\wedge dz\), with
-- \(\nu_j\ge1\), and \(\nu_j=1\) wherever \(N_j=0\) (the support condition of
-- \cite[2.3]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2005}), every pole \(s_0\) of the motivic zeta function of Denef and Loeser
-- of \((h,\omega)\), and of its local topological zeta function, gives the eigenvalue \(e^{2\pi is_0}\) of the
-- monodromy at some point of \(h=0\) near the origin \cite[Theorem~7.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2005}. The
-- candidates of that proof are those of \cref{fact:qozeta}, so it does not rest on the formula for the value of
-- \cite[Theorem~6.3]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2005}, which false reducibility can make inaccurate when \(d\ge2\)
-- \cite[Remarks~3.10 and~9.16, Example~10.1]{GonzalezPerezGonzalezVilla2014}.
cit fact:monodromyqo [the monodromy conjecture for quasi-ordinary germs]
  doi: 10.1090/memo/0841
  stmt: Theorem 7.5, of arXiv:math/0306249v1
  origin: ?{\text{cited: \cite[Theorem~7.5]{ArtalCassouNoguesLuengoMelle2005}, for an irreducible germ with the candidates of \cite[Remark~9.16]{GonzalezPerezGonzalezVilla2014}}}

-- An embedded \(\mathbb Q\)-resolution is a proper birational map onto a neighbourhood of the origin whose source
-- has only abelian quotient singularities and on which \(f\) has \(\mathbb Q\)-normal crossings. On it the local
-- topological zeta function is \(\sum_k\chi(Y_k\cap\pi^{-1}(0))\,d_k\prod_i1/(N_{i,k}s+\nu_{i,k})\) over the strata
-- \(Y_k\), with \(d_k\) the order of the group of the stratum \cite[Theorem~2 and Corollaries~3.4
-- and~3.5]{LeonCardenalMartinMoralesVeysViuSos2020}: the arcs through a point of \(\mathbb A^n/G\) split over the
-- classes of \(G\), and those of one class are the arcs of \(\mathbb A^n\) modulo its centralizer
-- \cite[Section~2]{DenefLoeser2002}. The zeta function of the monodromy is
-- \(\prod_{i,j}(1-t^{m_{i,j}})^{\chi(\check E_{i,j})}\) over the parts \(\check E_{i,j}\) of the exceptional
-- divisor that lie on one component and in one stratum of quotient singularities, with \(m_{i,j}\) the multiplicity of
-- \(f\circ\pi\) there, read on the quotient \cite[Theorem~2.8]{MartinMorales2013}. At \(d_k=1\) this is
-- \cref{fact:zetatop}; \(d_k\) is the index of the cone of the stratum, the factor \(b\) of
-- \cref{lem:cone}(countsplit) for a cone of two rays.
cit fact:qresolution [the zeta functions on an embedded Q-resolution]
  doi: 10.1016/j.aim.2020.107192
  stmt: Theorem 2 and Corollaries 3.4 and 3.5, of arXiv:1911.03354v2
  origin: ?{\text{cited: \cite[Theorem~2 and Corollaries~3.4 and~3.5]{LeonCardenalMartinMoralesVeysViuSos2020}, \cite[Section~2]{DenefLoeser2002} and \cite[Theorem~2.8]{MartinMorales2013}; at}\ d_k=1\ \text{it is \cref{fact:zetatop}}}
